      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS, A DUCK of a man generally makes a goose of a husband.</p>
        <p>IT'S a cold day when a henpecked man is not in hot water.</p>
        <p>SOME men will take more time to name a clog than others would spend in building a house. A GROSS OFFENCE--selling 140 eggs for twelve dozen. A grocer offence—potting sand in sugar. Wny do men always speak of their wives as their "better halves '2" 6imply because they " half " to.</p>
        <p>A WRITER says "poetry is unvoiced music." That substantiates our belief that the tomcat sings in prase. THE surest of governing, both in priTate family and kingdom, is for a husband or prince sometimes to drop their prerogatives. THEv are making the new styles of collars so high that before long young men will ha ,e to stand on tiptoe to see anything at all. I DOAN like de man what passes me in de road widout speakin'. De dog dat won't neither growl nur wag his tail ain't no a'rthly er count. QUEER, isn't it? A man who will swallow any kind of a dish with an imposing French name will be scared to death if lie catches a cold with a.Greek or Latin title.</p>
        <p>FRANK showed the picture on his slate. "It's awful bad," said teasing Kate; "just like the small-pox." "Why ?" asked he. "Because it's sketching, don't you see ?" CUSTOMER—" Have you some good imported cigars ?" New shopman—" Not just now; but we will have in about an hour. The printer around the corner is at work on the labels now." "I FEAR.," said Dr. Jalap, "that you have got some foreign substance in this prescription." "Possibly," replied the apothecary; "bat look at your prescription. It is foreign all through." IF rich, it is easy enough to conceal our wealth ; but if poor, it is not quite so easy to conceal poverty. We shall find that it is less difficult to hide a thousand guineas than one hole in our coat. POLICE SERGEANT (surprising his subordinate, who should be on duty)—" Here, here ; Oi'll have none o' that." Subordinate (devouring the last morsel)—" Be Jabers, Oi don't think yez will !" A LADY of about thirt7 years of age married a wealthy old bachelor of Dallas, Texas. A female friend asked her :—"But tell me. Why didn't you 'marry your husband ten years ago ?" " Well, you see, ten years ago he was too old for me." WHY should people never ba frightened at the appearance of a ghost ? Because, if it is a ghost, its appesgance must, in the nature of things, be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>ART DEALER—" Yes, that was painted by one of the masters. But, I beg your pardon, sir, you must not touch it with your umbrella." Flake Whyte—" What's the matter? Ain't it dry yet ?" P To tell what a man says, pay attention to the tongue. To ascertain what he means, pay attention to the eye. To talk in opposition to the heart is one of the easiest thin.gio LI the worlcl ; to look this opposition is more difficult than algebra'. I DON'T wonder that people talk of the good old times, said the president of the gas company. At • one time it was dark for three days and three nights in the land of Egypt. What a big thing it would be for the gas companies if we could have something like that in these days. " HERE, waiter, bring me a plate of corned beef _ _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>hash."" No co'n'd-beefhash to-night, sail." "But it's on the bill of fare." " Yaas, sail, we mos' allus has co'n'd-beef hasb, but, ye see, sah, all our dinuah customahs was specially hongry to-day,• sah." "What's that got to do with it ?" "Waal, ye see, sat', dey done ate up all de co'n'd-beef on their plates.—Cleicago News.</p>
        <p>A GENTLEMAN who has recently returned from a tour in the United States says :—" I was amused by an idea given me by an American in office. I asked how much his situation was worth; he answered six hundred dollars besides stf,alings. This was frank and honest. In England the word would have softened down to perquisites. I after- wards found that it was a common expression in the States to say a place was worth so much besides cheatage.' A Al ARSEILLES MERCTIANT, lately deceased, who started in business with a thousand pounds, be- came a millionaire. He left his property to a friend, with the condition that he should be buried with the sum of one thousand pounds placed in his coffin. The executor • bewailed this reckless waste of mopey, and was at his wit's end how to defeat the whimstcul clause in the will. At length a happy thought come. "I will put a cheque," he ! said, "for a thousand into the coffin." A MINISTER of the name of Sparks, whose pas- torate lay in the North, was the lather o: 13 chil- dren. At the baptism of the thirteenth, an aged brother divine, desirous to choose what seemed to him an appropriate description of the life of man, called on the congregation to join in singing the fifth paraphrase, beginning with the line, "As sparks in close succession rise." So unconscious was he that he could not understand the people's titter until, when he descended from the pulpit, his pun was explained to him. " REBECCA, you shall nod spheak mit dot Moses Levi vonee more." "Oh, ladder, you preak mine heardt. Ve vos almost engaged. Vy shall I not spheak of him ?" "He hal sheated me. He haf sheated me. He haf sold me a paste diamond for a shenuine shtone." "Oh, fadder, dot shouldt recommendt him to you as a son-in-law. If he can • fool a wise man like you, see vat a fortune he half in der chewelry piziness." "Veil, Rebecca, you vas schmarder as I thought. Get married yen you like. lam ankschious to go into bartnership with mine son-in-law 1."</p>
        <p>AN Austin lady, who had been to Galveston for several weeks, returned one day last week. She heard from the neighbours that her coloured cook had been entertaining the Reverend Whangdoodle Baxter during the lAdy's absence. "Matilda," said the lady very sternly, "I have told you repeatedly that you must not have any man visiting the house during my absence. That must not occur again; do you understand ? " "Ob course, hit shan't occur no moah. I didn't know dat you wanted de gemmans ter visit me while you was at home. You never told me da,t befoah."</p>
        <p>A RICH priest had hoarded a fine collection of jewels, to which he was constantly adding, and of which he was inordinately proud. Upon showing them to a friend, the latter feasted his eyes for some time, and on taking his leave thanked his host for the jewels. "How !" cried the priest ; "I have not given them to you ! Why do you thank me ?" " Well," rejoined his friend, "I have at least had as much pleasure from seeing them as you can have, and the only difference between us that I can discover is that you have the trouble of watching them."—Translated from the Chinese.</p>
        <p>AN old lady read a paragraph in one of the papers the other day, describing how a grindstone burst.in a sawmill and killed four men. She hap- pened to remember that there was a small grind- stone down in her cellar, leaning against the wall; so she went out and got an accident insurance policy, and then, sumn.onin,g her servant, and holding a pie-board in front of her face, so that if the thing exploded her face would not be injured, had the stone taken out into the road, where 24 pails of water was thrown over it, and a stick was stuck in a hole bearing a placard marked " Dan- gerous." She 55.-ys it is a mercy the whole house was not blown to pieces by the thing before this.— Wood and Iron.</p>
        <p>LAWYER: "You say you make an examination of the premises. %% hat did you find ?" Witness : "Oh, nothing of consequence—a 'beggarly account of empty boxes,' as Shakespeare says !" Lawyer: " Never mind what Sbake.speare said. He will be summoned and testify for himself, if he knows any- thing about the ease."</p>
        <p>T TIE bear and the wolf had been warm friends for,many years when the wolf one da7 asked for -a loan to help him out of a tight squeeze. To his utter astouishinent the favour was promptly refused. " Haven't we been iriends" asked the wolf. "Truly we have." " And don't you wish to be in the future ?" "It is for that reason," answered the bear, " that I refuse the loan. If you have no trouble in repaying me you will depend upon me in all future emergencies. If you fail to ropily,- you will become my enemy for Moral---to to your friends for sympathy— to your pawnbroker for loans. exoFF,son.---" I\li.E;s Reform, you are quite right ! Bat the truth is, I am sorry to say, that you are 20 years ahead of your age." 'Miss Reform hastily—" Dear me, professor, do you mean to say that I look as if I were 60 1"</p>
