        <p>,iTTLE .‘3. 14ax's.4 I a ..,, ,-,:,,,-, Ica, ...e.er:;,.- o: . I cirri,: in a Vial Coat, - which wli . ara-st the 1- cle,ty.l,lrt Ir</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>I hear Mr. Herbert Gladstone, the Under Secre- tary to the Home Department, proposes inspecting some day this week the conditions of labour in one of the large Northern collieries. Doubtless this has some bearing on the new Mines Bill now being drafted at the Home Office.</p>
        <p>The Wembley Tower is to have a competitor in a gigantic wheel and towers after the Chicago model. Messrs. Maudsley, Son and Field, of the Westmin- ster Bridge-road, are the engineers. The wheel will be .?,00 feet high, and have attached to its circum- ference forty passenger earriages,each to hold forty persons. The four steel column supports will have eight platforms, fitted with restaurants,dining halls and ballrooms. The Chicago wheel, made in twenty weeks, cost over £140,000.</p>
        <p>"The most careful and re:!ly,ctful consideration" will be given to the views of the Radical financial reformers by Sir William Harcourt. He reminds the signatories to the memorial that no revelations as to his intentions can be made before the produc- tion of the Budget. The Tory papers indignantly denounce the ptopositions made by Mr. Philip Stanhope and his friends. But Sir William Har- court knows that the country expects a bold finan- cial scheme from him.</p>
        <p>Every now and then, when nothing more attrac- tive in the political world presents itself for dis- cu,sion, the Tory papers take to prospecting the future of ths Liberal party when Mr. Gladstone leads it no longer. Judging from the reports that reach us from Biarritz, the Grand Old Man will con- tinue to vex the souls of the Tories for many days to come. Consequently, the ingenious specu!stions which I read in a provincial Unionist journal yes- terday represented so much wasted energy.</p>
        <p>Biarritz, where Mr. Gladstone is spending the Parliamentary recess, is best known now as the place where the Premier prefers to pass his brief holidays. Few people were aware of its excellence as a health resort until the third Napoleon made it fashionable. That not very lovable personage built pPlace there—and French "society" immechately thronged to the little town where the Empres!, Eugenie's splendid residence was the centre of at- traction.</p>
        <p>I am told by a person well acquainted with the London tailoring trade that the clothing of the poor is now on the whole made under healthier conditiohs than that of the rich. The tendency of West End employers is more and more, he says, not to provide a workroom but to put their work out ; and in con- sequence of their demand for rooms " sittings" rents are going up rapidly in the crowded districts lying behind Regent-st., Oxford-st., and Piccadilly. On the other hand, owing to subdivision of labour in the cheap clothing trade, work is more and more concentrated in factories, which are perforce kept in a tolerably sanitary condition,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>The story told as to how Lord Rosabery suc- ceeded in quieting the opposing parties during coal conference at the Foreign Office by ordering lunch at a critical moment is repeated isy the Lon- den correspondent of the Liverpool" Daily Pos." Mr. Whitehead.of Staffordsnire, who was present at the great conference, thus told what happened : " We were getting very warm that time—very warm indeed. In the middle of it Lord Rosehery said : There is some cold meat in the next room, I,entlemen ; I think we had better adjourn for lunch.' And then everybody burst out laughing, and good humour was at once restored.</p>
        <p>Lord Salisbury was a party to the "compromise arrived at between Mr. Balfour and the Government, with regard to the Parish Councils Bill, Since that time 31r. Balfour, in order to appease the more viol- (nt spirits among his own party, has been at some pains to claim perfect liberty of action for the Peers. Some people now believe that the ex-Premier will not depart from the position which he took up when the compromise was arrived at. and as he has those " highly competent. critics," the gentlemen of the Douse of Lords, well in hand, he can act with a straightforwardness which the forces behind Mr. Balfour would not tolerate. The Tory chief, at all events, means to make up his mind in quite a leisurely fashion.</p>
        <p>In his sketch of Professor Tyndall in the " Review of *Reviews," Mr. Grant Allen gives a charitable explanation of the violence of tie political passions which induced the great scientist to pen some very extraordinary manifestoes during his later years. "Tyndall came to England," he writes," essentially an Orangeman. Had lie mixed with Liberal Eng- li,hmen only, it is probable lie would have got rid in time of his Irish prejudices, like so many more of us whose Irish Protestant descent has not inter- fered with the development of our political princi- ples. But the adverse influence of Carlyle confirm- ed him in all his original preconceptions. When the great split came, Tyndall tdok the wrong side, and fought for it Noith all the obstinacy and vigour of his Protestant Irish nature." Mr. Grant Allen is. I chink, rather too hard on Irish Protestants as a -class. Very many of them are men of avowed Liberal and Radical opinions.</p>
        <p>1 hear that Airs. Gladstone stands in greater need of recuperation from the mild air and Atlantie breezes of Biarritz than her distinguisl-ed folsband. She is still very weak and ailing, for although not mech was said about it, it is the fact that not only wa, her attack of influenza rather severe and debili- tating, but she is still suffering from the effects of erysipelas which followed on the influenza. One of the last acts of Mr. Gladstone before leaving Eng- land was to forward a cheque for £ioo to head the subscription about to be raised for a memorial fund to the late Sir Andrew Clatk. Mr. Gladstone ex- pressed not only his warm approval of the memo- rial, but also his intention to be present at the public meeting which will be held in due course to initiate the memorial, which will probably take the form of certain additional buildings for the the se- gregation of infectious patients and other purposes necessary to complete the efficiency of the London Hospital, to which Sir Andrew Clark was so much ,itt ached and where he did such good work for the poor. It is, I hear, not proposed to increase the number of beds, which already exceeds that of any other hospital in London.</p>
        <p>At a meeting just held under the preskency of the Duke of Cambridge. Canon Wilberforce told a very remarkable story from his own knowledge. Ile was standing at the bedside of a patient. in a very critical state along with Sir Andrew Clark, when a telegram from the South of France was handed to Sir Andrew. The latter having read it passed it to the Canon. It urgently summoned tlie physician to a patient who desired his services and proffered a fee of .£5,000. "What will you do ?" asked the Canon. "Nothing will induce me to leave this bedside," was tl.e answer, " until the battle with death is decided and so long as my ser- vices are needed." Ile accordingly telegraphed a refusal. Some time afterwards Canon Wilberforce happened again to be with Sir Andrew Clark. when the latter said to him. "The cheque came after all, and the fee was not lost. I was summoned again, and was able to go." " What will you do with the cheque?" said the Canon. The answer was," I ,hell give it to the London Hospital." "And you." said Canon Wilberforce, turning to the chairman of the London Hospital Board who was present. "know well how it was applied." This version of a story which, one way and another, has been a g nod deal in recent gossip is entitled to considera- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>"TEE STRAND MAGAZINE."</head>
        <p>"TEE STRAND MAGAZINE."</p>
        <p>A new volume of "The Strand Magazine," as web as " The Picture Magazine" commences with the January number. In lieu of "The adventures of Sherlock Ilcimes," (by whose death we feel as if we had lost •a wrsopal friend), a series of " Stories from the Diary of a Doctor "take a prominent position in the magazine,and perpetuate the interest so keenly taken in it from the bei-:inning of its career. A descriutivo article on the Royal Palaces of Amsterdam and The Hague, with their royal occusants, the Queen of Holland and her mother the QueerT-Regent, is contributed by Mary Spencer. Warren, and well illustratf d from photographs, a privi- le4e accorded for the first time, the palaces never before lotving been photographed. " Personal Ileminicences of Sir Andrew Clark," by E. H. Pitcairn, with a couple of portraits and numerous illustrai ions, is an article every Englishman and woman too, should read. Jr will inspire them with new hopes, new courage, and new</p>
        <p>" UADIII7III,B UOCOA ha?, in a remarkable degree'. those e 4 it! ural elements of sustenance, v..hich give the system eridur- tmc,. nnd hardihood, with a steaely aq.iort tar renders it a in st acceptable and reliable beverage."—thaith.</p>
        <p>• ENLlGHATiolg.—lntending einivanti to :New Yerl. Bovril Philadelphia, San Franc;sco, CArta,,,, China, 'Japan, Ails tralia, or New Zealand, should tae t&amp; kets (lowest. rates') at Or, " Examiner " Office, by Onion, American. AuEtralizn Oriental. ?ind (A.lft r Was ter, ageat."Exam!our. 021ce, Bewsey-st., Warringu</p>
        <p>A blouse I particularly admire is of tie useful and comfortable shape, admits of being copied in almost any material, and is fashioned, with the new basque, which I can recommend for all figures, both the stout and the thin! The model is of crepon, apsicot-coloured, and banded with butter-coloured lace insertion. Do not tire of this trimming yet awhile, for it is likely to remain in fashion for some thus to come.</p>
        <p>Capes continue popular, and are to be purchased most reasonably, a guinea or 30s, procuring a charming mantle of sufficient thickness and distin- guished appearance. Of course, for this price, these capes are unlined, but I would recommend you to invest in a few yards of cheap shot silk, and lining your cape therewith. This will cost much less than buying a silk-lined mantle, and the trouble is infinitesimal</p>
        <p>A novel coat Of loose and becoming style hat the sleeves, deep-pointed yclze, and side panels of black moire antique, outlia,3d with fur, white the back and front of this elegant mantle are formed of plain cloth or velvet. The sleeves are enor- mous, and allow ample room for themost elaborate dress trimmings. Such a mantle is remarkably well suited for matrons' wear; it would also form a stylish evening cloak if smartly lined. The Marie Stuart bonnet, of open-work jet, and mounted on a colour, with strings to match, should be adopted with a coat, of this kind. A fan bag is fashioned from wide yellow ribbon, the long edges being sewn together with over and over stitches to within some distance of top and bottom. The top of each ribbon is cut in a deep point; one end is allowed to stand erect, the other is turned down over the bag. A ribbon as a draw- string is in a pretty bow at each end, and fastened to the top of the bag. The loose ends at the bottom are fringed. A design is embroidered on the upper side of the bag and the word "Fans" Is worked diagonally across the bottom. Yours lovingly. JENNIE.</p>
        <p>\ ladame Pate will, in the course of this month, bein her farewEll tour of the provincrs, and M May will definitely bid adieu to the public. An American writer observes that Mrs. James Brown Thoer's beautiful hair is now thickly streaked with white.</p>
        <p>The urcat YeAuty of hext seasnn Will be, accordinf.: to gossip, the daughter of Lord aii.d Lady Archibald Campbell.</p>
        <p>A much-reputed palmist ptudied Mrs. Besant's hand when she went to Kumhakonam, and next day she heard what he had to say as the result. The palm- ist's " shlokas," Mrs. Besant declared, " gave a very accurate sketch of my ;ife, with cue or two details never printed."</p>
        <p>It is generally supposed that fruits, although plea- santly refreshing. to the palate, are devoid of nutritive value ; bat this is not so, for many of them are of very decided service as food, and those that are not, on ac- count of the quantity of water they contain, are of use in another way, inasmuch as they supply salts that are necessary to keep the blood in a healthy condition. Rice is deficient in natural fat : therefore to make it a useful article of diet it should be cooked with articles of food that will supply the deficiency, such as butter, milk, meat, &amp;c. It is a mistake to boil rice in water, as it loses valuable compounds during the process of cooking. Unless boiled in milk or soup it, stiould be steamed. Cheese contains a very fair amount of nutritive and bone forming material, but as it is not very rich in heat giving matter it should be used with other foods that are such as a macaroni, rice, or bread, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Female Sanitary Invectors. The National Health Society, taking note of the fact that two women from among is former students have been appointed sanitary inspectors to the Kensington Vestry,has threw n its training course open to all women, whether intending to work for the Society or no. Stu- dents must be 25 years of age, and must either be qualified nurses or go through a hospital course, the Society adding a further training in sanitation, hygiene, physiology, and nursing. Hitherto employmont of aui certificated students has been found in connection with county councils, but the Society considers that were more qualified women available they would probably obtain appointment as sanitary inspectors. It is to be hoped that in this event they will receive better salaries han the paltry 1360 a year which is all that the Kensing- ton Vestry has so far seen lit to pay.</p>
        <p>Labour is Life. _</p>
        <p>"Labour is life," said-Sir Andrew Clark, in a lecture, " but," he continued, " worry is killing. It is bad management that kills peoole. Nature will let no man overwork himself unless he plays her false—takes stimu- lants at irregular times, smokes too much, or takes opium. If he is regular and obeys the laws of. health, and walks in the way ot physiological righteousness, nature will never allow him or any other person to work too much. I have never yet. seen a case of breaking down from mere over work alone ; but I admit that it is necessary above all things to cultivate tranvility of mind. Try to help your patients to exercise their wills in regard to this—for will counts for something in secur- ing tranquility—to accept things as they are, and not ts bother about yesterday, which is gone for ever ; not to bother about to-morrow.which is not theirs : but to take the present day and make the best of it Those affection- ate women who will continually peer into what lies be- yono never have any present life at all—they are always grizzling over the past or prying into the future, and this blessed to-day, which is all that we are sure of, they never have."</p>
