      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>Patti's music is all written one note lower than it it usk-d to be, but her box-office scale remains at the same old notch.</p>
        <p>ME DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>With the advent of the New Year the annual sales are put in full swing, and there are rare bar- gains to be had, such as blouses of silk, satin, or chiffon, plain and shot velvet, tulle spangled net, and sequined gauze. The fashionable serpentine crepe in every possible shade is inexpensive, and so pretty. It should commend itself to you, for although of ample width and silken appearance, this delightful material costs but is. 6d. the yard, and may be manufactured into day or evening blouses, with or without additions of coarse lace of the kind known as Venetian; while, again this crepe may be fashioned into the now popular again, which readily transform an old gown into one of more than ordinary attraotivenesap</p>
        <p>Out in the north-west of Canada women are very scarce indeed, and as a consequence more highly prized than in the old country. At Edmonton, servant girls were obtaining last autumn sixty pounds a year, with board and lodging ; and even then, as the proprietor of one hotel pathetically remarked," they will get married, and we have to go to the bAher and expense of getting others." Mrs. Showalter, wife of Jackson Showalter, champion chess player of America, is not only a handsome woman but is such a remarkably clever player than her ad- mirers have denominated her the "chess queen." Several years ago Mrs. Showalter defeated Lipschutz in a game in which she had the odds of a roAk. She is a Kentucky girl, and is as proficient on horseback and with the gun as she is in playing chess. One of her most recent victories was over C. 0. Jackson, champion of Indiana, whom she defeated in three games out of four contesting the fourth game to a draw.</p>
        <p>The large picture hats are very much worn, and with the hair prettily arranged at the back of the head are generally becoming. They are in coloured felts, in velvet, or in moir6, but the last-named, though it has a newer effect, is not so pretty. For a windy day, or for skating, there is nothing so suitable as a neat little Tarn o' Shanter. A Tam o' Shanter of ruby velvet, trimmed with a band of sable, a sable head and tail on the left side, and white quills jetted in black would look very pretty. Violet in every possible tone is becoming wonder- fully popular, and my experience is that in one shade or other this colour will be found universally becoming. The deep shades, for instance, improve a brunette and set off a blonde complex on. A pretty model is crowned with a tiny bonnet made of this colour. Bands of violet velvet in roll form cross the crown, which is striped alternately with tan-coloured lace insertion. Rosettes of violet blossoms tinish the sides and centre, and a couple of jet ospreys are adcif)d as a finish.</p>
        <p>THE FX AMINER. SATURDAY, J % NUARY 20, 1894. LADIES' LETTER.</p>
