      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>Alcohol.</head>
        <p>Alcohol.</p>
        <p>Alcohol quickens the heart, makes it do unnecessary work, and therefore makes it weary. If any healthy man present has a very good circulation,his heart will be doing the work of lifting 122 foot-tons every twenty-four hours ; i.e. it will naturally be lilting a weight of 122 tons one foot from the earth. If, however, he by alcohol interfere with the work which is according to nature, he may run up the work,by the quantity of spirit he may imbibe, until it shall amount to twenty-four tons more, not to the strengthening of his heart, but to the reduc- tion of its power. He may gain a short spurt, but no more; the remainder is lost labour, labour in vain.—Dr. Richardson.</p>
        <p>Simple Dishes. A simple dish which is equally suitable for light sup- per or luncheon is made by carefully breaking an egg into a small china scallop shell which has been buttered and sprinkled with grated cheese ; more cheese, mixed with chopped parsley and seasoned with pepper and salt, is placed over the egg, also a tiny piece of butter ; the shells Mould then be put into a quick oven for 10 or 12 minutes. Sardines served on slices, cut lengthways, of hard-boiled egg, garnished with watercress, and ac- companied by brown bread and butter, help to vary the monotony ot light luncheon dishes. Baked pears are particularly nice when sent to table cold with a little sifted sugar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Hints.</head>
        <p>Hints.</p>
        <p>To renovate velvet, free it from dust by laying face down and whipping smartly ! then brush with a soft hairbrush ; damp on the wrong side with borax wat,r and hang pile inward in the sunshine to dry, taking care that there is no fold or wrinkle on the line.</p>
        <p>Ilt-gular hours for meals are all important to health ; anyone who has to do active work of any kind, either mental or physical, must, know how essential it is that the heaviest meal of the day should come after the day's work is (:.one.</p>
        <p>It is stateo that chemically the apple contains a larger amount of phosphorus, or brain food, than any other fruit, besides which, whilst the phosphorus in the apple will, if partaken of freely, stirAulate the brain, its juice and malic acid act upon the liver and tend to eliminate effete matters from the system in the simplest and moat natural manner.</p>
        <p>To be Remembered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>OUTWITTING AN EXCISEMAN.</head>
        <p>OUTWITTING AN EXCISEMAN.</p>
        <p>In a little village in the Orkney Islands lived .Maggie , who kept a sort of half-way house for the con- venience of travellers ; but, unfortunately or otherwise, she could not obtain the licence necessary for the sale of alcoholic liquor,. A stage-coach passed daily through the village was noticed, at various times, to put off a jar at Maggie's premises ; and the neighbours were cruel enough to insinuate that it contained a beverage stronger than milk. Men were n6ticed to,enter her heuse fre- quently, and leave in an advanced stage of ietoxica- tion ; so clearly the village gossipers were correct in their surmise that a shebeen business was carr4.ci on. Time confirmed. the theAr:v,and eventually the old lady's reputation spread over a wide territory, ins much that tae excise officers from an adjoining town devised a , scneme to trap her. Accordingly an excise man was ' deputed for the job, who acidentally (on purpose, as it were) found himsol under Ma.zgie's hospitable roof. Ringing a table bell, a characteristic figure made her , appearance, who inquired her visitor's wants. An order was given for some bread and cheese, which were soon placed on the table before him. Apparently his wants were not fully satisfied ; so, after a little delay, Maggie was again suminened, and confidently asked if she could supply a little whisky. To the exciseman's surprise,she replied in the affirmative, and a bottle was placed be- fore him, with a polite request to help himself. He poured out a glass of the liquor, at the same time laying soveleign on the table, which she picked up, and re- tired for what he thought was the change. He fla,tered himself that his little game was to be successful ; hot after waiting a considerable time,she did not return with the change; so the bell was again rung for her re-appear- ance." Were you not aware that I gave you a sovereign?' interrogated the offieer. " Yes, indeed, sir, I ken it is a sovereign ; And you art a real kind gentleman." "nut, mada m."he protested," surely the refreshments you have supplied me with do not amount to that sum ? " " Wee), wed," returned MaTie, not a bit disturbed, " ye must ken that I dinna sell whisky in this !loose. The refresh- ments I dinna charge ve for ; but what I gi'e, I gee, an' what I..get I keep. Good day, sir." The exciseman was quite dumbfounded at the retort, as the landlady bowed him out ; and he had to stand the derisive taunts of his brother officers for many. .a day afterwards, in addition to being deprived of his " change " with no alternative but to grin and bear it.—" o;assell's Saturday Journal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>"JUSTICES' JUSTICE" RECTIFIED.</head>
        <p>"JUSTICES' JUSTICE" RECTIFIED.</p>
        <p>On the 2nd inst. the Towcester bench of magistrates sentenced a tramp to a month's hard labour for having stolen a cake of the value of 3-1-d. from a baker's shop. The tramp was arrested soon atter the theft was com- mitted ; but the poor fellow—who subsequently told the magistrates that he had not had any food for three days and that the temptation was too much for him—had by that time eaten the eak-i. The severity of the sentence was strongly condemned by the Press, aryl on the peti- tion of the Northampton Trades Council the Home Secre- tary on Tuesday ordered the man's immediate release.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>G g F. t C 0,, • ez".</head>
        <p>G g F. t C 0,, • ez".</p>
        <p>GIVE PA`IE &amp; COMFORT DURING TEETHING. Pleas..nt, Coollna Aperient for Children of all A. 4.35. Try them end prove their value for yourselves. Sold everywhere in packets, J. lt,d. and 2s. 9d., or pit ree 14 or 34 stamps. T. PRITCHARD. CHEMIST. CHEADLE, MANCHESTER ~lateCheril on Road).</p>
        <p>"You have been kind to me, a stranger, yet I cannot confide to you the pitiful story that makes my life a blank," said Uldene, her large, dark, pathetic eyes filling with tears. "I will carry it down to the grave with me unrevealed." "I an sorry," said Emily, pityingly. "I thought perhaps I could help you.'' "How could you help me when Heaven itself is against me?' sobbed Uldene, bitterly. "My life has been a mistake and has gone all wrong from beginning to end. There is no help for it, no way out of it. lam entangled in a web woven by fate." "Do you intend remaining in the city 7" asked Emily, attempting to change the subje ;t, which she saw was so bitterly painful to the lovely stranger. It I cou7d find anything to do," faltered Uldene. She had realized it would be the worst thing sbe could undertake to attempt to find pupils for music here; her face was so well known her identity would be sure to be discovered.</p>
        <p>"I am sure that I can help you in that way," responded Emily. "Madame was saying only 3edterday we needed at least two more young ladies in the establishment. I will use my influence with her to try to get you in." "H,ow shall I ever thank you 1" murmured Uldene. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"By not attempting," responded Emily, promptly. "I am always glad to extend a helping hand to young girls who are inclined to help thenu.elves ; it's every one's duty. It is late now," said Emily. "We won't talk about it any more to-DOI% You shall go with me in the morning to see madame." Although Uldeue lay down upon the snow-white, inviting couch no sleep came to her that night. All through couch, dreary, moonlit hours her dark, curly head tossed restlessly to and fro, and the dark, sombre eyes never closed. She could not still the voice in her heart that kept repeating : "He eid not mourn for your loss. He was thank- ful to be set free that he might wed her whom he had never ceased to love. How bitterly he would hate you if you came back from the grave to part them."</p>
        <p>"Oh., Rutledge! my lost love!" moaned poor Uldene, under her breath, "would that I had died rather than live to witness your love for another?"</p>
        <p>Early the next morning Miss Lennox and Uldene presented themselves at Madame Dubois' establish- men;.</p>
        <p>The madame looked in wonder at the won- drously beautiful face before her. "I should like to take her in," she said, aside, to Emily. "but, I dare not on account of my son. No. no, Emily ; I tkre not. Something warns me I must not. I teel it in my heart." "She is so young and friendless," said Miss Len- nox; "and with a face iike that, it would be most dangerous to cast her adrift on the world." "That is true," responded madame, nervously. "But what greater danger could she encounter, Emily, than meeting my son? There is a skeleton in every houmehoid. You know what ours is. Therefore, I say, it would be best not to take her in."</p>
        <p>A happy thought occurred to Emily. " You send out a great deal of work, madame. Why not give her work to do outside ?" "She is a straager to me, Emily. I had. rather not, unless yo,t choose to be responsible for it ; " and madame ,iniled faintly. "I am perlebtly willing to agree to that arrange- ment," reSpooded Emily, bravely. "I cannot tell why, but 1 take a great interest in this young girl, and I cannot help ire conviction which steals over me, that if I should not, watch over her she would destroy herself." "You are an angel to the wret:hed and needy," replied 111a.datile Dubois, warmly. " You shall have your way. I suppose your prot6gee will share your apartments, in that case, for the present." " We have Ade that arrangement, madame," said Emily. . .</p>
        <p>A few moments later she crossed the room te where Uldere was sitting. "Miss Dan," she said—that was the namt Ultiene chose to call herself—" fate or Providence. whichever you will, has been kind. Madame Dubois will furnish you employment. It will be sent yon at the cottage." "I am very thankful to madame, and to you," responded Uldene, gratefully. The next two weeks dragged slowly by. It was only by hard work Uldene forgot for a moment the bitter pain at her heart. She had promised herself, after gazing upon the face that was dearer than life itself to her, she would go quietly away; but she found hertielf too weak to carry out her resolve. She must be near him though separated from him as far all the earth from the sun.</p>
        <p>At the end of the second week a pitiful event nappened, that changed the current of three lives. One rainy morning, one of the shop boys of Madame Dubois brought Emily a large bundle of work from the establisltment, with the message it might be completed at lice home, if she prefecred, as the young lady whom the work was for would call there, in the course of an hour, to give instruc- ticms concerning it. " Oh, yes, it is the bridesmaid's dress that came in to madame yesterday,' said Miss Lennox, un- wrapring the bundle, and disciosiog to view soft, billowy folds of wilite surah silk and old point lace.</p>
        <p>Uldene bent over it with a sigh. It brought with it SUC't pain u 1 recol!ections of the life, so short and bweet, which she had pat behind her for ever.</p>
        <p>" Beautiful ! s it not ?" said Emily. "And it is a he,tutiful young girl, indced, wh is to wear it. Yes, Miss Ncdiy T.,lepits will look a.itnui Ui3 bwcet as the inide, I think."</p>
        <p>"Noddy Temple!" How the hmir,e startled Uhler°. "Miss Nedily vvW he very poxlieular over it, I'm hum,' pursued Emily, " for it, is to be \v,&gt;rn at one of the graie,t, wet-lin:m.3 we have had hs season. • Toe hri:j(:::::rootii is 111.odseme, and very wc!althy. flu is the son of • the . late Semlt,r Chester. VVoy, Alias wh,,t are you doing? You're It :1),,04 the ku waiQir ail over the i,eauti, fol. white !" peci Emily, in dismay, as she, sprang to 1-(seue tt. itomm. Uldcnt's lap. " Are you goinf. to femn t., 2d i lean ?" she asked, g.zitig uuximubly niic 1.4,11 LeutiiuL wuiLe t&amp;CO.' Yila look as though you had ecen ghokt."</p>
        <p>ON. Verlie Sefton's visit to the Renwicks was fast drawing to a close. On the following week she was to return to Richmond. Nalla and Mark could spare their darling no longer. Verlie had insisted upon a year's betrothal before she would name the wedding-day; but her impatient, handsome lover would brook no delay. You must marry me at once, Verlie, my darl- ing," he declared; "then this perpetual, haunting fear that I may lose you will be tor ever laid at rest."</p>
        <p>He pleaded so eloquently, and she loved him co well, bow could she refuse? And, at length, much to Rutledge's delight, the order for the wedding trousseau was given, and Webs consented to become his bride as soon as all arrangements could be coNpleted. When Vellio wrote to Neddy Temple asking her to be one of the bridesmaids, Neddy had bent her dark, curly hair over the letter with a startled cry of dismay. " Verlic:—going to be married—to—Rutledge Chester I" she gasped. "I can hardly believe it. I should almost iaucy poor Uldene's ghont would rise between them. Still, if he is bent upon marry- ing again, I would rather it would be Verlie who is to lake Uldeee's place in his heart than any one else."</p>
        <p>, Preparations for the marriage went steadily on. Rutledge Chester had purchased one of the most magnificent villas on the avenue, and it .was being rapidly put in readiness f.y the decora- tore and upholsrerers for the occupancy of the bridal pair when they should return from their tour abroad. There was one event which happened that annoycd RutieOge mere than he cared to o A n. Ile had crossed the lawn, and was entering throng') one of the long French windows of the drawin-rown, when he was suddenly brought te a statokiili by the conversation of the decorators in an adjoining room. lie kue..v he should have made his presence known to them, for he was too honourable to piay the part of an eavesdropper ; but the sound Of 1.03 own name, and the words which followed after, held him spdiboond, Mr. Chester's bride will tied this rather an unweky house for her," said one of the worii.men t hid companion. " You may 1,:t11.11 and sneer all you like but I tell you otriens are onions, and si..ns are signs. When a hird flies in thrrutin lie op,u window of a house they are preparing lo,ide, or a black cat stra.:,e in, look OUL for a t r tvedy, I say." " Paha.w " laughed his companion. . "I don't believe in such nonsense. It a absurd." " It is ??,,,t nonsense," returned the first speaker: "it is wavning of impending evil, as I have actually experienced. A few years ago, I was decorating the walla of a beautiful house, which was being made ready to receive a lovely young bride, when, all of a sudden, a ra‘en, idaek as rdeht, fingered in through the open window, quite in Lhe snipe manner as the bird hew into the house to-day, and, ,Am in this case, it made the tour of the whole house before it flew out again." Here the man came to a full stop, breathing hard. _ - -</p>
        <p>........ "Well, did anything come of that?' laughed his companion, good- intmouredly. ‘• This nitwit came of it," returned the other, slowly; "as the bridal couple weft crossing the Chro,.hold, surrounded by hundreds of gay, laugh- ing friends following in the rear, a dark form sprang out from the shadow of the vestibule. Those nearest the groom and bride caught a fleet- ing glimpse of a woman's face, burning with the raging tires of jealousy and. hate. There was a startled scream—a pitiful groan. The bride fell back in the arms of her frantic bridegroom—dead I —stricken to death by the small, white hand of a beautiful rival that had plunged, into her white breast, a long, thin, jewelled silver pin, which she had drawn from the meshes of her raven-dark hair. Yes, the bride fell dead on the threshold of the house she was never destined to occupy." "So you think the presence of the bird foretold that tragedy ?" asked the other, thoughtfully. "I am sure of it," was the reply ; " and that is not the first instance of the kind I have heard oi, either. I could tell you a score or more—" Rutledge Chester waited to hear no more. With a white, i-et face, he turned on his heel and walked rapidly away. He tried to laugh the matter off, hut soinehow it troubled him, even though he told himself it was ridiculous folly for a person of sense to give one moment's thought to superstitious, omens or signs. " Notting; could happen to my beautiful Verlie." he muttered, impatiently. "I will forget the matter.'' As he turned the corner of the avenue he beheld a natty little phaeton, drawn by a coal-black pony, whirling rapidly toward him. Rutledge's heart beat with pleasure, and his face flushed as he saw that its occupants were Verlie and Neddy Temple. Neddy's sharp eyes had been the first to discern him. "Look who's coming, Verlie !" she exclaimed. "Good gracious ! bok at his face ! —it's the colour of my flaming, red silk umbrella. He's blushing like a schoolboy as his eyes fell upon . you." It was now Verlie's turn to smile and blush con- fusedly. , "Well, I never saw such a pair of lovers as you two are, anyhow," cried Neddy, laughing uproari- ously, much to bashful Verlie's distress. Neddy saw Rutledge had drawn close to the edge of the pavernent, and was waiting for them to appro,ch ; and, seized with the irrepressible spirit, of iniehicf that characterised her, the grasped the reins and the dainty ivory riding whip from Verlie's hand, and with a cut from the whip which the pony was not soon to forget they whirled with the velocity of the wind past Rutledge and on down the avenue, withent giving him an instant of time in which to salute them. ' "Oh Neddy, you little vixen, he will feel so hurt about this," cried Verlie, as soon as she could regain' her breath: but Neddy only laughed the _ more uproariously. "He will have you to himself soon enough," ithe declared. "I was determined he should not have not one single moment oi your 1%407 this morning."</p>
        <p>As they rode along through the golden sunlight —girl-like, they fell to discussing the approaching wedding, and where it was to take place. "Mrs. Renwick is trying to induce me to have the marriage take place here, while mamma is equally anxious that it shall take place at our home near Richmond; but father has quite a different view of the matter, and I must say I like his idea the best," smiled Verlie, "and that is that I shall be married where I was born—and that is at Black-Tor Light-House, on the Florida coast."</p>
        <p>"Black-Tor Light-House !" echoed Neddy, aghast. "Why, Verlie Sefton, I actually think you have lost your senses. I—l thought you were going to make an elegant, fashionable affair of it," she cried disappointedly. "That will make it none the less so to be married there," V erlie answered, serenely. "In- deed, it is one of the most weird, romantic spots in the whole wide world."</p>
        <p>" Too weird," cried Neddy, shrilly. "I have al- ways heard and read such strange tales and tradi- tions about these isolated light-houses, that some- how the very mention of one makes me shiver. It is a marvel to me that you and Uldene could ever have passed your childhood there." "I was contented—Uldene never was," sighed Verlie, thoughtfully. "Her one longing was to break away from its monotonous loneliness and see the gay, bright world beyond. She was very much like you, Noddy, in those old days. It makes illy heart ache when I think of Uidene," said Verlie, in a low voice. "I loved her so well I would have willingly given my life for her if would have saved her.''</p>
        <p>"In that case you would never have been Mrs. Rutledge Chester, replied Neddy. The words were thoughtlessly, carelessly spoken, and Noddy repented having spoken them the mo- ment after they were uttered. "Heaven forbid that I should be his wife at the. expense of Uldene's life," murmured Verlie. "I, who would have suffered death to have saved her 1" "Forgive me for bringing up such unpleasant. thoughts, dear," sobbed impulsive Neddy, throw- ing her arm about Verlie. "We will forges all about it by going to see the flower show. Every one we know will be there. After that you shall go with me to try on my dress • it's to be my bridemaid dress. Look out, Verlie, I may out- shine you at the grand affair, if you're not care- ful," laughed Noddy. "You are certainly pretty enough to," replied Verlie. "I will be very pleased to go with you to see it tried on."</p>
        <p>"I went yesterday to see about it, but there was so much confusion there about some girl who had been taken suddenly ill, that I concluded to wait until to-day," said Neddy. "We will take the poor girl who has been taken ill some fruit and flowers and a few delica- cies," suggested the generous hearted Verlie. "It would be a perfectly splendid idea," chimed in Neddy. Au hour later the phteton stopped before the humble cottage that sheltered Uldene, (To be continued.)</p>
