      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PARTED BY FATE;</head>
        <p>PARTED BY FATE;</p>
        <p>OR THE MYSTERY OF BLACK-TOR LIGHTHOUSE.</p>
        <p>BY LAURA JEAN LIEBEY,</p>
        <p>Author of "lone, the Pride of the Mill," &amp;c.,,dr,c,</p>
        <p>CFIAFTER XXXI. 17f.DENE FINDS A FRIEND.</p>
        <p>_ Uldene was conducted by her strange companion into a narrow side street, and after traversing it for some length, she stopped before a small, unpre- tentious cottage, and opening the gate, she bade Uldene follow her in.</p>
        <p>"I have two small rooms here," Miss Lennox explained. "I rent them of the widow lady who owns the cottage. I am employed in the dress- making establishment of Madame Dubois, on A Avenue."</p>
        <p>Uldene was ushered into a scantily furnished but scrupulously neat apartment, and bade to remove her hat and sacque. In vain during the hours that followed were Miss Lennox's attempts to draw from her guest's lips her history—who she was, or from whence she came.</p>
        <p>"I have seen a ghost of the past"; it has con- fronted me," Uldene muttered silently to herself. But aloud she answered: "I did feel a little faint. lam better now. flave I ruined the surah silK ?"</p>
        <p>"I took it from you just in time to prevent your doing so," smiled Emily. "I was telling you about the wedding which this is to be worn at," she went on, holding the half-fashioned dress off at arm's length to admire it.</p>
        <p>"It quite startled me when I read the announce- ment of handsome Rutledge Chester's approaching marriage. It carried me back to a pitiful para- graph I read in the papers about two years ago, about the death of his first bride. Ah, me how time thee! Two years! "She lived in the great stone house on the hill, and they say she was as beautiful as a poet's dream—those who have seen her. I wonder that he could ever have forgotten her so far as to marry again, poor lady! Alas! nowadays they are scarcely off with the old love before they are on with the new. "It was rumoured a few months ago that Mr. Chester was to wed pretty Neddy Temple when they met abroad last bear ; but it seems that the rumour was false. He had another lady-love in his mind's eye. As tor Miss Neddy—Miss Dean, sureiy you are ill! You are going to faint." "No !" muttered Uldene, piteously. But, despite her denial, she suddenly threw up her white hands and fell face downward in a death- like swoon to the floor at Emily's feet. Poor, tortured soul 1 she had borne all she could. Had her heart broken with one awful throb in her bosom at last?</p>
        <p>Qaiclily calling in her landlady from her room across the passage-way, Emily explained what had occurred in a few brief words, and they lost no time in disrobing Uldene and bearing her to her couch.</p>
        <p>Tne usual remedies which they applied failed to bring back the fleeting breath to those pale lips, and, in alarm, the nearest physician Wa summoned.</p>
        <p>"It's a bad case of brain fever, induced by some great and sudden shock," was the doctor's verdict, as he bent over the beautiful, hapless Uldene. "I fear the youna lady is destined to be confined to her bed for many a weary week." "Is it dangerous? 1 mean, do you think she will die, sir ?"</p>
        <p>"It is a pretty severe case," replied the doctor, inbiously. "Ba+, while there is life there is hope. The chances are evenly balanced as to her recovery or—''</p>
        <p>' "Her death ?" breathed Emily, in a low voice. The doctor nodded. If Emily could have read the future, she would have prayed Heaven to take Uldene then and there.</p>
        <p>CLIAPTER PREPARATIONS FOR VERLIE'S WEDDING GO STEADILY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>CORRR-q'ONDENCE WITH Mh. S. WOODS, M.P. 1 Our St. Helens cormsbondea sends the lollowing,. on Monday front Mr. s. ‘Vood*, the Labour ,c,eitib:,r for Ince : RaLiunal Liberal Club,Whit,Thill-place, S. W. Jarluilry 13,1894. Dear Sir,—ln the name of that form of solidarity that- , gaininq sirength day by day, both amongst working tiAl in every country and amongst Labour representa-- vi-s iii evt-ry Parliament, I beg to ask whether you will . rite it word of.sy mpathv for the Labour M.P., ,:iignor- q•epli de Felice GinfTrida, who has just been illegally ?,rrested to Italy, his sole guilt consisting, in being the iend -and Organiser of the down-trodden, famished, mil helpless Sicilian peasants and sulphur miners ? As Daly no member can be arrested, or even prosecuted hout the con ,ent of the house, unless guilty of COM-• ron law crilnes, avd as the voice of public opinion in England has ;;Iways acted as a stong deterrent upon tb lorei4fn Governments as have violated constitu- :onal laws and trampled upon civil liberties, an expres- si,w of sympzithy from you for this member of the Italian Chamber who dares to uphold the rights of a class. i,mefully treated by landlords and masters and over- ...t by the authorities, would be regarded throughout to,e. whole Peninsula as a prote:q against the present 'shiner. This Cabinet, after sl:Goring down more than 200 ,tArving peasants and disso!ving many Lthourers' Unions (•• FascAlei Lavoratori ") has now declared Sicily a slate of siege—the last ecsource of barbarism.— ours, &amp;c., PAUL VALERA, London Correspondent of the" Sucolo." TO S. Woods, Esq., 111 P.</p>
        <p>13, Royal-avenue, Chelsea, London. January 14, 1894. Dear Sir,—On my arrival in town to-day I found your avert' duty waiting, to whicti I hasten to offer a humble ,•ply. I read of the arrest of Signnr Joseph de Felice .;inffrida, the Labour M.P., and patriot in Italy, for ,inmitting the unpardonable sin of defending the p"Oor, he helo:es,,and down-trodden in their struggles against he monopolists, the mighty, and the privileged, with a ,udoering diguAt and a deep feeling of shame for the . ohnc authorities of that country. To think that such event could take place in the nineteenth century,and the land ot Dante, Garibaldi, and Joseph Mazzini, R.here the people have declared their independence on. .;i.any occasions in the past, is enough to baffle the 7•te,irest comprehension ; but as it has been from the ginning, and in every country in the world, the r,ater the opposition the grosser the tyranny and the user the clouds which overhang the onward march of .I.,,rty and freedom, the sooner it will make its appear- ty:Ceand the day of emancipation dawn. Tell my friend -)Olding the same position, engaged in the same noble . Horse, but it may be using different weapons on ,cnount of the different circumsances, that in his suffer- li,, has my entire sympathy, and I feel sure the mpathy and prayers of my colleagues in the House of Commnits, in the adverse position in which he is at pre- ',lA. placed. He may be for the present shut out from time, friends, hnd the world ; hut let this thought cheer h,re,— hat he suffers in a noblo cause, that his present sufferings will crown him wi:b honour, and work out an ii:•aictilabl, amount of glory and progress to the cause ior which he at present suffers. Kindly assure him of my entire sympathy and congratulations, and believe to he, yours obediently, in the sacred cause of the ple, S. WOODS. I Paul Valera, F,sq,</p>
        <p>WHY is trouble always brewing? Why net baking, butchering, washing, or doing something else besides this everlastinghrewing ? "Tins room is very close," remaelted a guest to the head waiter of a New York Btoadway restaurant; "can't I have a little fresh air?" The well-drilled automaton raised his voice to a high pitch "One air!" he yelled, after a pause adding, "Let it 13,: fresh !" IF we were to choose the most appropriate aymbol of the fleeting, the evanescent, the perish- able, the decaying, the here-to-day-and-gone-to- morrow, perhaps it would be a pair of boy's boots.</p>
        <p>" Rich the treasure, Sweet the pleasure, Sweetest pleasure after pain."</p>
