      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON SOCIAL PROBLEMS.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening the second lecture ef the Winter series in connection with Runcorn Liberal • Club was given on Wednesday evening to a good • audience. Mr. B. Kniveton was the lecturer and dealt with the subject of "Social problems and their `-aOlUtion." Mr. R. H. Posnett occupied the chaireind in opening the proceedings, said there had been a grea deal of discussion lately about having lectures in connec- tion with the club, and a number of them had felt that they were devoting too much time to recreation and to, little to the literary part at the club ; and there were Many requests made to the Committee to get up perio, -Cal lectures during the winter session. The Coinintttee had arranged a course and that was the second lecture. 'The Committee wished the scheme to be a thorough go- ing one for be felt that it would give permanency to tie club. They wished to cater tor all sorts, and speaking aa one of the Committee they were pleased te get up these lectures, and they bad arranged for another out- by one of the secretaries ot the Financial Reform Union rroceeding, the Chairman said the present Goverinnent Would be remembered for one thing, namely, the intes- tine:Mon of the eight hours system in the Government hotories. (Applause.) He believed that the moral force af the action cf this Government in that direction woo li' Ultimately result in every works being placed upon tile 'eight hours principle. It was one of the greatest aehieve- *lents of the present century. (Applause.) • Mr. Kniveten said when considering the progress made ill the last half century in education, sanitation, an( I averything that affected the welfare of the people it nled strange that they had not yet realised their ideal What the world ought to be. Their subject covered e "17 wide area and they could only touch on (1/ne of the principal points. The labour problem was elle of very great difficulty and was most perplexing li() all social reformers, philanthropists and statesmen. A urge number of the Labour classes were in receipt, ef nuon wages, and tolerably constant work, but in the section known as unskilled labourers, there was a num- -I_3er approaching three quarters of a million who had to pbsist on low wages, and whose work was very irre,/.0- 'ar• These persons went largely to swell the ranks ot c4:lr Paupers. The thoughts of intelligent men had peen d lareeted to this great social question and they aske ~i d lentliselves what they could do to give the lowest classes ut labour to elevate them to a state of independence. The -re was the great sweating question, which was one ,n,f the greatest blots of the present age, and the lecturer iilustrated the fearful extent to which it had been, car- d. Then there was dangerous and unhealthy occupa.• 113 es• The number of fatal accidents in our industrial establishments amounted to something like 3,000 a year. What did this represent in widows and orphans ; ninety- Cent . of these men were bread winners of al :re"37, and their removal by accident and frequently LW accidents ot a preventable character, must entail an ftinL°llnt of misery and wretchedness to those left behind, lykuo too often swelled the ranks of the pauper qlass. ,p iLuen there were trades in which little regard seemed to Zheapaid to the health of the workers. The long home men worked the badly constructed nuilaings in Which • they did their work, the insanitary condition Surrounding them during the greater portion of their life :salt these tended to lower the tone of their physical l(lealth, produced disease and in many cases premature ..eath, and all this from preventible causes. As to Vessive hours one could scarcely imagine that in these c7YB of trades unionism and organisations of all des- _riPtions that it would be possible that workmen Should be taxed to the extent of lie 15, and 16 hours a V. Nevertheless they were assured on undisputable evidence that auch was the case. Auother problem was sanitation while great strides had been made in this Pect, and while every town in England was possibly liuperior to what it was fifty years ago, yet every town lad its slums, its courts and its alleys, where it was ittipossible that health and morals and the comforts of life could be enjoyed to the extent they ought to be. We knew for a fact that these places produced drunkards and paupers to a very fearful extent, and it was one of the most perplexing problems of the day how beet to rat with them. Next came the probletn of the drink Lraffic. We could not escape the great fact that we were eareeedingly prodigal with our money in this direction. f`. hundred and forty millions were spent annually upon Intoxicating drink, one half of which was practically so !'huell wasted wealth. If half of that sum were directed !titell:)ther channels we should have such a state of trade n l t_l is country that there would not be a single man out Of labour °l.k who wished to work, or one badly paid for his that • These were some of the social problems Confronted us at the present day. At the time we had made wonderful strides dur- ing he Past fifty years. There was now an amount e exceeding Of the million pounds standing to the credit chiefly working classes in our Post Office and trustee 13,77:prs banks. Then there were the savings r.preAented tliat thee,o-operative socieies and building societies, so</p>
        <p>, . vs_ _: accumsuated savings of the working clases of sutWountry could not be much less than 200 millions Ttivd!tr!o2a we had a diminished criminal population. I n ung• This was a very satisfactory state of things. last Lila/Jen something like fifty per cent. during the ~, ilialt centurv,ancl there were several of her Majesty's I,,,acc on sale at the present time, and if things went on rno the 'anrie way.we should not require Knutsford many hadre, Troelars. The spread of education and other agencies u 1, •</p>
        <p>were confronted with the problems he had acts :ght ahout this change. Notwithst andirig these f, we 117,1:,•ti,nhed, and the object of his address was to indi,cas. te 'w•ple--- 'le conceived to be a solution of these problems. - 'tst, the labour uestion. What shall be With' done the areat garmy of unemployed men, chieftY Skilled labourers ? They authori contact with ty nf men who came in cont. had it on the t_ileth that there were very few of the vicious class cantritiobangnsnttthem ; they were men anxious to work but olotaiti work. Various remedies had been r soa-. sted, and nne was shorter nours for those already work intigis. co'ruhideYdhd had a wonderful illustration of what in the vicinity. Brunner, Mond's Works at othersnwhichad and Mr. Platt's at Salford and one or two direction made a most interesting experiment in this althoucrh "Erunner, Mond and Co. had told them that i d b 'neV i Y, there had reduced the time to eieht hours per , t was no increase in the cost of production— testimony. 7 on— their products were produced as cheaply as • :_v_uen the men worked twelve hours a day. This was Loaarkahle testimony Another benefit accruing was a _trunished sickness arnongst the men; their health had -at a higher level, and they could produce as -.La:leen work ,_ eight '0 hours as they did in twelve hours eii, ~.svin--.Y• A further satisfactory result was that the heswin had diminished drunkenness. Under this eight 613141.8 n: sYstern, Brunner, Mond and Co. employed of so-lt;e Men and here they saw their one way h 1,,,-- p ...v.,ng the question of the unemployed. eir- toa—siins large increase of men would not app y, wrnanufactures hut still if the eight hours !re generally adopted scores of thousands of :gen Walking about the streets starving, men anxious 4 for ond iwiiii work, would have honest employment th olution of und em. Another movement that tended to the S n the question was the action of General Booth i connection with his social scheme. Mr. liniveton ,t tlo fe roro d t h e w o r k carried on on General Booth, sfarm kocl. acr'bs in O Essex, and said he was on the right lines . ime w enefactors d prove that he had been one of the greatest oulof the 19thcentury. Continuing Mr.Knive_e tosn Ilaid we found many or these men in our towns because they could not earn a livelihood in our villages simply on account of our had English land laws. Competant !Iticuiturists told us that the land in England was starv- i"2?oN; for labour and here we had hundreds of thousands of urers starving for want of labour. We wanted to i*,rl,l;teg the two together; but the bad laws prevented such 41 ,ctsirable consumation. I\4. Kniveton gave an illus- , ion ef this which had just occurred in Cheshire, a ' 1k49d1 Practically going out of cultivation because the tv ,lord would not give the tenant security for the capi- wLoe was prepared to sink in the land, and there , ih?,s, consequently a areat loss of national wealth eli'le diminished production of the soil. We spent Noti:'°oB a year in foreign produce a great portion of tn7could be grown at home,but for bad landlaws. It hsolutely necessary for the welfare of the corn- for the workmen and the wealth of the nation this constant influx of country labourers into the NILO should he stopped. They hoped soon by a vigorous surZY to turn the landlaws upside down and make them iatisilisi that the tanner should feel that every pound he tilt, Di hit° the soil was as safe to him as though put into tio4"ank of England.Mr.E:niveton also mentioned emi gra- -1 ' ,a, a relief to the unemployed and dealing with .the Iva 'ling system said he believed we were on the high- ad Y.for seehlinistrative policy pursued by Mr. Asquith would it solving this problem. The wise, and vigorous thntlled to him, shortly kill the sweating system, and be conditions of labour were such now that it would °el's, difficult indeed for sweating' to be continued,</p>
        <p>and that a better state of things would prevail for those who had been justly described :fls white slaves. In regard unheilthy dwellings eight per cent of the population of this country lived in two roomed tenements. This was not a 3 it should be. Every honest man who worked, who was industrious and thrifty ought to have a four- roomed tenement. Here was the land question again. How was it that men and women had to live in places one than those in which horses and cattle via re housed It was because of the enormous ground rents paid to men who took from the land of this country enormous in- comes, and never contributed sixpence or an hours' labour to that which so greatly enriched them. In Run- corn they saw the same thing operating. There was a field not half a mile from that club, the ren` of which not more than four or five years ago was about £lO a Year. What was the ground landlord thawing to-day. Considerably more than £lOO, perhaps 200 (A. voice :over I think) and yet that landlord had never contributed sixpence towards the improvement of the land, never even paid rates to the town except en agricul- tural values. It was on account of the land laws of the country—the ground rents and the • le4se system, that we had the Jerry builder constructing dwellings in which human beings could not live and be healthy and moral and happy, and it was not until they dealt vigorously with this land question and curtailed the power of the ground landlord, and com- pelled him to contribute to the improvement of the pro- perty from which he derived princely revenues, that we could expect much improvement in sanitation. It would be one of the grandest achievements of the closing years of the 19th century if we could sweep all the slums and alleys out of existence 'rid proclaim that the age of the jerry builder and the slum squire should cease. (Applause.) If we could deal effectively with the drink question the • ffe,ts upon the social, educational and moral condition e the country would be most marvellous. Supposing o it export trade tell off thirty millions, the effect would • felt in every workshop in England. Suppose we re-. aced our national drink bill of 140 millions by one half —we could never expect to make everybody teetotallers. eti surely £70,000,000 a year would he sufficient for all reasonable drinking purposes—and the other seventy millions was spent in clothing, pictures,books,music and a!I the comforts and convenie ces that every workingman ,might to have in his house. Why we should see such a sate of trade in this country of which we had no pos- sibla conception. And this was work we could do our- s eves. We might depend upon it that so long as we eontinued to spend 140 millions a year in drink, so long should we have to battle with these social problems this terrible incubus of pauperism,and destitution and want, and every workingman ought to look upon the drink tretaie as one of the greatest cures of his country, and corn o the conclusion that the beet side of a public-house was the outside. In conclusion Mr. Kniveton urged his La arers to teach his own sphere of daily life,and endea- veur to stimulate public opinion upon these things, by talking about them,and attempt to make themselves and those who lived and worked with them better. By thus acting we should contribute to the sum of human happi-. et.; s and bring about that desirable period when this world would be as good a world to live in as it was eess hie for good legislation to make it. (Loud ap- plause.) A discussion followed in which Messrs. W. Handley, Page, and Boyle, and a stranger from London, who an- nounced himself a social democrat took part. The opinion was several times expressed that the temperance societies and the religious bodies were not taking their share in the social movements of the time. At the call of the Chairman Mr. liniveton was thanked for his interesting and eloquent address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY. In the House of Commons on Friday, Sir J. T. FIIIIBERT, replying to questions by Mr. Molloy, said the quantity of quartz crushed in North Wales during the Last seven years was 43,600 tons, yielding 23,718 ounces of gold or nearly lldwts. to the ton. When any definite prcposal 'as made to work the goldfields on Crown lands the-Government would carefully consider it, with the desire to facilitate the development of the industry. Mr. Bryce was interrogated by Mr. A. MORTON as to the legality of fees, varying from £6 6s. to £lO, wl.ich he suggested the clerks of the peace in Lancashire charged upon newly-apPointed justicesi. Mr. BRYCE said he was informed that the charges on the appointment of a county magistrate were five guineas for the writ, includ- ing payments, and two guineas fee to the clerk of the peace. He promised to make inquiry as to the legality of the charges. It was resolved, on the motion of Mr. GLADSTONE, that as soon as the Local Government (England and Wales) Bill was passed the House at its rising should ad- journ until Monday, the 12th of February., Mr. A. C. Morton obtained leave to bring in a bill to prohibit the hunting, coursing, and shooting of animals kept in con- finement. The Parish Councils Bill was further consi- dered as amended in committee, and several amend- ments were dealt with. The report stage was completed at five minutes to twelve, amid cheers, and Mr. FOWLER moved that the bill be read a third time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>The House of Lords held a formal sitting on Monday in order to give a first reading to the Local Govern- ment (Parish Councils) Bill. The Marquis of RIPON said the second reading would be set uown for Friday pro .fornid. It was honed that the Bill would pass through all its stages before the House of Commons re- assembled on the 12th February.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>FIRE AT AT HAYDOCK COTTAGE HOSPITAL</head>
        <p>FIRE AT AT HAYDOCK COTTAGE HOSPITAL</p>
        <p>A STARTLING OCCURiIENCE.</p>
        <p>At midnight on Wednesday; a fire was discovered near the fireplace in the lower ward of the new wing, of the Haydock Cottage Hospital. It appears that when the new wing was erected about twelve months ago, some rubbish was left in the space be- tween the concrete bottom of the fireplace, and the fire- proof bottom of the grate. and this had apparently ignited, and set fire to the joists of the wooden floor. Shortly after twelve o'clock Sister Lloyd, matron of the hospital, whose bedroom adjoins the ward, was ;:wakened by a strong smell Of burning. On going into the ward she found the place filled with smoke, and al- most suffocating. Three patients, two men and a boy, were asleep, but one of these called out " Sister" as Miss Lloyd went into the room. The other nurses in the hospital were called, and the patients were removed into another ward. Buckets were then carried by the nurses and thrown over the fireplace, but as smoke con- tinned to issue from between the boards of the floor, Dr. Hayward, medical officer, P,)lice-constable Addison, and a joiner from Holly Bank were called. A per- tinn of the floor was then broken up and the fire extin- guished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>TEMPERAMENT.</head>
        <p>TEMPERAMENT.</p>
        <p>Temperament is not without its influence on disease. We all recognise the existence of different varieties of temperament ; even the least observant mentally con- trasts the typical nervous, excitBble Frenchman with his dull, heavy, phlegmatic Dutch confrere. Many temper- aments are readily recognisable. First, there is the man with sanguine temperament; he is quick and lively in his manner, has an excitable pulse,a florid skin, a flushed face, eats and digests well, and sleeps quickly. He is generally thought to be pre-disposed to inflammation, but this is net so in reality, and almost the only thing I lie is especially likely to suffer from is an accident, the ' result of his plucky, daring, and impetuosity. Then there is the man of phlegmatic or lymphatic tempera- ment, with his cold hands, pallid skin ,and fair complex. ion. He usually eats well, has a fair amount of ability, awl a cool, calm, calculating disposition. He is rather liable to suffer,from the effects of cold, and in winter often has chilblains. Next there is the billious or melan- cholic individual ; he is usually dark, both physically and mentally ; he has dark hair, and dark eyebrows, and a sallow complexion. He is a heavy sleeper, an,Z is often languid and tired. He suffers from disturbed digestion, a coated tongue, constipation, and flatulence. He is not by any means a good patient, and when he gets typhus or typhoid fever it is apt to go hard with flim. Lastly, there is the nervous man,fidgety, restless, easily excited, easily depressed, up one moment and oown the next. He is impulsive, but soon gets tired of his hobby, and takes up something else. He is usually a short sleeper, and any excitement or anything wrong with his affairs will often keep him awake all night. He is a likely subject for tic and spasm of all kinds, and is on the whole not an unlikely person to become flys- -1 terical. These are the chief forms of temperament, but it must be remembered that two or more may be com- bined in the same individual. Fortunately, we are not all built on one of these four types.—" The Family Physician."</p>
        <p>--A7TIEE‘ RING RE31.4.1.—A raven-hoarse tenor was tormenting the ears of an audience of opera- goers. One--only one—cheered him lustily. "What o earth do you mean by it?" asked an indignant neighbour. "Whs. I want to cheer Lim till he bursts?" trivia irtsurnen'were-aliaisSing a friend who had just died. "Faith it's a long slape he'll be havin' thi,3 toime," remarked Pat, dolefully. "Sure It's a. cake he'll be havin' as well, thin," replied Mike, with an expectant air of comingenjoyment. BENRE the wedding day she was dear and he was a treasure, but afterwards she became dearer and he the treasurer.</p>
