      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>111 E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY' 20, 1894,</head>
        <p>111 E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY' 20, 1894,</p>
        <p>THE Wr4AN MIRACLE.</p>
        <p>A WOMAN'S RESCUE.</p>
        <p>[From the " Wigan Observer."] Take an ordinary map of England, a map such as is used in our schools, and you will probably not find a place called Bindley; in Lancashire, marked upon it. But it would be wrong to conclude because Hindley is t marked on the map that it is an insignificant place. Hindley is a town of considerable importance in the Wigan coalfield. It has a population of about 20,000. Cotton spinning mills and colleries meet the eye at every turn. It has a free library, a public park, two repre- sentatives on the Wigan Board of Guardians, a repre sentative on the Lancashire County Council, and it is one of the most important parts of the Ince Division, now represented in Parliament by Mr. Sam. Woods, M.P. The residents are proud of the place, and they have every reason to be. There is a woman in Hindley who is very proud of something in her possession, and even a philosopher would not condemn her for it. The other day a " Wigan Onserver " reporter had a chat with this woman. He called purposely to learn all about a subjct which has created a great local sensation, and found the lady in question justly proud of a discovery she had made. Her name (says our representative) is Mrs. Henry Lloyd Roberts, and I found her residing at 201, Stoney-lane, Hindley, near Wigan. It was a gloomy, miserable day when I called, and the drizzling rain made it still more dreary. Once inside the house I forgot the miserable conditions outside, as Mrs. Roberts, in a bright chatty way, told me the story of her trouble and of her triumph. It all happened this year. A change came over her, and the world seemed no longer bright, nor life wcrth living. The woes of indigestion and nervousness came upon her with terrible force. "I could not eat anything," she told me, "and I was so nervous that when I only ex- pected a letter I was all of a flutter. I could not sleep. In fact, I was afraid of going to sleep, for fear I should not wake again. A knock at the door drove me wild. If I walked a yard my heart would palpitate, and I had got to think I had heart disease. When I went out I walked like a snail. It was agony! When I suffered from pal- pitation I could not move about. My face and lips were pale, and people used to stare at me like a walking ghost. I was bad for a long while, and I went to an infirmary from June to the end of October last year. When I went I thought I should have died on the way. They did me no good ; indeed, I was worse when I stopped than when I began. The neighbours said, 'You do look bad! Mrs. Roberts again casually referred to her pale face, and I glanced at her and remarked, "It is not pale now."</p>
        <p>"No," replied Mrs. Roberts with a smile, "I think I look as well now as ever I did."</p>
        <p>" Tell me what brought about this change ?" I asked. c;Then Mrs. Roberti told me that one day she was read- ing the" Wigan Observer" and there she made the discovery which led to her triumph. She read an account of the wenderful cures effected in Lancashire by Dr. William's Pink Pills for Pale People, and she said to her husband, " Don't you think these will do me good ?" He, as a good husband would do, was quite agreeable to her trying them. She wrote for a supply, and the first three boxes did her good. " I have had eight boxes altogether," she said, and there was a ring of triumph in her voice as she finished the sentence, saying, "They really have done me a wonderful amount of good. I intend to go on with the Pills," she added. "The pal- pitation is nothing to what it was. Before I c ommenced with them I could only eat cornflour." "You had to live on slops, I suppose ?" "Yes," replied Mrs. Roberts, " but now," she said (and again there was that ring of triumph in her voice) "I can eat a chop." She drew a small box from her pocket and showed me the attractive Dr. Williams' Pink Pills which had wrought such a wonderful transforma- tion in her health.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Roberts has spoken of their virtues to several persons, and recommended them to take Dr. Williams' Peak rills. "I would advise anyone troubled with their nerves to try them," she said. Before she used the Pills she said she had regular attacks (lasting forty-eight hours) of a neuralgic headache, from which she had been a great sufferer. She spoke of the agony she en- dured, and of the " remedies " she tried to no purpose.' As the reporter rose to go, Mrs. Roberts said the Pills had, beyond all question, done her the good described. That was the truth, and other people ought to know it, as they might be benefited by the knowledge. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are one of the greatest modern medicines, an unfailing cure for rheu- matism, neuralgia, paralysis,locomotor ataxy, St. Vitus, dance, nervous headache, and prostration ; diseases of the blood, such as scrofula, chronic erysipelas, &amp;c. They are a splendid tonic, and restore pale and .sallow com- plexions to the glow of health ; a specific for all the trou- bles peculiar to the female sex ; and in men they effect a radical cure of all cases arising from worry, overwork, or excesses of whatever nature.</p>
        <p>The Pills are sold by all chemists, or may be had direct from the Dr. Williams Medicine Company, of 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, England (and of Brockville, Canada), in wooden boxes bearing the trade mark and wrappers at 24. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 1.35. 9d. Des- criptive pamphlet sent free by post on application. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are never sold loose, and any dealer who offers substitutes in this form is trying to defraud you ; the box must be in a pink wrapper bearing the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. The price at which they are sold makes a course of treatment comparatively inexpensive as compared with other remedies or medical treatment.</p>
        <p>ImPovvAtlsnEn blood leads to all sorts of diseases, surdi as anxmia and scrofula Impoverished blood is impos-ilde when Seott's Emulsion is taken, for Scott's Emulsion feeds and enriches the blood with substances invaluable to health and strength. This is the wqy Scott's Emulsion builds up the system—through the blood. Ph)sicians, the world over, endorse it. Chemists sell it. 2s. 6d, and 4s. 644.—(16)</p>
