      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>IRISH NATIONALISM IN RUNCORN. MR. W. ABRAHAM, ?LP., ON CURRENT POLITICS.</head>
        <p>IRISH NATIONALISM IN RUNCORN. MR. W. ABRAHAM, ?LP., ON CURRENT POLITICS.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Michael Davin (Run- corn) branch of the Irish National League of Great Britain,a public meeting was helçl in the Foresters' , Rail, Devonshire-square, Runcorn, on Friday night Ist week. There was not a very numerous atten- dance. Mr.J. W. Boyle,the president of the branch, occupied the chair, and there were on the platform Mr. W. Abraham, M.P. for North-east Cork; Councillors O'Donnell and Gough, Widnes ; Mr. Murphy, organising secretary of the district, Birmingham ; Messrs.B.Knive- ton,J. Clarke, P.Noon, M. Gilligan (Runcorn). O'fiagan, Campbell, Horan (Widnes), and E. Kennedy (branch secretary). 'the Chairman said the Irish National League was a democratic institution. The members believed in the policy of the people being governed by the peopls and for the people, They had as president in England Mr. T. P, O'Connor—(applause)—a gentleman who had won the hearts of his fellow-countrymen all over the world, and had gained the respect and esteem of thousands of Englishmen. By his wonderful powers he had advanced his position in the world. Proceeding, he spoke of the House of Lords having thought proper to shelve the Home Rule Bill. Some persons seemed to have the idea that as a consequence the measure was dead, but he might remind them that it was a great Bill which would certainly have a resurrection day. So long as there were Irishmen alive who loved their country, and had a desire to elevate their countrymen and bring about a higher state of things,there would always be men cs mime to the front on behalf of the sister isle. They were in- formed ny certain parties that if Home Rule were car- ried there would be a portion of the inhabitants of Ire- land annihilated on account of their religious opinions. It was a great mistake to embrace such an idea. In the past,lrish Dissenters and Catholics had stood shouloer o sheulder in defence of their rights ; the same thing existed to-day, and it would continue in the future. (Applause.) There were Catholics and Protestants fight- ing for their country,aud so long as they were combined it was impossible for the minority to be crushed. He did not think such men as Mr. Abraham, who was a Protes- tant, would stand up for that measure if they thought that the evil prophecies would be fulfilled. Mr. P. Noon moved the following resolution :—"That we, the Irishmen of Runcorn, take this opportunity of expressing our full confidence in the Irish Parliamentary party, acting under the chairmanship of Mr. Justin M'Carthy, and also our belief that their continual efforts united with those of the Radical party in Parliament, will bring about a speedy settlement of the Home Rule question." In doing so he referred to the House of Lords being a great obstacle in the way of reform in this country, and remarked that if the people only united, the obstruction would be removed. T'ne Peers did not , represent the feelings of the people, and consequently they had no right to oppose their expressed desires, as , shown by returning to power a Government pledged to give Home Rule to Ireland. Mr. Gilligan seconded. He contrasted the political situation ten or twelve years ago with that of the present day, and stated that he was sure that all Irishmen must he proud of the progress made through the arduous labours of a determined Irish party. They, however ' had not yet finished their work. There was still the House of Lords to be dealt with, and if Mr. Gladstone only accomplished this as effectively as he had dealt with the Liberal Unionists and Tories in the House of Com- mons, the Peers would soon be brought to their senses. If the Liberal party permitted the House of Lords to dictate to the House of Commons, good government would be at an end. Mr. Abraham, M.P., who was received with applause, referred to the pleasure he experienced in meeting a number of his fellow-countrymen in Cheshire, and then expressed his acknowledgment of the resolution sub- mitted, because it spoke of their confidence in the Irish party under the leadership of Mr. McCarthy, and con- tained the hope that notwithstanding the opposition of the House of Lords to the passing of th 6 Home Rule Bill, they would by the aid of the Radical party soon have an Irish Parliament assembled in Dublin to make laws on their own soil. Let them review their position' let them look at a few of the political problems of the day and take stock at the close of the session which would pro- bably end osi the morrow, and see if the progress made was sufficiently satisfactory to cause them to support the Liberal Government, and in that division to vote for their worthy representative, Mr. Brunner. (Applause.) He wished to pass under notice the administrative action of Mr. Gladstone's Government, and the measures which he had carried through the House of Commons. In the first place with regard to the Home Rule Bill, which had passed through its various stages in the Commons after meeting with extraordinary obstruction, they had rea- son to congratulate themselves upon what had been ac- complished, for although it had its defects, he was not afraid to declare that in the judgment of Irish represen- tatives the Bill was a large, a wide and a good measure of Home Rule for Ireland, and was one which in Irish hands and in the Irish Parliament would confer upon their people at home the inestimable boon of managing their own affair's, developing the resources of their coun- try, and giving them the opportunity of living in comfort, decency, and prosperity. (Applause.) The Bill occupied 82 days in the House of Commons,and yet one of the objections taken to it by the House of Lords was that it had not been fully discussed, although they dismissed it after a debate extending over 33 hours. They were not suprised at the action of the Lords; indeed if they had acted otherwise they would have been perfectly astonished, for every child knew that there had not been a single measure promoted for the benefit of the people which had not met with obstruc- tion at the hands of the hereditary legislators. The speaker instanced the Reform Bill of 1832, the repeal of the paper duty, the corn laws, free education and other matters, which had been obstructed in their passage by the Upper House. He showed how necessary it was for the Irish party to support the present Government in carrying out the mandates of the people at the last general election, avid stated that if these met with the same fate as the Homo Rule Bill, they would appeal to the country, when they had perfected their machinery, and then they would obtain the verdict of the electors as to whit action should be taken with the House of Lords. Not withstanding, the unprecedented opposition met with, they had abundant proof that the present Government was the hest they had had for many years. It was not to be judged simply 15v the Acts passed and placed on the statute book, but by its adminis- trative power and laws, which were far in advance of any former Liberal Government. This they saw in the Education Department and the Board of Trade, the latter of which had passed an Act to regulate the hours of railway servants, by which those agerieved could appeal to the Board of frade,who would set on foot an inquiry,and if the complaints were well founded, would compel the company to reduce the el women ronetmiarss:tn)i f made tathleeibri enseentri ateapnpnt ous nn tbeFedur rt otohtte hr factorye, otf. fih ecree. inspectors, r been ei ei an d bnae and - inquiries instituted into dangerous trades. The quali- fication for being a member of the Board of Guardians had been reduced to £5, so that it was now possible for the people to elect the man of their choice, who would know how to sympathize with the persons requiring</p>
        <p>assistance. These were proofs that showed that Irish- men ought to suppert Liberal Candidates. Irish- men claimed thot as le,virded their own country they were more advanced than the Radicals on this side of the water. welshie-n were trying to secure dise-stab- lislonent, which had alrea*y been grant-d to Ireland, so that every church there was dependent upon the free will offerings of the cengre,gation. He also instanced 'that with regard to the fixing of rents in Ireland they were on a superior footing to other parts of the United Kingdom. He proceeded to deal with the Parish Councils Bill. He expressed regret that the electors at Horncastle two days previously, had by their decision shown that they did not fully understand the benefits to be conferred upon them by it, for they did not return a member to support the Nlinisterialists. Mr. Abrahams showed that the Government had recognized the prin- ciple of an eight hours' day, for 20,000 workmen in the Government departments were to receive for 48 hours per week the same wages as they had hitherto obtained for 54 hours each week. (Applause.) This would also apply to the workshops of those who received contracts under the Government. The action would give a great impetus to the leaders of labour. It showed that the working classes had done right in placing Mr. Gladstone in power. (Applause.) The principle of shorter hours had been accepted, and the contention that more work would be better performed and at a less cost had been adopted. He was surprised that there was not a wave of enthusiasm throughout the country for the particular decision of the Government. The Li irrals had made an honourable undertaking to put Flom&amp; Rule in the fore- front, and this they had fulfilled, for they could not blame them for the action of the House of Lords. The Irish party during the next session would remain loyal to those who had been loyal to them, and would aid in overcoming the obstruction and the machinations of the Unionist and the Tory party by giving every support to the Government. (Applause.) In that particular divi- sion they might be tempted to be satisfied to rest on their laurels, seeing that they returned Mr. Brunner by a larger majority than on any previous occa- sion. But they ought not to relax their efforts, for much had yet to be accomplished. He urged the Irishmen to joln the local branch of the League, and thus show that they were in earnest in con- tending for their rights and privileges. It was their duty to render ail the aid they possibly could to the party which had rendered the people of Ireland so much ' assistance, so that they could pass measures which would be for the benefit of the people of the United Kingdom. (Applause.). The motion was unanimously carried. Mr. Murphy, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Abraham for his excellent address, expressed the opinion that with regard to Home Rule Irishmen were on the brink of a glorious triumph. He had every confidence in Mr. Gladstone—(applause)—and believed that that great statesman would find a way of effectually dealing with .the House of Lords. The League had made satis- factory progress, for in Great Britain there were about 2,000,000 Irishmen who were scattered in every town, village, and hamlet, and in many places they held the balance of political power. There were 600 branches of the organization in the country, and his desire was that every Irishman in and around Runcorn should become members of the League, and thereby render valuable service to the Liberal party. Mr. Kniveton seconded the motion. He congratul- ated the Irish party upon the advancement made since 1886, and expressed the opinion that there was every reason why they should take courage,for once a measure had passed through the House of Commons it was bound despite opposition,: to be placed on the statute book. Englishmen and Irishmen were working together for one common end, namely, the benefit of the people. The motion having been carried,.Mer. Abraham said a few words in acknowledgment, and at his request, seconded by Councillor O'Donnell, the meeting, accorded a vote of thanks to the Chairman, who it was stated was the right man in the right place as president of the local branch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>PARR'S BANKING COMPANY AND THE ALLIANCE BANK LIMITED.</head>
        <p>PARR'S BANKING COMPANY AND THE ALLIANCE BANK LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The 29th annual ordinary general meeting of this Company will be held at the Cannon-st. Hotel, London, on the 25th inst. The following report of the directors will be submitted :—" The Directors, in submitting to the shareholders the balance sheet of the Bank at 31st December, 1893, certified by the auditors, have to report that the position and pros- pects of the business continue to be in every way satisfactory. Including £33,858 4s. sd. brought forward at 30th June. 1893, the net balance of profits, after pro- viding amply for all bad and doubtful debts, and rebat- ing the current bills at 5 per cent., i5,£133,052 15s. 2d. From this sum the Directors recommend the payment of a dividend at the rate of 19 per cent. per annum, free of income tax, fdi• the quarter ended 31st December, 1893, to be payable on Ist February, 1894. £47,500; divi&amp;nd at the same rate for the quarter enoing 31st March, 1894, to be payable on Ist May, 1894, to the shareholders whose names shall be on the register on 19th Apr.l, £47,500; to be carried to property account, £5,000; leaving balance to be carried to next account, £33,052 154. 2d. A sub-branch of the Bank has been opened at Woodford, E.:sex. The Directors who retire at this time by rotation are Mr. E. W. Nix, Mr. E. Banbury, Mr. A. Hewlett, Mr. T. 11. Dixon, Mr. Alfred Turner, Mr. H. W. Meade King, and Mr. Cecil F. Parr, all of whom are eligible, and are recommended for re-eleotion. The auditors, Messrs. Turquand, Youngs, Bishop,and Clarke, London, and Messrs. Stead, Taybr and Stead, Liverpool, also retire, and are recommended for re-election."</p>
        <p>Thr, Royal Laundresses say Critchley's " STARCH GLos- is unequalled for imparting" a beautifnl finish to starchse linen, &amp;c., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn- wall, says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without yom "Starch Gloss." From Mrs. —, Bradford : "Dear sit . lam pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-calleo Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at Id., 3d., and 6d.„bN most starch selless. If not in stock will procure it. T. CRPTCALEY. BlACkburn. Pzoprietor.—Adst.</p>
        <p>Lord Bonnet, only serving son of the Earl of Tanker- ville, used to be a scoffer, but now travels with Dr. English, the British Evangelist, and conducts the singing at the revival services. Lord Bennet, who has a fine baritone voice and the prospect of inheriting a a large 'estate, is about to return to.Erwland, after a six month's campaign of revivals in the west. A despatch from the city of Mexico slys : " There is an unusually large number ot editors in gaol here at present.</p>
        <p>THROAT IRRITATION AND COTIGH.--qoreness and dryness. tickling and irritation,inducing cough and affecting the voice For these symptoms use Epps's Glycerine Jujubes. In con- tact w,th the glands at the moment they are excited by the at of sucking, the Glycerine hi these agreeable confections becomes actively healing. Sold only in boxe, 70.. tins Is. 10.. lapelled " JamEs EPPS "&amp;.. CO., Ltd., Homceopathic Chemists, London." Dr. Moore, in his work on " Nose and Tnro:it Diseases," says : " The Glycerine Jujubes prepared by James E pps and Co., are of undoubted service as a cura- tive or palliative agent," while Dr. Gordon Holmes, Senior Physician to the Municipal Throat and Ear Infirmary,writes: " After an extended trial. I have found your G'yeerine Jujubes of considerable benefit in almost all forms of throat disease."—Advt.</p>
