      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>CONFERENCE AND PUBLIC MEETING AT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The annual Session of the Cheshire Division of I tile Order of the Sons of Temperance was held on Monday in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom, G'reenway-road, Runcorn. Bro. Edward Hughes, G.W.A: of Chester, presided in the unavoidable ab- sence of Bro. Edward Key, G.W.P. There was a full attendance of officers, and representatives were Present from Chester, Crewe, Weston, Northwieh, Winsford, Hanley, and Runcorn. The Session open- ed at ten o'clock, and amongst the business tran- saction was the election of officers for the coming year, which resulted in the following appointments; —Bro. Edward Hughes, of Chester, G.W. Patriarch Bro. W. Heath,of Cre.we,G.W.A. ; Bro. S. M. TemperleV, of Runcorn, Grand Scribe ; Bro. J. Jones, of Runcorn, G. treasurer ; Bro. Rev. M. F.. Wynne, of Chester, G. chaplain ; Bro. Samuel Henderson, of Runcorn, G.D. ; Bro. J. Prophet, of Winsford, G.S. ; Auditors, Bros. r. Ravenscroft, of Weston, and A. T. Hibbert, of Chester; Committee of Management, Bros. Mansley, of Chester; A. Noden, of Winsford ; G. Leicester, of Northwich ; W. R. Hamilton, of Chester ; and T. White, of Runcorn. Messrs. J. Ainsworth, of Crewe ; W. Parish, J.P., of Helsby ; aud A. R. Norman, of Runcorn, were appointed ' arbitrators, and the trustees selected were Bros. J. Bow- ring, of Crewe '• T. Heesom, of Runcorn ; and Bro. Geo. ! Cooke, C.C., of Hanley ; Bro. Thos: Cooke, of Runcorn, was appointed Grand Patron of Cadets, and Bro. D. Bisbrown, of Runcorn, was elecged to represent Cheshire I, Grand Division at the National Division Conference, to ' be held at Newcastle in Whit-week. The cordial thanks ! of the division were given to Km T. Cooke for his 17 years service as Grand Scriber and great regret was ex- pressed that his daily avocation would no longer permit him to retain the office which he had occupied with so much credit to himself and advantage to the Order. The thanks of the division were also conveyed to Bro. E. Key, for his services as Gr.W.P.,during the past year. the repr2sentatiyes were entertained to a capital dinner prepared by Mr. Robert Shingler. In the eveoing, a public meeting was held under the presidency of the Rev. A. W. Riley, curate of Holy Trinity Church, and he was supperted by Messrs. A. T. Hibbert, J. A Gornall, W. R. Hamilton Watkin Mans- ley, and Wm. Formston, all of Chester,.Messrs. S. Tem- perley, D. Bisbrown, and T. Cooke; 01 Runcorn. The room was crowded. In opening the proceedings 'rho Chairman said from what he personally knew of the members of the Sons of Temperance, that they would do their best to make that meeting what it was announced to be—a pleaFaut evening. He trusted the result would be what they had prayed for, an increase in the good work of temperance, especially in connec- tion with this order. The extensive ramifications of the order were something to be proud of ; and, as they knew, when they grew into large numbers, strength was given to that cause or any other cause. The old saying that unity is strength, was perfectly true ; and he was sure the workers in the cause in Runcorn would be encouraged by the goodly number gathered at that meeting, and that this would mark the continuation of much successful work in connection with the Order in Runcorn. (Applause.) The speaker said he had never had cause to regret becoming a total abstainer, in fact he had become more confirmed in his opinion that it was the best course for a man to pursue to keep his mind and brain clear. (Hear, hear.) He explained that the Sons of Temperance had a twofold object, firstly to make men sober and better, and consequently happier, and secondly to teach habits of providence and thrift, while charity was also a prominent feature in the or- ganisation. Temperance was the primary principle, yet it was also sought to raise a spirit of independence amongst men, and the Order claimed to confer that blessing upon its members. (Applause.) The best way to secure independence was by having an indepen- dent will and mind, and exercising it instead of being a slave to any habit or any man. (Applause.) Mr. T. Cooke, after some introductory remarks of a humourous sheracter, gave all'an invitation to join the Sons of Temperance. He was anxious, he said, that they should all become teetotalers and take care of themselves in their younger days so that in their later years they would not fall into his hands, as relieving officer, by making an application, for the "House." (Laughter and applause.) He explained that the age of admission in the order was from sixteen to 45 years,and the entrance fee was 2s. 6d., and one of. the benefits secured was ss. to 15s. per week in sickness. according to the contribution. There was also medical attendance provided and funeral benefits, both for the member and his wife. The children were not overlooked, for there was a Cadet section in which he took an especial delight and they were anxious to make all the children in the town teetotalers. Both boys and girls were taken from 5 to 16 years of age, and the entrance fee was 6d. and a shilling, according to age. Twopence a week would provide 3s. per week in sickness, medical attend- ance and £3 at death. At 16'rears of age, the members were transferred free into the Sans of Temperance or the Daughters of Temperance. To encourage the children there was a summer treat, and prizes were offered by the local branch and by the National Division. He strongly urged those present, whether they joined the Order or not, to become total abstainers in their own interest. Be had had experience of' the blessings and enjoyment. that came of a sober existence and could therefore, strongly and sincerely recommend the signing of the pledee. (Applause.) The Rev. J. Simpson. in the course of an address, des- cribed the miseries brought about by intemperance,and the advantages that accrued from total abstinence, and from being connected with a society like the Sons of Temperance. During the evening a miscellaneous concert was given. A pianoforte duet by Miss A. M. and Mr. W. Cooke re- ceived a well-merited encore, and a similar compliment was peid to Mr. J. Leathwood for an effective rendering of" The Skipper ot St. Ives." The Misses I-leathering- ton received an ovation for their rendering of the duet "Over the Hawthorn Hedge," and the humourous song " I cannot sing," by Mr. G. Lacey, was received with much lauehter, and he was re-called. Miss Ainsworth gave "The Wishing Gate," in charming style, and a like remark applies to Miss Heatherington's singing. Mrs. Temporley gave due effect to the bright song "Twickenham Ferry." Mr. D. Smith was very successful in the song "Have courage to say no." Mr. J. Bisbrown caused much merriment by the humorous Lancashire recitation " Bowton's Yard." A most en3ovable ant pleasant evening was brought to a conclusion by the Doxology. Mr. lA'm. Cooke ably offi- ciated as accompanist. The audience conveyed their thanks to the various vocalists and instrumentalists by acclamation, on the motion of Mr.D. Bisbrown,seconded by Mr. S. M. Temperley.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodall, M.P., Financial Secretary to the War Office, addressing his c,onstituents at Hanley on 'thurs - day evening, stated that he had just completed arrange- ments affiicting some thousands of workmen in the clothing fac,ories, and he honed that on the Ist of February from 15,000 to 16,000 Government workmen would enjoy the advantages of a change which would he followed as an example by other great employers of labour.</p>
      </div>
