      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ALLEGED MURDEROUS ASSAULT NEAR T. .HELENS.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED MURDEROUS ASSAULT NEAR T. .HELENS.</p>
        <p>TWO MEN ARRESTED</p>
        <p>Two colliers named Arnold and Griffiths were arrested at Presco-, near Sr. Helens, on %londay, on the charge of committing a murderous outrage on Police-constable Simpson on Saturday night. The officer served one of the accused with a summons for drunkenness, when it is alleged they both at- tacked him and seriously injured his head. The deposi- tions of Simpson,who was stated to be in a dying condi- tion, have been taken before a magistrate. The injured constable is a married man, an has a family of four children. According to a statement he has made, about half-past five on S turday night he served a summons at the residence of the prisoner Griffith,. After he had served it he was returning ou his beat when he heard a noise of someone on the foot- path near to the house where Griffiths lived. He went back and met the two prisoners. He said to them, " What are you making such a noise as this about ?" Griffiths said, " We are only enjoying ourselves a bit." Both the prisoners and himself walked back along the footpath into the highway, and he left the two prisoners in front of Griffiths' house. He told Griffiths he had left a summons for him with his landlady, and he said. " What is that for ?" Witness replied, ." It is for being drunk that SaturJay night I brought you home." "If you have brought me a summons it will be a bad job for thee," said Griffiths. /loth prisoners then went into Griffiths house, and he had got about 150 yards from it when he heard someone come behind h-m. stopped and looked round, and saw the two prisoners following him. Proceeding, witness said : When they got up to me the prisoner Griffiths said, "What did you leave that summons there for ?" " I said, I told you what I left it there for, Peter. It was for being drunk that Saturday night I brought you home." The prisoner Arnold at this time did not speak, but walked past me. The prisoner Arnold got behind me and threw his arms round my waist and got both of my arms under his and held them fast. The prisoner Arnold said," Now, P., I have got, him fast. Into him, and let us down him." Griffiths struck me once or twice in the face with his fist. He then got hold of me, and he and the prisoner Arnold dragged me down. When they dragged me down, Arnold was on the top of me with his arms round me, holding my arms fast. The prisoner Griffiths struck me a great many times in the face with his fist, aid kicked me with his clogs about the face and head a great many times. The prisoner then ceased kicking, arid knelt down with his knee on my left shoulder, and said to the prisoner Arnold, "Hold fast Bob, I have not finished with him yet." The prisoner Arnold said," I have got him fast ; pitch into him, do for him ; he has been on my track a long time ;he is always watching for me." Prisoner Griffiths struck me in the face again a few times with his fist. He then got up off my s'houlder and said to Arnold " Hold fast, Bob, I will do for him yet." He _ _</p>
        <p>, then commericed to kick me again with his clogs on tke head and face. At that time 1 im.naged to get my right arm free from the prisoner's (Arnold's) grasp. I then put my hand up to my face to save mv eyes an 4 face from the kicks, and in doing so I received a good many kicks on my hand. The prisoner Griffiths then ceased kicking me, and he said to the prisoner Arnold, "Let him go Bob ;he will not go much further new." The prisoner Arnold, who had been holding me all the time, got off me, and they both went away to Griffiths' lodg- ings and left toe lying in the r jad. After they had gone I got up on my feet, but staggered and fell down again. After a time I got up again, and managed to get home. I have been in great pain ever since from the effects of those kicks, both in my face, arms, and hands. I have been confined to bed, and am attend ,d by Dr. Barlow, of Prescot. I have known both prisoners for some time, .</p>
        <p>_ The prisoners, who asked no questions, were remand- ed until Monday.</p>
        <p>- On inquiry af the residAnce of Constable Simpson, on Monday night, Mts. 6impson stated that her husband's condition was much improved, and that he was doing as well as could be expect-A. They had then no fear of a fatal result. The injuries consist chiefly of a large wound on the top of toe head, a serous wound on the forehead, and injuries to the right hand, several bones of which have been broken. The face and body are also badly bruised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SCAFFOLD ACCIDENT AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>THE SCAFFOLD ACCIDENT AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ALLEGATIONS AGAINST A FOREMAN.</p>
        <p>At the Griffin Inn, Peasley Cross, St. Helens, on Monday, Mr. Brighouse held an inquest touching the death of Richard Ellison, aged years,a joiner, who lodged at 47, Crab-st.. but who was killed on Friday last by the breaking of a scaffold at the Hardshaw Brook works of the United Alkali Com- pany. Mr.J. Massey appeared for Mr. James Lathom, timber merchant, who was carrying out the work on which the accident happened.—Mrs. Elizabeth Boardman, of Croft, near Newton-le-Willows, aunt of the deceased, gave evidence of identification.- -James Colquitt, who appeared Yfith his face ban- daged.said he lived at 48,Parr-st. He was a labourer for Mr. Lathom. Joseph Thisnall was foreman for Mr. Lathom. They were erecting a roof over some plant at the Hardshaw Brook works. They had just finished a ! similar joh at Kurtz's works. There were four portions in the roof, and they had got the planks and principals ready for the first portion when the accident happened. The building consisted of iron open columns. There were five sets of columns which wore about thirty feet high. They had to erect a scaffold across the pillars because they could not make a derrick on account of the casting on the ground. WitQess, the deceased, and John Livia sey were on the scaffold when one of the principals, a three-inch plank,nine inches wide, and twenty-four feet long, broke, and they all fell. The ends of twenty planks which formed the scaffold were e • ing on the one that gsve way. They grumbled to Chisnall, their fore- man, about the plank being out of " plumb." They told him that it was not fit ta go on, and that it wanted two props under instead of one. said it was only half an inch out of " plumb," and told them to go on working. They said it was two inches out. Cnisuall did not order them to strengthen it,but told witness later to put a spar over the bend. While he was putting the spur across, the plank broke. The accident occurred five minutes after they complained to Chisnall.— The Coroner: You are quite at liberty to open your mind, although your employer is here.— Witness, continuing, said Chisnall told them that by shifting the principal of the roof, which was :thou 3 and which was resting chiefly on the plank which broke, it would be all right. Witness was of opinion tl-at the accident would not have happened if there had been two props up. At Kurtz's they said ameng themselves that they did not think themselves quite safe on account the bend in the scaffold. They did not tien make any complaint to Chisnall. Ellison said to Chisnall before the accident, "Well, Joe, I am not a man for Lrumbling, but it's really not safe.' That was the first time Ellison had spoken. Ile was a very quiet fellow.—The Coroner asked if Chisnall was present, and he was brought into the room.—The Coroner, addressing him, said : I have called you in because of the evidence this man (Colquitt) is giving about you. When the law imposes a duty on a man,whether he is master or foreman, or anything else, and he wilfully neglects that duty, and death ensues in consequence, the man who is to carry out that duty is criminally responsible and should go for trial if it is .proved. The Coroner then read a summary of the evi- dence to Chisnall, and Colquitt said it was correct.— Sergeant Jackson, replying to the Cot oner, said Livsey was very bad,and it would be at least a fortniehttefore he would be able to attend.—The Coroner (to the jury) : In the face of this evidence you cannot possibly finish without Liysey. This man Chisnall should have the opportunity of being legally represented.—On the ad- vice of the Coroner, Mr. Massey, Mr. Chisnall, and the jury did not. ask any question from the witness Colquitt. —Richard Norris, 37,Parr-st.,said he was working on the • scaffold. He heard a conversa'ion between the three men and Chisnall. They c,anplained about the plank be ing bent, and said it was not safe. Chisnall said, "If it's not good enough put some more props under." They did not stop work to put any props up, and none were up.—Sergeant Jackson said the planks had not been dis- turbed since the accident, and the Gironer and the jury decided to inspect the plaee.—The inquest was ad- journed until Tuesday, the 30th inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>LORD CLANCARTY AND THE HOUSE. MAID.</head>
        <p>LORD CLANCARTY AND THE HOUSE. MAID.</p>
        <p>THE LORD COMES OFF SECOND BEST.</p>
        <p>" My word, Gregory, we will not carry coals," says one of the characters in " Romeo and Jul,lt." A girl who was employed as housemaid in a Lsndon mansion, seems to have been of the same mind as 6arnpson, for she firmly but politely declined to "carry coals," even ‘. hen requested to do so by so great a oersonage as Lord Clancarty (better known, perhaps, as Lord Dunlo). The, girl excused herself on the gr amd—and many will sym- pathise with her—that it was no part of her duty to c4rry coals whilst there was au able-bodied footman in ne house. " His Lordship " peremptorily dismissed her and she was compelled to leave the house at half past nne o'clock at night, a policeman being called in to assist in her removal. It appeared that the girl had b-en " taken over" with the house which " his Lord- ship "had hired, and on the case coming before Judge Stone at Brompton County Court on a claim for wages his Honour found for the plaint iff on all p)ints. He was decidedly of opinion that Lord Clancarty had no right to dismiss the servant, and he added tfiat m houses of such a class as that tenanted by the Earl and Countess of Clancarty the housemaids eught not to be called upon to carry coals. Furthermore, the Judge intimated that in his opinion the girl had treated the Ecrl of Clancarty "with the greatest respect</p>
        <p>RUPTURE CURED J. ,-,10.1XN, Hernia Specialist. 64, Chancery-line London, selids hi i hook on rupture post free. Branch Chambers, 26, King-at., Mancnester.—Advt.</p>
