      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>By Mr. WILLIAM ELLIS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <p>By MR. THOMAS SUTTON.</p>
        <p>THIS week we supplement the views Warrington and of Mr. F. MONKS and Councillor It. the Canal.</p>
        <p>T. FAIRCLOUGH with regsrd to the Ship Canal, as given in our last issue, by inter- views with Mr. John Crosfield and the various members of the Rivers Committee of the Town Council. It is the Rivers Committee which has more immediately in hand the town's interests, so far as they are affected by the Canal, and it is to. that Committee that the ratepayers naturally look for the initiation of any steps it may be deemed desirable for the Corporation to take. Mr. Cvosfield, is, of course, through his firm, very closely concerned with the navigation of the river which, it now seems probable, will be more or less prejudicially affected by the opening of the Canal. It will be noticed that the members of the Rivers Committee are without exception quite alive to the need of some arrangements being made which will enable local traders and manufacturers to avail themselves of the advantages of the Canal. It may, therefore, we suppose, be taken for grant- ed that the interests of the town will not be allowed to suffer through the indifference and apathy of the municipal authorities. Of course, the Ship Canal Company itself is the supreme arbiter as to whether any immediate and effective action be taken. But we do not • anticipate any reluctance in that quarter to comply with reason- able requests. There is no suggestion that the Canal Company should be asked to carry out large and unprofitable schemes so far as this locality is concerned. All that will be asked of them will be that they should give facilities for the gradual building up of an import and export trade, a trade which will benefit the Company just in pro- portion as it benefits Warrington.</p>
        <p>Two or three European countries National Discontents. . seem to be in a truly " parlmas " condition just now. There are disturbances in various parts of Italy which may easily develope into a revolution. Beginning in Sicily, the disturbances have spread to the main- land, and it may need all the promptness and resolution of SIGNOR CRISPI, who is once again at the head of Italian affairs, to stem the tide of dis- content. What Italy wants just LOW is a great reforming statesman, with the will and the power to resist the sinister influences of vested interests where those interests are contrary to the public good. There can be no doubt that it is sheer misery which is goading the Sicilian and Italian peasant and labourer into revolt. It is to be hoped that those who now hold sway in the land of Dante and Mazzini will have the sagacity to see that reform and not forcer is the proper remedy for the present evils of the body politic. Otherwise, there may at no 'distant period be one of those fratricidal social revolts, which bring such deplorable consequences in their, train. Then, again, it is said that in Russia—that dark and onainotta blot on the political map of Enzope—social discontent was never so prevalent and widespread as it is now. And here, too, as almost almost always the ea,se, discontent springs from the impoverishment and oppression of the people.. One writer tells us that "systematic oppression of a diabolical kind has driven the people back upon the laws. of nature, which take precedence of all °then:laws." What the result may be it is hard to say, for after all Russia is in some respects a little,known country, but anything in the nature f a revolution there would be a dire menace to. Europe.. It could hardly take place without pre- cipitating that conflict of the nations for which the great powers of the- Continent are ceaselessly preparing..</p>
        <p>A RUNISVAT HORSE A horse attached to a cart containing pigs,belorrg- ing to Mr. Shaw, butcher,. Bridge-street, took fright in the Central Station goods yard on Sunday even- ing, and dashed down the slopo into Winwick-St. Here it narrowly escaped running into the Salva- tion Array,. which was, pw.sing at the time. The horse was quickly brought to a standstill, not, however, betore it had knoc-ked down a lamp-post</p>
        <p>at the entrance to the station. Tanna Storm, towels. sheets, and quilte, and all hon!,-6. linens, hemmed and m,rked ett-h of charge during sale.—J.. and W: Inerroa's, Sankey•sst., Warrington. dvt. METHODIST FREE CHURCH, BUCKLEY-STREET. The members and friends of the above church, turned up in large numbers on Saturday to a wel- come teaegiven in honour of their new pastor (Rev. W. L—McNair), of London. A recognition meeting was afterwards held in the, chapel, presided over by Mr. T. Morris, F.G.S., and it was largely attended. Mr. Simpson introduced to the meeting the new, ministereand in a few well &amp;room words welcomed on behalf of the church, expressing the hope that,. ae-he had received a unanimous call, that which eley were lookinaeforward to would be fully realised.. Their new pastor's preaching abilities were sath that he could say Mr. NlcNair was second to none in Warrington. Mr. Morris also spoke words- of welcome and enconragement to Mi. McNaitte and trusted that the Church would so improate That his stay would he long and useasl. Letterseof a?ology for noneuttendance were read by the secretary, Mr. Alfred Scott, from the Revs.- J. ISOnge,. Kirkland,. and Drummond, all expreasing welcome to the pastor that had been chosen, and .also hsom Messrs. Monks, Long, Tharme and oehers, :Mr. Monks accompanying his good wishes wi a cheque. Mrs. MoSiair sang a solo. "A sure retreat," and' a; recitation was given by Mrs. Wooley, The 'road to Heaven." Miss Green, of the Railway, Mis- eionegave a 'short address and extended the hand of welcome to the new pastor and his wife irk the int7eaests of the town of Warrington. Mres'eott Isangt a solo, "'Come," and Mr. McNair ,ariefly iadristessed the meeting, returning thanks ;tar the 'welcome that had been accorded him on haltalf of :ltica wife and himself, and saying that one thing isms one only was his desire—that he should spend :riardi be spent in the service of his Masterswho had itdioe so mush for him. Mr. 113qte sang, a solo, "Safety," and the proceedings were closed by the Pastor proncancing the benediction. CARPETS made and laid Free of char,e, Linolennes measure and fitted free of charge daring ,sale.—J. and W. Durrozes Sank ey-st., Wallington. SCKTO MIATH PENNY Tho meaabers of the stockton Heath Church Penny Bauk were provided with an, excellent tea and entert,uinMent in the schoolroom- on Monday eveningey Mrs. Wright, of Underclile. The fol- lowing ladies assisted Mrs. Wright iii serving up the repeat. :—Mrs. Vere Davies, Mrs, Vernon, Miss. Tofts, tie Misses Wright, and othera. After tea a short hat bright entertainment was given. Master Logan and the -Misses Wright sang several pretay songs, which were received with roands of applanee. ' by the children and a number of their parents who had been invited to the entertainnsent. Al terwards Master Logan, who had kindly brought his lantern to the school, showed a number of views, 10Ca:# and otheywise, and concluded with Fltme comic :dining slides, which were much enjoyad by the little folks. The Rev. H. it C. Vernon said .aow pleased No was to be with them, and to seett hem all so happy. He concluded by proposing a voe of thanks to Mrs, Wright, for her kindness in providing tbem with , the tea and evening's entertainment. Tl:a propeA_ tion was carried with rounds of appliance. llr. Hallows said the bank had grown very aauch laty the numbers on tlioe boeks for the hat year lasing 304. This iticrease was asainly due to the kindly interest, taken in the bank by Mrs. Wright and Miss Tinsley. lie regretted Miss Tinsley's.tabsenoe that evening, owing to a cold, but though absent she had not forgotton them, as he had seen a hamper of oranges put, in one corner ofthe school which she had provided for them. (Applause..) He c.enclinleel by asking them all to return t heir beat thanks to , Mrs. wi.jaat, miss Tinsley, and others. This having been givea in a very hearty maener a pleasant even- ing wae brought to a close. Each member, as he or I she went, home, was presented with an orange by I Mrs. V. Davies, on behalf of Miss Tinley, itna a I mince pie by the Misses WORM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>EXAMINER</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. JANUARY 20. 1894.</p>
        <p>THE coal trade has been rather The Coal Trade. prominently before the public during the present week. The Conciliation Board which was formed at the</p>
        <p>memorable conference under the presidency of Lord ROSEBMRY, and which is composed of equal numbers of miners and employers,met on Monday and discussed sundry matters affecting the trade. There have been rumours of a renewal of the dis- astrous conflict of a few months ago 7 but, fortu- nately, these may bo dismissed as• having no foundation. Of course, under the tercsathat were arrived at through the Rosebery confmnee, the old dispute might be re-opened on th 3 first .of February, but then is no prospect of such a re- opening. The very fact that the Concihtion Board, after discussaig the proposed new rules, adjourned till the 13th of February indicates that there will be no immediate recurrence of the dis-</p>
        <p>pute. Another coal misis could only be Brought about in two ways—either by a demand on the</p>
        <p>- part of the miners for an increase of wageo..,,or by the coal-owners ina.3ting upon a redustion. There is no likelihood of either of these courses</p>
        <p>being taken, at all events, for some time to come. The miners, we.may readily believe,,have Ino desire to ienew a ccnflict which inflicts upon</p>
        <p>Ibern so much suffering and loss,especially athey would have little public support behind them in a Ilemand for higher pay. The coal-owners, onithe other hand, cannot finds any decent pretext, for aafling upon their workmen for a reduction when Oaf is selling at its present price. Everything :that has happened since the termination of the great lock-out goes to prove the contention that w*.s made on behalf of the miners that the .real</p>
        <p>ievanee of the colliery owners was not against miners' wages but against the ruinous ". cutting." policy that had prevailed; in their .own ranks.. and English coal too,is such an entirebt in-</p>
        <p>dispensable and irreplaceable commodity in the modern life of England, that there is absolyztely no need for those who have it to dispose of to sell</p>
        <p>it at a price which will not yield a living wage to the mine nor a living profit to the capitalist... So far as the experiences 0: the last two or three months go, it would appear that the one section of the community which Ilea- a right to complain is composed of the un- fbrtemate. house holders, especially in the larger towns, who have had to Ivay abnormal pricesfon 'their fuel. Thee prices have, in many instances,. • ranged Ler above the figeire which, it is under- /stood, gives an ample return both for wages-and capital. The fashion is, of course, to melee the un:ucky " middleman" the scapegoat for imposii- ,tions of this kind, but we are probably not far wrong in assuming that a large part of these, extra prices has found its way into the coffersoef the colliery companies,. In many instancesein- deed, these companies, are their own "iniddle- men," and, as far as the others are .concerned— well„ they know the prices at which coal is-being retailed. • • eetteee—e- The miners have been "prOneinent during the week in another way,, or on Friday the delegates. • of the Miners' Federation of Great Britain. met in conference at Leicester. The delegates were cordially welcomed by the raunicips, authorities of • the enlightened and go-a Lead midland -; • town: A recepti6n of this kind is, it .; seems, something new in the history of the Federation, and it iv •-therefore, a suggestive indiceti•on of the high,posiUoie. which the representativies of organisei labour now held in the community. In his presidential address, Mr. Pickard, LP., naturally, alluded at some length to the aspute of last year, andi his enumer ation of the divid ends earned by seine of the companies wftich took part id the lock-out affords interesting reading. These., divido.eds range from about thirty per cent. dowieweeds to a not very low figure. It would Seem that, in regard to numerical strength, tie Federation has not been weakened by the trials it underwent; indeed there is every prospect that it i about to extend the area of its influence, fora large body of Scotch miners llas applied for admission and will be accepted upon specified conditions. This exten- sion of the Federation will, we believe, be an advantage not only to the miners, but to the coun- try generally. It. will not make the Federation powerful enough to enforce unreasonable con- ditions as against the coal-owners or the public, but it will certainly help to deter the employers from making any such unjustifiable demands as that which brought about the stoppage of industfy last Year.</p>
