      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>WYCLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>AIiNZAL TEA MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual congregational tea meeting in connec— tion with the above-named church was held on</p>
        <p>Thursday evening in Wycliffe Hall. About 300 persons sat down to tea. The Indies who presided at the tables were the following: Mesdames</p>
        <p>Yonge, Armitage, Garland, Wyllie, T. Wallington, P.T. Winskill,.Barker, H. Rathlmi?,. Fleming, Scott, Poole, Atkinson, S. W. Jeffery, Harbourne, F. Birch and Yeadon, Misses Gray, Knowles, and Paul. After tea an adjournment was mace to tne church, when the organist, Mr. W. 11. Payton, played several selections on the organ,whilst the hall was arranged for the meeting. In a classroom, adjoining the hall both useful and fancy articles were offered for sale.</p>
        <p>Mr. Z.. Armitage J.P., presided over the after proceed- ings,and he was supported on the platform by the pastor. the Rev. J.. Yonge, and the following deacons-:- Messrs. G. Wallington, T. Wallington, J. Podmore, R. Wyllie, T. Hawthorn, and P. T. Winskill. In the course of a few introductory remarks, Mr. Armitage said he was very much pureed to receive from Mr. Yonge a few days ago an invitation or command to hold himself ready for that position. He began to consider what Mr. Yongers mo- tives might be, and he came to the conclusion that he had chosen, him because he didn't want one whc,would</p>
        <p>occupy much time. His usual custom on such occasions when occupyine the position of chairman was to make a few introductory remarks. Although we had - got to- - the third week in January, he honed it was not too late.' to wish all connected with Wycliffe a Happy New. war. It was a cause for anxiety and sorrow for those con— nected with Wycliffe Church, to know that the num- ber in church fellowship had slightly diminished during the last two years. Whatever the cause might be it re- mained with them at Wycliffe to make the Place of.use to all who were round about them. (Applause.) While regretting the slowness of some to take up workin con- nection with the place, they must not forget the large number there were actively engaged in work of one kind.. or another, especially in the younger members having an attachment to the place,which sp• ke well for the future.. Mr. Armitage then referred to the immense change which had taken place in the membership list during the 26 or 27 years he had been connected with the church*. owing to removals and deaths. They must look to some ' quarter for recruiting, and filling up the ranks. It did. , not do to depend upon new comers. They couldn't-ex- pect that people who had not been with them very long would take as deep an interest in the church as-those who had been in the congregation all their lives.. The - greatest hope they had was in the younger members-of the families of people who had been con fleeted with, them for a number of years. The speaker afterwards- referred to financial matters in connection with ,the. church, regretting that the total amount raised during 1893 had lallen below the "four figures." Their income for many years past had exceeded £l,OOO, but last year the total amount waa slightly under that amount. The choir opened the prograsnme with the glee " The. Woods," which was well rendered under the conductor— ship of Mr. G. Wall.isigton. The soloists were Mrs. S.• W. Jeffery, Mrs. G., Jeffery, Miss Westgarth, Mr. W. H. Wallington, Mr. G. 11. Yonge, and Mr. A. Griffiths, and 7 all sang in such good style as to receive hearty receptions., The duet, " The Larboard Watch," by Messrs. W: H. Wallington and A. Griffiths, was a decided success, as was also the trio" 'Twas 3 ou "in which the same vocal— ists were assisted by Mr. Hibbert. A most interesting. feature of the entertainment were the readings by Ms. A. Cornvy, who rt-ad Tennyson's " To-morrow," and, Mr. H. M. Ashton, who showed considerahle aptitude in' this direction. The audience showed their appreciation by honouring him with two encores. Mr. R. H. Pear— son contributed a violin solo with all bib well-known taste and skill. The duties of accompanist were carried' out by Mr. W. H. Payton. On the proposition. of Mr. J. Podmore. seconded by Mr. R. Wyllie, a cordial vote of thanks was accorded the' 'Chairman, those who had provided the entertainment,' and the ladies who had assisted at the tea. The Rev. John Yonge in supporting the proposition, referring to • ithe chairman, said they hadn't a better friend, or more :faithful servant, in their whole brotherhood, than Me• 'Armitage, and he was very glad he had been able to take' , the position of chairman that night.—Mr. Armitage.re-. plied, and the proceedings terminated with the singing. Of the Doxolcgy.</p>
        <p>• THE REPORTS FOR 1893. From the Wycliffe manual which has been published, this week. containing the reports ana balance sheets or the varied institutions in connection with the Church,Wo have extracted the following: THE PLEASANT SUNDAY AFTERNOONS FOR MEN. "Since the inktug.uration of this society en Sunda'', March 5th,1893, its career has been one of tllurnPl2•" Looking back over the months gone iey, it must be ae- knowledged that this -work has been one of the graadeat successes which, as a church and people, we have ever . achieved. The success of the movement was guarantee from the very outset, by the ready manner in which all the help required in so many varied ways was give°. And to the same spirit o 5 fiaelity to the various -detail duties, bestowed by al the workers, its present flourishing position is attributable. If the same spirit continua to actuate all who now share in the labour, the P.S.A. will be carried on to unknown heights of power and use-• fulness. 1 he attendances. at the meeting have been realli wonderful, especially considering the exceptionally trf, ' ling season the movement has had to encounter. The summer months of 15aa, were indeed great testing thnee• The Sundays might have been supposed to he far Mere' fascinating for outdoor enjoyments, than for the inside bf a church .where the was oft-times most unbes-t:. able. Yet, during those very trying and tempting dal, the attendance never lagged, but rather steadily in' creased. There have been 44 meetings held since thee: livening day up to,December 315 t, with an averagli; ' attendance of about 600 men ; the aversr, :latterly being of course, even larger still. TW„, total number of subscribing members enrolled up date is 1,108 with a. weekly average attendance of 49,4', This average is estitsated upon the whole period, t_n weekly attendance being now much larger. The benev; lent fund has t ePn the means of rendering very valusbie assistance in cases of want and distress.„ During ,tts unfortunate stoppage of works, owing to the coal as pute. the Commitsse of this fund was able to rerio`l timely help to upwards of 50 families. The meetin,lS have been presided 0,7 e/ each week by a different chaPty man, for, although the Pastor is President, having bee— Dv• elected to that ofThee by the members themselves,. pie deemed it desirable to enlist as much variety as possy in every direction.. Gentlemen in different seers to parties have presided, both Churchmen and Liberals and Conservatives. Vie welcsone to our platform who will help on our sup: namely, to benefit the men. All the me maintained the spirit of the motto "Brief, II therly." Many testimonies have been -given accomplished in various was, and we II encouragement to go forward in our 'wink. go on as we have begun, the Wycliffe P.S.A mighty instriamen, for good in tnarrington." TEE DAY SCHOOLS:4 The managers of the day seheols report t year ending September 301), 1893, -xas a vet one to all concerned in the management of schools in Warrington. The attemdaece reduced in the first quarter, by resseon of the I and in the last quarter, owing to. the collie closed anti the consequent stoppage of tract number of children were unable to pay the despite alishis, on the whole, the year has' fut. In the, anerage attendance there was a Upper 5ch001,,388 ; infants', 14Z-2;; total, 530. aeers, however. would like to sea an averale 1894. This is by no means impossible, if gence is paid to school attendance, by all cot M. Inspector, E. L. Phelps, Ease., visited ft October 23rd and 24th, and after a very sexist tion maale tire followlng repast :—Mixed children are orderly anti attentive, and • work reaches a high stand:, ad of accuracy gence. The second and third-standards are. wail taught, but the fourth a-Samdard shows ' clictationsand reading in this and in the, is fairly intelligent. The c.seaposition oistht seventh, stand: rds and arithmetic gene,ria much praise, and the clasasubjects and sinr 'have