        <p>SATURDAY NIGHT CONCERT AT WIDNES. Th',e.7.,-.)tid weekly concert under the auspices of the United Alkali Company, was held on Saturday evening at the Ba: ha Assemhle- Rooms, Widnas. There was a crowded audience. The entertainments are proving yen' Popular. The following arti,tes took 'part :—Messrs. J. Morrison, J. Fox, C. Finn. R. Dutton, and J. Platt. • GO to PRITCFIA.nD's, 51. High-st., Runcorn. for the best FIVE GUI,EA. Wawa a working man can have. —Advt. A DISHONEST DOMESTIC SERVANT AT WIDNES. At Widnes Petty Sesions, on Thursday, before the Mayor (Alderman Gossage), and Mr. Stewart H. Brawn, Catherine Curley, 29, Canal-St., Runcorn, was charged OR remand with stealing a curintity of wearing apparel, value £2 103. belonging to Edward M'Conville, draper, 57, Lugsdale-road. The prisoner who was a domestic servant. at the prosecutor's shop, pleaded guilty. She was sent to gaol for seven days. SPLENDID NEW CURRANTS 2d. per lb, at COLLIER'S.—Advt. BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS, RUNCORN. The first meeting of the winter session after tie Christmas vacation was held on Wednesday evening, under the presidency of Mr. T. Simistor. Tfritore was a good attendance. •A paper was read by Mr. A. J. Wardle on the position of woman. A discussion of a very animated character ensued, in which Messrs. Palin and milling,ton stoutly upheld the progressive view of the question, whilst Messrs. Jeffrey and A. D. Jones took the apposite side. If you want your WATCH RAPAIRED by skilled workmen take it to PRITCHARD'S.— Advt.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA CHURCH LITERARY AND SOCIAL CLASS, RUNCORN. A meeting in connection with the above class was held On Tuesday evening in the Lecture Hall, under the pre- sudency of the Rev. J. Simpson. There was a good attendance. According to the syllabus it was an" Open night," and consequently members sent in papeas to the Chairman for his criticism. The subject was " My fav- ourite book, and why I like it." Some of the papers Were excellantly written ,and indicated a high moral tone With regard to the selection of the writers. NASITLET'S DAMSONS 21b. for 6d. at COLLIER'S.—Adtt.</p>
        <p>SODDEN DEATH AT A WIDNES WORKS. On Monday afternoon, a joiner, named Francis Old- graves, Ditton, died very suddenly at the Liverpool ' Silver and Copper Company's works. It appears Old- i graves went to his employment as usual, about 6 a in., and partook of lii; dinner at twelve o'clock, after which he Went to the time office to see the clerk. A few minutes afterwards he was found lying in an uncoNscious condi- tion on the railway siding at the works He died whilst heing conveyed home. Decuirsed had been under the care of Dr. Hutchinson for some time, who expressed the opinion that death was due to heart di-ease. • JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- Runcorn.—Advt. LECTIIRE ON DOMES AND DOME BUILDERS AT RUNCORN. On Friday evening last week Mr. J. Wilding, assistant surveyor to Runcorn Board of Commissioners, delivered his lecture on "Domes arid dome builders" to a good audience in Greenway-road Board School. The chair was occupied by Mr. F. Wisvvall, C.E., who in introduc- ing the lecturer mentioned the series of very interesting and instructive lectures that had already taken place in the town, and pointed out the value they had in the ad- vancement of general knowledge. He spoke upon the importance of the great national movement that was taking place, a share in which he was pleased to say they in Runcorn were taking no inconsirtiorable part. The lecture proved highly interesting and Mr. Wilding was eordially When in want of a CHASTE and ELEGANT WHDDING or BIRT !ID AY PRESENT go to PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-street.—</p>
        <p>INTERCHANGE OF. PI7LPITS AT RUNCORN. On Sunday evening the annual i ,terchange of pulpits at the Nonconformist Churches in Runcorn took place. The preaches were :—At St. Paul's Wesleyan, the Rev. W. Albert, Primitive Methodist ; at Halton-road Wesleyan, the Rev. J. Simpson, C ,tigregationalist : at Bethesda Congregational Chapel, the Rev. J. P. Williams Presbyterian ; at Greenway-road Primitive Methodist Chapel, the Rev. T. Casely, United Methodist ; at St. John'srPresbyterian Chapel, the Rev. J. Hanby,Wesleyan and at Ellesmere-street Malted Methodist Chapel, the Rev. T. IViikes, Wesleyan. The services were all well attended. A the close a large number of members con- Erected with the various churches assembled in the Con- greg,atioual Church, where a united sacramental service was held. It was conducted by the Rev. J. Simpson, assisted by the Revs. T. Willies, T. Casely, J. Hanby, and J. P. Williams. Mr. Simpson delivered a short address.</p>
        <p>k'itrrcutßn's have now stood the test for fifty yetrs. and qtlxlity and first class workmanship are still guaranteed.— Atfrt.</p>
        <p>Ron KELLY RETURNs TO WIDNES. ' On Itionday,at Widnes Police Court, before Councillor NV. W. Gossage,the notorious Rose Kelly made her 107th aPPearance, on a charge of being drunk and disorderly. Ltortnight ago she was discharged and her fare paid to wlgan on her promising to remain there. P.C. Evans stated that defendant was drunk on Saturday night,and behaving in a disorelerly manner, and stopping people in the street. Mrs. Kelly pleaded guilty to having hi.en djunk, but not disorderly.—Mr. Lewin (deputy clerk) -"Ow do you account for your presence here when you . Promised Mr. Deacon to go away ?—Defendant : I have °tbing* to say this time, Mr. Lewin, because I broke VY Promise last time I was here.—Superintendent ,„eignley : This is her 107th appearance,—Defendant : -1.,' you keep on taking me every time it will soon make I shall petition the Rome Secretary for a pension tor the work I have done for them in prison. (Laughter.) 7,41 r. Gage: You will be fined 10s. and costs, or 14 52;15 in uefa.ult. If you come here again you will be sent to th;: sessions.—Mr. Lewin : Yes, and you will get twelve months as a rogue and a vagabond.---Defend : I mi.dit, as well steal something, and they will 417 give ine twelve months. etu-lonnt's, teL 1840. flake! Collier's are always baking.— Ad. OLD MEN'S TREAT AT HALTON. Ai:the annual treat to the male veterans of Halton and A r3alt ri e t was given on Wednesday Evening, at the Norton Hotel, a-goodly company assembled to partake of er:tist substantial dinner, well served by Mr. and Mrs. Iv_ . Tie chair was occupied by the Rev. G. D. th, and the vice-chair by Mr. T. Wright. Among 11;:e.nPresent were the Rev. J. Hanby, Mr. Stokes, and bbn...'"ve• The loyal and patriotic toa,ts having been _ ,!L..tfe.d that of in,Zn_red at the call of the Chairman. Mr. Wright sub- " The bishop, clergy, arid ministers of Z.1,1_1. nominations." The Chairman acknowledged the a7w4atv . During the evening Miss Wright sang" Far great 'an one or two other selections which were e 5' appreeiated, they being interpreted in a most effct- . lye Way• Mr.. Patron contributed a humorous • which caused considerable merriment. • The old P veo. , ia _ - vere mmensely interested with this showing of . lan"tuenrlber of views by the Rev. J. Ilinby's oxyhydrogen Preyn, and much enjoyed the musical entertainment ' ' liberlaled, Tile old people were entertained in a most gz,. itfashion, they being supplied with fruit, tobacco, in their w's they that no deaths had taken place forced toranks during the year, hut that many were en- nui an .nn Absent through sickness. This was the weetersary. li. \Ni For , niv - to, home made bread,try conwrai's.—Adot. At Widne_NACrimo IN WIDNES DISTRICT. Petty Xt (Allpl Sessions, ayor on Thursday, before the Peter 1(0w5,•,-:11:ail Gossage) and Mr. Stewart 11. Brown. on remand wiirat old man, of WarriT,ton, was charged 16th No„,,mber' raching on the Bold-hall estate on the The defendant a nadf't. Mr. j. .W. Knowles prosecuted. seven gamekeeper,' a "mher of others were found by , cation took p!,,,,,, t. P•aching on the estate, and ,an alter- keepers named Pri:ween the prisoner and one of the in the struggle fell 0- Prisoner kid the worst of it, and been in hospital sincveer a net and broke his leg. He had the bench, seven titoe-sl;risoner had been 24 thnes before months•for that class of °r Poaching and had served three for a mowil without tl °len". James Boult, He was sent to gaol Throne- ~. ~,,''' °Winn of a fine.—Goorge Hunt, Ditton, were sumtnl.- `ldulligan. and Wm. Clarke, all of licerpe. Mr. J. 0. 4)119_ for killing- game without a Earl of Sefton, a p '.Witt. St. Helens, on behalf of the and Mr. J. vv. kPri7.Zl in support of the int,rination, Hunt and Mulli:.an the case avin t Pleadpcmr d Adefruided Bonk and Clarke. earilty, and Mr. Swift said 'End Clarke was very weak, and he Would offer riaoece(7ri,d.encia a..:anst them, and the sum- monses were previowth. 1,,,en eon,vu.nt: y dismissed. Hunt, who had 10S. A ilei!, f: id of a similar offence, was fined nd e''sts, and ligan. 5,. and costs.—William TraviA, lialopaiik land beloneire, , ,was summoned for trespassing on .. to Earl Sefton Mr. J. 0. Swift (St. Helens) appe., r 1 ' ' * under-ke, •I. e, in support of the information. An nd t t pt,r named Geonze Haywood saw the defendant two others on the 17th November li ''. ' tiri ing afence pparentiv for the pur y,se of‘taking'rabbits. , .Defendant ave a false Darn. MAI 2' Dsl 0 ft, 1,4 IPS'. He had twice previ- y b..,n convicted of a similar -2. s , irrh.:di offence, and was fined • .ng costs, or n month's imnrisonment.</p>
        <p>WIDNES COUNTY COURT. A sitting was held yeste ua3 by Judge Shand,but there were no cases of public in erest. SHEEP WORRYING AT NORTON. Two sheep have beet w-frieu ,Jo toe .Niarsh a,t liprton this week, and the police are inveititing the affair. The animals belonged to Mr. Finch, far,ner, Astmore. COLLIER'S use over ten tons of Flow* every week.—Aairt.</p>
        <p>WIDNES CYCLE CLUB. The annual meeting was held on Wenesday under the presidency of Mr. T. FL Thomas. The reports showed that the year's doings had been triost successful. Officers for the ensuing year were apro,inted. RUNCORN MASONIC LODGE. The quarterly meeting was held on Thursday evening when there was a good attendance of members. Bro. Burgess W.M. conducted the proceedings, and at the conclusion, those present sat down to supper. JOSEPH PRITCHARD, 51, High-street, Runcorn, keeps SPECTACLES to suit all sights, from a shilling a pair.—Adat. WIDNES CHURCH CHORAL :7;OCIETY. The members of this Society gave the oratorio "Christ and His Soldiers " to a good audience, in the Parochial Rooms, on Thursday night. The pincipals were Mrs. A. H. Rhodes, Mrs. Fred Owens, and Mr. Thomas Barlow.</p>
        <p>Mn. HENRY LEA OF FARNWORTH. The dead body of this gentleman, who nad been mis- sing since the 18th December, was taken from the St. Helens Canal, near Pilkingt:m's works, on Tuesday by a flAtman named George Beckett of Runcorn. The inquest was held yesterday by Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, and as there was no evidence to show how he got into the water, an open verdict was returned. He was over 40 years of age and had been employed as shipping clerk at Messrs. Gaskell and Deacon's Works for 27 years.</p>
        <p>EVERYTHING is 801 d for smallest Profit at CoLLlEß's,92,Crreen- way-rd.,Halton-rd.,Bgerton-st., Bridge st.6,Courch-st.—Adet.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN :SCHOOL BOARD CONTINUATION CLASSES. As a means of illustrating the geography letssons,form- ing part of the syllabus at these classes, the lantern was again called into requisition on Tuesday evening. The many beautiful and interesting spots in the neigh- bourhood of Glasgow and Clyde were brought before the notice of the pupils by a large number of first-class pic- tures. Afterwards some of the principal places of in- terest in London were shown. Mr. W. Epps gave a graphic description of th.! various places, and the lan- tern was manipulated by Mr. T. It, wyer. The proceed.- ings were much enj, yed by the students, and the efforts of the teachers in making the classes as attractive and profitable as possible are much appreciated. The enter- tainment was repeated to the scliolars•of the day school on Welnesdaw. Mr. J. F. M ttltoti, the headmaster,act- ed the part of guide. PURE IRISH BUTTER. Is. per lb., at COLLIER'S. —Advt. PRESHNTATION TO TICE REV. A. W RILEY, RUNCORN. On Tuesday eveniug,a meeting w,A,s held in the Infants schoolroom of Holy Trinity Church, in connection with . the Men's Bible Guild, at which a presentation was made . to the Rev. A. W. Riley, who, after fulfilling the position of curate for two years, is taking his departure for Bristol. There was a large attendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. H. A. flankinson. Mr. Kilcross, in presenting a purse of gold to Mr. Riley spoke in very high terms of the valuable services he had rendered during his stay among them. His sermons had been very highly appreciated. The presentation was a free will offeling of the people in acknowledgment of the numerous kindnesses they had received at his hands. Speeches were also made by Messrs. Henderson, Owen, Smith, Cunningham and the chairman. Mr. Riley, in acknowledging the gift said in every depart,nent of the church he had done his best to leave it in such a way that his successor could carry on the work. He was going to a larger field of labour. DELICIOUS CRISP CRUST round COLLIER'S bread.—Adat.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD RUNCORNIAN. Another old inhabitant of the town passed away, on Wednesday, in theperson of Mrs. Mary Peacock, widow of the late Mr. John Peacock. of Regent-street. Runcorn, at the age of 78 years. The sad event took place at the residence of her son-in-law, Mr. Henry Briscce, 16, Church-street. The deceased had resided in the town for over fifty years, and she assisted her husband for a long period in farming on land in Higher Rune, rn, which has been utilized for building purposes. She had a very retentive memory, and was able to discourse at length upon Runcorn hal a century ago, and the contrast she made between the state of the town at that time and the present was most interesting. The place was considered when she first knew it, as more of a watering place than anything else. She was greatly respected. She was born at Fernie Lea Farm, Beeston, and lived with her father at Barrow Hall, near Chester. She was a member of a very old Cheshire family. Her remains will be interred in Runcorn Cemetery on Sunday. Over 6,000 delicious loaves sold every week at COLLIER'S</p>
        <p>MORE COAL THIEVES AT WIDNES. At Widnes on Thursday, before the Mayor (Alderman Gossage) and Mr. Steward H. Brown, Catherine Regan, 7, Water-street, aged 10 years, and William Hayes, 14; Water-street, 15 years, were charged with stealinc, 451bs of coal belonging to the United Alkali Company. Regan was discharged, and Hayes, who had twice been before the court for cal stealing, was fined lOs ,or seven days' imprisoument.—Ann 3,-trie Woods, 20, Water-street, was charged with stealing 351bs. of coal belonging to the L. and N.-W. Railway Company. She had previously been convicted several times for similar offences. and Inspec- tor Keighley said she was continually stealing coal, and Rave the police a lot of trouble. She was sent to gaol br a month.—Margaret Dawson, Can al-hank, pleaded guilty to stealing a quantity of coal belonging to the L. and N.-W. Railway Company. She had been many times before the court for drunkenness, assaulting the the police, &amp;It., and had served nine months imprison- ment for unlawfully wounding. She was sent to gaol for 14 divs.</p>
        <p>COLLIF.X'S OVEN, are going n!ght and Advt. FUNERAL OP MISS JANE CROSFIELD. The funeral of the late Miss Jane CroNfield, of Frodsham, took place at Stoekton Heath Church- yard, on Saturday, the remains being interred in the family grave. The Rev. H. B. Blogg, vicar of Frodsham, officiated. The mourners included Mr. .f. Crosfield and Miss Crostield, Mr. and Mrs. Charles Reynolds, Mr. Alfred Thomas, the Misses Crosfield, of Fulwr,od Park, Aigburth, Mr. Sydney Crosfield, Mr. Ernest Crosfield, Mr. J. J. Croslield, and Mr. Arthur H. Crosll7.ld, Mr. Blaine and Mr. Robert Davies. There was a large number of fl )ral tributes. Mr. Nicholson (Messrs. Garnett and Sons) superin- tended the funeral arrangements.</p>
        <p>LAM-Pg.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger. Warrington and Earlestown.</p>
        <p>PADGATE CHURCII SCHOOLS TEA PARTY. On Friday evening last. week the annual tea party was given to the children attending Padgate Church Sunday school. After tea an entertainment in which the children took the principal part was given, a large number of parents and friends of the children being present. The Rev. W. R. Hart- wright, Th. (curate), presided. The entertain, rnent consisted of songs, recitations, dialogues, .9tc. A number of dumb-bell exercises were gone through Mr. F. Black well, the schoolmaster, deserves special mention for his untiring energies in training the children. At the close of the performances the prizes earned by the children for attending the Sunday school during the past year were distri- buted to the sticce,sful ,cholars by Mrs. Lanham. The prizes, which took the form of books, numbered upwards of 100 volumes. AVERT'S SCALES.—Agent GARLAND, Bridge-street, War-</p>
        <p>rington WARRINGTON CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY LIMITED. The quarterly meeting of this Society was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-st., on Monday even- ing. There was a fair attendance. Mr. T. Birchall, J.P., president of the Society, occupied the chair, and he was suaported on the platform by Mr. W. Jeffery, the vice-president. The 132nd quarterly report and statement was submitted. From it, it appears that the total sales for the quarter were £21,246 15s. 11d. Amongst the items of expendi- ture was the sum of £54 which was given in goods during the recent distreas, to the Warrington Dis- tress Fund. The usual dividend of 3s. 2d. in the X was declared. Mr. T. Birchall was re-elected presi- dent. The following Committee men were also re- elected : Messrs. Robert Cook, Win. Jones, Samuel Dunbobbin, and Thomas Critchley. THF. ICE FATALITY AT WARRINGTON.—INQUEST. Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest on Saturday at the Police Station, on the both of John Newns, five years of age, son of John Newns, wheelwright, who was drowned whilst sliding on a clay pit off hardy- street on Wednesday evening. Lord Newton, the owner of the ground, was represented by Mr. Fowler (Messrs. R. Davies and Co.),and Mr. PardeY, the agent, was also present. The mother of the de- ceased said he went out about four o'clock on Wed- nesday afternoon, and a few minutes afterwards word was brought; in that he had fallen into the pit.—By Nlr. Fowler : There was a sheet of ice on the pit. She could not say whether the pit had been dry all summer.. In answer to the Coroner as to whether the water in the pit was of use to any- body, witness said it was a sin to have it there, it smelled so.—Another witness, who spoke to seeing deceased and another little boy walk on the ice, also stated that the pit smelled horribly in su-nmer. The Coroner said it was not right that a sheet of water of no u,e to anybody should be left unfenced in the midst of a populous district.—The jury re- turned a verdict of " Accidental death," adding that-the best thing to do was to fill up the pit at once, it being dangerous and unhealthy.—Mr. Fowler undertook to have other pits tilled up as soon as possible.—The Coroner said he was perfectly, astonished .that the owner of the land and the Cor- poration should have 'allowed the pits to remain as they were for such a length of time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>TUE E'( A MINE R. A TURD it V. JANUARY 20, 1894.</head>
        <p>TUE E'( A MINE R. A TURD it V. JANUARY 20, 1894.</p>
        <p>THE DARESBITRY HIGH.WAY BOARD. THE SHIP CANAL BRIDGES.—STRONG COMPLAINTS. The quarterly meeting of this Board was held on Thursday afternoon in the Board room, Daresbury. In the absence of Mr. Parr, chairman of the B.Jard, Mr. T. S. Burgess, the deputy chairman. presided. The Lancashire, Derbyshire. and East Coast Rail- way Company forwarded to the Board a notice stae.ing that they intended in the session of 1894 to apik4y for extension of their powers, and the Clerk in- ti Mated that the same conditions would be given to the Board; as were given to them when the Company made a similar applicati•ai iis 1891. A long discussion then took place with the reference to the delay caused to vehicular traffic bv the keeping open of the swing bridges at Lower Walton and Stockton Heath. It was contended that the gates instxad of opening outwards should open inwards, and several members complained that they had been kept waiting an unnecessary length of time. One member said he had been kept half-an- hour waiting, and another said the road had been st,,pped from 11-30 a.m., to 2-30 in the afternoon A krg‘i number of school chiLiren Were also prevented from going home to dinner. The clerk was instructed to obtain an injunction against the Canal companv if necessary, te restrain them from using the bridges unless they were in working order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORIMPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The Sons of Temperance had a mpst successful time in Runcorn on Monday. The Grand Division of Cheshire held their annual session during the day, and transacted the needful business of the Order and elected the staff of officers. The retire- ment of Mr. Thomas Cooke, from the position of Grand Scribe, which be has held for a period of 17 years, was a source of univets-al regret, but the Session had to bow to the inevitable, and appoint a successor. It would be impossible to over-estim- ate the services Mr. Cooke has rendered to the Order in Runcorn district,and it will be gratifying to tem- perance workers to know that he retains his official connection by the acceptance of the position of Grand Patron of the Cadet Section.</p>
        <p>At night there was a public meeting and conceit held under the designation of a " pleasant evening' and interspersed with vocal melodyand instrumental music,were instructive speeches. The large audience which assembled, thoroughly enjoyed themselves, and as everything was without money and with- out price, there was the act led pleasure of no drain on the pocket. And in this regard, Mr. Cooke in his speech could not forbear a rather hard rap at our religious organisations. Not only, he said, was- the entertainment free, but there was also a free programme thrown in, which was very different to the practice followed at the churches or chapels,for if we did not pay when we went in, we had to pay before we came out, while if we went to the Salva- tion Army, we had to pay when we went in, pay in the midole of the service,and then pay to come out. There was much laughter at this comparison. There is no doubt that the free " pleasant evening" was much enjoyed. But it was worth the money it cost the Order; what a splendid advertisement they have obtained.</p>
        <p>During the recent dispute in the coal trade, the Runcorn Alkali Works succeeded in giving relief to their men thrown out of employment without mak- ing any special appeal to the public, and Mr. J. Poole has forwarded me a copy of the balance sheet which will probably interest many readers of the " Examiner." The total receipt amounted to £lll 12s. 3d., of which £9O us. 61 was collected from various sources, and -.C2l Os. 9,1. was received from the football match between Runcorn and Widnes. The credit side of the balance sheet showed that £lO was paid to Wigg's Works, and £5 to Weston Works, being proportion of the proceeds from the football match. Relief was given in groceries, &amp;c., to workmen, to the extent of £B7 75., and the sum of £5 10s. was spent in the purchasing of potatoes as relief. The balance in hand, £3 15s. 31 has been transferred to the treasurer of the Works Sick Club. The balance sheet was audited and found correct. by Mr, John Robinson, awl Mr. Stonewall Matthews. The record is a highly satisfactory one.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Wynne Humphreys, who has obtained an appointment under the Board of Trade has departed for London to assume his new position. His re- moval will necessitate his retirement from the Board of Commissioners,on which body he has been a very useful member. " Rumour" has already made free with several names as his probable suc- cessor. It is said that the vacancy has been"offered" to Mr. Fisher, who retired at the last election, and that gentleman declined the proffered honour. The names of Mr. Thomas Dean and Mr. Thomas Jones, who were within half a dozen votes of each other at the election, have also been mentioned. It is safe to say that the choice of the Commissioners will not be known until the meeting next month, and the rateyayers are awaiting the result with in- terest.</p>
        <p>The Vicar of Runcorn, Canon Maitland Wood, gave his annual address to the parishioners at. the Congregational tea meeting on Monday evening. His report as to.the position ot the church during the year will be considered satisfactory by those interested in church work. There had been a steady increase in the number of communicants. He also made an appropriate reference to the loss sustained by the church in the death of t 790 members of the ' Speakman family. The Vicar had to complain of the attendance at trorning services. No doubt all the places of worship in the town are far from tilled. Evidently the devotional feeling does not operate so strongly early in the day. There \is not the slightest doubt it. is the habit to take an extra ' dose of " nature's sweet nstorer," sleep, on a Sun- day morning and who can wonder that it is so, after the worry and turmoil of six days' business. Probably the Vicar's appeal may induce some of his flock in future to sacrifice the blankets for the music and prayer of the tabernacle.</p>
        <p>If the Vicar's strong reference to the condition of Greenway-road on Sunday evenings, leads to an im- provement, he will earn the gratitude of hundreds of townspeople. Greenway-road is decidedly the pleasantest thoroughfare in the town, and as it leads into the country lanes beyond, its popularity as a promenade need not, therefore, be a matter of surprise. Every person has a right to use the road within certain bounds; but no person is at liberty to annoy other pedestrians, either by taking an un- due proportion of the roael,or by unseemly behaviour ot manner and speech. The simple rule of keep to the right "suggested by the Vicar. would pro- bably effect some improvement, and it must, be left. to the police to do the rest.</p>
        <p>It had used to be a common footnote to ulcer- tisments of entertainments "Children in arms not admitted," ly.it this form of announcement appears to have fallen into disuetude. The organisers of the Perish Church concert on Monday, were evi- dently not ',ware of the treat in store,or the custom would have been revived. "Children in arms" were present in force, and they made their presence heard if not felt. The music that the poet tells us has power to soothe the savage beast, had quite the opposite effect upon the "rising generation" as represented that evening. No sooner had the vocalis,t risen to the point, when the ears of the au- dience were to be enchanted with the concord of sweet sounds, than a squall would arise from some part of the roomiand at last, the Vicar,after making several appeals to the mothers, evidently lost patience and commanded a mother to take out "that child." And no more did childish treble mingle with the harmony that proceeded from the plat- form. Poor mothers; happy unconscious infants!</p>
        <p>There was a gratifying attendance at the second of the winter series of lectures at the Liberal Club on Wednesday night, and the great and absorbing interest with which the audience followed Mr. Kniveton in his address, was flattering testimony to his powers as a lecturer. Some very important. points were touched upon, and the lecture will well repay perusal. The Club Committee are on the right lines in thus catering for the literary and pol- itical tastes of the members, who are exceedingly well catered for as regards amusements and social gatherings. One of the Secretaries of the Financial Reform Union is announced to deliver the next lecture, and this should he of especial interest, at a time when the expenditure of the country is advancing.by leaps and hounds,</p>
        <p>ENTERTAINMENT AT LATCHFORD. An entertainment was given, on Monday night, ' in the Parish Room, Latchford, when there was a very large audience. The programme was a good one, and consisted of a dramatic dialogue from " The Hunchback," a sketch from " The Shang hraun," and also a sketch from " Meg's Diversion," which were performed by Miss Helen Conway and Mr. W. R. Duncan. They also sang a duet entitled " Prythee, pretty maid.,n." Two recitals" Carl. the martyr," and" Captain Quarters" and a song " Four Jolly smiths" were given by Mr. Duncan, and Miss Conway also gave a recital.</p>
        <p>STorr.s.—GAII.LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, Bridge-street. Warrington. CONSERVATIVE WORKINGMEN'S BALL. The Conservative workingmen's annual ball took place at the Conservative Club on Monday evening, there being nearly three hundred persons present. The assembly room was decorated as on the occa- sion ot Thuisclay's ball, and the supper tables were also sumptuously furnished. For this latter feature Mr. Ingham, manager, was responsible. Alderman Wright and Councillox. Smethurst were the M.C.'s, and discharged their duties in an admirable manner. The great bulk of the work in the way of carrying out the arrangementB tor the ball fell upon the shoulders of Mr. 11. 11. Daintith, the lion. sec., who was ably assisted by Mr, Thomas Mason, the hon. treasurer. Among those present were Mrs. Reynolds, Miss Reynolds, Mr..Svlvanus Reynolds, Mr. P. Goy Reynolds, Captain Itivanson, Mr. 1)( 11,haff,, Mr. Percy Mortimer, Mr. John White, Mr. John Lee, Miss 0. Gret ton Cope, Mrs. Grounds, Miss Grounds, Mr. A. Grounds, Councillor Smethur,t, and Alderman Wright. Mr. Percy Rundall's band supplied the muslc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>EMXIANUEL CHURCH- TEA PARTY AND</head>
        <p>EMXIANUEL CHURCH- TEA PARTY AND</p>
        <p>SOCIAL WITHERING.</p>
        <p>The annual tea party in connection with the Emmanuel Church, was held in the schoolroom over Arpley Station, on Thursday night. About a hun- dred people sat down to a substantial tea. The following ladies presided at the tables :—Mrs. Reed, Mrs. Parsons, Mrs. Flesketh, Miss Evans, Mrs. Landon, Mrs. Henry Garuly; Mrs. Norman, Mrs. A. Gaudy, and Miss Hindiev. After tea there was a small sale of work in the Church parlour. A programme of anthems by the choir, sacred solos, trios, violin solos, and piano solos, was pleasingly rendered. An eloquent and inter- eAing address was delivered by Mr. J. Wakefield Mac Gill, secretary or the Manchester City Mission. After the address by Mr. Mac Gill, the Rev. E. T. Reed in reviewing the work of the past year said that the number of scholars attending the Sunday schools ' had considerably increased during the year, and several additional classes had been formekwhico met in the , smaller schoolroom. An additional room was secured at the beginning of the year for the accomoind:ttion of the Young Women's Bible Class.. This room, whet was next to the Church parlour, was cleaned and beautifully papered at cost price by Messrs. Allen and Sons. It was to be regretted that owing to lack of funds this work had not yet been pail for. In connection with the church they had again to thank Mr. Pave for hi; very kind interest taken in the fabric. A sum of £3293. 41. had hen received towards the reduction of the debt upon the Church, thus reduc- ing to £lBB. It was interesting to notice that in two vors the debt has baen reducsd from £345 to £lBB. I had been spent the evening votes of This was more than the re.duction in the six previous nyleeaertsingAfct:nrcaludneledasawnitthevtehnein thanks. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>ANNUAL MEETING OF THE HOSPITAL SUNDAY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF THE HOSPITAL SUNDAY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The annaal meeting of this Committee was held at the Infirmary yesterday afternoon, There were present the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton (in the chair), the Revs, R. V. Pigott, J. Kirtlan, Owen Watkins, G. D. Mason' E. S. Jackson, Davies, McNair, and Firth; also Messrs. John Fairclough, J. Roberts, and F. W. Monks,and A.J. Jolley (hon. secs.) The following report was submitted by the secretiries : " The collections made in 1893 are satisfactory if regard be paid to the depression which was so generally felt in the town during the early part of the year. They amounted to £2lB 1., being £lO is. 6.1. less than was collected in 1892, As compared with that year nine places of worship (seven of which are outside the town) have this year failed to contribute ; whilst on the other hand, three, one tewn and two country, have renewed their offertories. The nine contributed in 1892, £l9 153. 10d. ; the three in 1893 £5 16s. 11?2-1., which more than accounts for the diminished total. The expenses have been for advertising £7 15s. ,for stationery and pos. ' tages £3 134. 9d., total £ll Bs. 9d., as compared £9 4s. 3d. in 1892, and £ll Os. 6d. in 1891."— The report was adopted, after which votes of thanks were passed to the hon. secs. Mr. F. W. Monks and Mr. Jolley for their services during the year. Mr. Monks resigned his office owing to pressure of business and Mr. Jolley was appointed sole secretaty. The next hospital Sunday was fixed for the fourth of March. This com- pleted the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PADGATE IVESLEYA.N SUNDAY SCHOOL</head>
        <p>PADGATE IVESLEYA.N SUNDAY SCHOOL</p>
        <p>--- - The annual tea meeting in connection with the Padgate Wesleyan Sunday School, was held in the schoolroom on Thursday evening. A good number sat down to tea, towards which the following ladies and gentlemen had contributed trays ;—Mesdames Houghton, Royston, 'Fowles, London, Harrington, Ward, Hindley, Deane, Steel, Evans, Pearce, Young, Yates, William Smith, White, Hall. John Houghton. T. Bennett, William Bennett, Walter Bennett, Harris, Richardson, and W. E. Thornton ; Missses Hankey and James; CLauncillor A. Bennett, and Mr. Jackson. Alter tea, the annual public meeting was held, the chair being occupied by Mr. Edwin Richardson. The Chairman, in his opening speech, after wishing all pre- sent a lia,pny and prosperous new year, expressed his ' regret at the absence of Mr. William Bennett, who had been laid aside with a very serial's illness. Ile wished to express the sympathy of the meeting towards him, and he hoped that Mr. Bennett would long be spared to the thurch. Mr. G. F. White, the secretary, in giving the annual report of the, Sunday School, said that they had been plodding along with their work, although they have not made much noise. The number of scholars had been slowly but surely growing. There were 174 sholars on the books, an inorease of 13 upon last year. The aver- age attendar.ce had been 79 in the morning and 125 in the afternoon. There were 18 teachers, whose average attendance was eight in the morning, and ten in the afternoon. The officers for the ensuing year were Messrs. Henry Ward and Frederick Wright superintend- ents ; Mr. Frank Harris, treasurer ; Mr. T. S. Steel, librarian ; and Mr. G. F. White, secretary. The cloth- ing club had been very successful, as also the Band of Hope, and Mr. Henry Ward and Mr. Frank Harris had organized one or two public meetings, and there were others in prospect. There were 300 books in the library. He was very much pleased that the elder scholars remained at the school, and were very regular In their attendance. With regard to financial matters, there W S at present a balance of £3, and he hoped that their friends would continue their liberal support in the future as they had done in the past. The Rev.G.D.Masonsaid that he hadalways liked com- ing to Padgate since his first visit. He was glad to be present on such a pleasant occasion at the annual gather- inc, of a Sunday school, as he considered . that Sunday , schools were more than mere auxiliaries of the church; they were essential to the church's welfare. The pre- sence of so many at the meeting was a good evidence of the sympathy with which the work was regarded. and he was sure that if at any time it became necessary to enlarge the accommodation of tke school, those present woald do their share of the work. During the evening. the following programme was given. Song ; " Come lasses and lads," Mr. E. Booth; recitation, "One legged goose," Mr. Dundan ; violin solo, Miss Birchall; s*mg, "Dapple grey" Miss Gunton; recita- tion, " It never touched the ground," Miss Mary Ward; pianoforte solo, Maria Hall ; dialogue, Miss Jane Car- penter, and Masters Robert Carpenter, and Arthur Ward ; Lancashire recitation, Mr. Grounds ; song, "Life is jolly," Mr. James Bennett ; song, " Horns Sweet Homo," Master Harry Bennett; recitation, " How Ter- rence Saved his bacon," Mr. Duncan; song, "Friar of Orders Grey," Mr. E. Booth ; recitation, Miss Minnie Fletcher ; recitation, Miss Mary Bennett; song, Master Barry Bennett. Miss L. Bennett ably accompanied on the piano. An exhibition of musical drill was also ex- cellently given by a number of childun, who were trained by Miss Het tv Ward. At the close of the meet- ing a hearty vote of thanks was accorded the chairman, and others who had helpcd to Make the entertainment a Success. Last evening a, treat was given to the children of the Snnday school and afterwards a suctessful entertainment was given by a number of the scholars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>110DSHAM.</head>
        <p>110DSHAM.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF LOCAL SHIPPING ACCOMKODATION. •</p>
        <p>MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS.—The opening meeting, of the latter part of the present session was held on Fri- day evening, in the presence of a representative number of members and friends. The meeting was presided over by the Rev. R. Renton. The programme was a success- ful one, comprising choruses hr the choir, songs, piano- fore and violin solos, humorous readings, vocal duet, and trios. 'RAILWAY ACCIDENT.—ShortIy after one o'clock on Wednesday a goods train ran off the print; whilst b“ing shunted jumped across two lines of metals,and narrowly escaped falling dov-n an embankment. The rails were vet y badly twisted, and both the up and down lines were hli,elred for some considerable tine. Several pasFeenuer tra,ins -wore delayed. ANCIENT ORDER OF DRINDS.—ThP usual half-yearly meeting of this ar,eient order was held on Saturday even- ing, when several new members were initiated and other 'business done. The medical officership was tinder con- siderat ion for a consider, tile me, lA' hi-11 it was decided Ito give the present medical officer not ice. The election of a new medical officer was then taken in liand,aiid it was nnanitnougic- carried that T. J. Selby, M.D., L.R.C.P., Ste., be elected to the post.</p>
