      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTING A POLICEMAN. James Brown, who said he was for three years in the Coldstream Guards, was charged at the Borough Court, 1 on Tuesday, with being drunk and disorderly in Bank- street, and also with assaulting P.C. David Jones, at the same time and place. Prisoner pleaded not guilty. Mr. James Brayshaw, caretaker for Mr. A. Browne, deposed that the prisoner was misconductmg himself at his back door, the previous night. He refused to go away, and witness brought a policeman. But the prisoner had re- moved, and they found him in Bridge-street with two other men. The constable apprehended the wisoner, and on the way to the piilice station the officer was abused by the prisoner. P.C. Jones ftesed to appre- hending the prisoner, and for a time the prisoner walked quietly to the police station, hut when in Bank-street he struck witness in the face. The prisoner ran away, but be (witness) soon afterwards re-captured him. A scufll.-t ensued on the ground, in which the prisoner was assisted by two frienas. Witness's ear was bitten. Another constable afterwards came to his assistance, but the other men got away. Inspector Spinks said he was at the police station when the prisoner, who was drunk, was brought in. Constable Jones' ear was bleeding,and looked as if it had been bitten. The magistrates com- mitted the prisoner to gaol for one month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT..</p>
        <p>SATI.I.II;;Y.-- Refo—re Messrs J:•Burgess and J. Ueddes.</p>
        <p>THE RESULT OF " TREATING."—Two young women, named Mary Jane Hamblett and Winifred Jennings,both well-known to the police, were charged with stealing the sum of about 15s. from the person of John Moly- neux, while in the Unicorn Inn, on Friday afternoon. From the facts stated by the Chief Constable, it ap- peared that complainant is a labourer employed at a glass works at St. Helens, and during the past seven weeks had been training with the Liverpool Militia at Orford Barracks. The regiment was disbanded on Fri- day morning, and the complainant went into the town. He had in his p4ssession at the time 245. He met the prisoners and, at their request, accompanied them to a public-house, when he treated them to seveial glasses of beer. They afterwards went to the Unicorn Inn. where the complainant fell asleep, and when he awoke he found that he had been robbvd. and that the women had disappeared. He gave information to the police,and the prisoners were cleverly apprehended by Detective Monaghan.—The prisoners admitted robbing the com- plainant of ss. 5d., which they divided between them.— They were committed to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>HAD TO BE " CEMCICED "OUT.—James Mannion,a farm labourer, from Bold, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Lythnoe's-lane on the previous night.—lnspector Spinks proved the case. A Mr. Hunt, who lives in Lythgoe's-lane, said the defendant walked into his house, and used language "that was not ex- actly nice." Witness " " or" chucked " him in- to the street, but prisoner came back again. (Laugh- ter.) He said he would not go out for anyone, and witness had to put him out again —The Chairman : You had to persuade him again. (Laughter.)—Mr. Hunt : Yes, I chucked him out.—Mannion was committed to prison for 14 days with hard labour. MONDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), and Mr. 8. Wright. DEUNIEENNESS.—Cha ries Crol t, 359, Winwick-road,and William Harper, 26, Haydock-street, were each fined 53. for this offence. James William Woodward, and Mary Parry were ordered to leave the town Tor the same offence.</p>
        <p>CHIMNEYS ON FlRE.—Cath.rinp rt,,binson, 9, Tanners- lane. and James Potter, 13, Powys-street, were each fined is. for allowing their chimneys to be on fire. THESDIY.—Before Mr. R. tilrnett (chairman), and Mr.</p>
        <p>E. J. Gust.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASES —Mr. G. Riley, school attendance nffic,.r, prosecutrd in the following cases of persons summoned for irregular school attendance: Moses Williams, forgeman, 6, Back 6tamfnrd-st. ; Ralph Bird, fkgeman, I,Park Place ; Robert Cade, 39, Orford- lane ; Wm. Jones, forgelnan, Dudley-st ; Richard Booth, forgeman, 373, Winwick-road ; Mary Eden Holding, 37, Beswick-s.t. ; Joseph Gerrard, bricksetter, 13, Hopwood- st.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman),</p>
        <p>and IMr. John Geddes</p>
        <p>AN ABSENTEE.—Frederick Leslie, was charged with absenting himself from the South Lancashire Militia on the 27th of June 1892. The prisoner was arrested at the Orford Barracks, and pleaded guilty. He was fined £5 or one month's imprisonment. ASSAULT.—Thomas Burke was charged with being drunk and disorderly at Market Gate,the previous night and also eharged with assaulting James Leach and Alfred Gough at the same time and place. P.C. Turner said at 11.20 the pr-viotis night, tie was in Buttermarket st., c; hen he saw the prisoner who was drunk, assault two men. James Leach and Alfred Gough deposed to being kicked and struck by the prisoner without any provocation whilst standing talking in Buttermarket-st. For the defence Burke contended that he was struck by the complainant Gough. The magistrates fined the prisoner 206. and costs or ere month's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY.-134.f0re Alderman Harrison .(chairman), Dr.</p>
        <p>Goma!! and t:ouncllor Rotierts.</p>
        <p>THEFT OF A SUIT OF CLOTHES.—Peter Adcrofr, 53, Watkin-street, was charged with stealing a suit of clothes valued SI 195., the property of John Whitfield, on Dec. 22. From the statement of the Chief Constable, it appeared that Whitfield was a tailor, residing in Bat- tersby-lane, and the defendant who is also a tailor occas- ionally worked for him. Just before Christmas, the defendant called upon Whitfield and asked for something to do as times were bad. Whitfield, on the 22nd Dec- ember, handed to defendant a suit of clothes to repair. Sometime afterwards he heard that defendant had left Warrington with the clothes, and communicated with the police. Detective Mon .ghan met defendant on the previous evening in Tanners-lane and arrested him. It was subsequently discovered that he had pledged the trousers and vest, and sold the coat.—Prisoner was fined 10s. and costs. A SCENE IN THE WORKHOUSE.—Annie Smith alias Brown, was charged with bring drunk and disorderly in the workhouse on the previous afternoon. She was sent to gaol for 14 days. DRUNK AND DISORDEULF.—C3therine Stokes, no settled abode, and James Wilson, 3 Garden-row were fined ss. and Costs. MAINTENANCE ORDER.—Peter Alfred Newton, farmer. Bold, was charged with deserting his wife Ellen Newton in February 1890, and failing to maintain her. Mr. Jenkins who appeared for the comi lainant said that Mr. and Mrs. Newton were married at Bowdon Church on February 14,1884, and had two children. The husband left his wife on the above date, and had not contributed a single farthing to his wife's suppoit since, although he had signed an agreement to contribute 7s. a week to- wards his children. Be had plenty of means, and could well afford a substantial sum. The complainant ask,-cl for 15s. a week.—The magistrates granted the appli- cation.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTS.—SamueI Taylor, 16 Howarcl-st., was fined 10s. and costs fer assaultinz arid heatirg Jaltles Davies and Sarah Davies on the 15th inst Davies ard another man were returning home on the night in question, and when in Howard-st., they tell over the defendant who</p>
        <p>was lying full length across the footpath. The defend- ant then got up and knocked Davies down inflicting a wound upon the head. Later in the evening Mrs. Davies went to the house of the defendant and asked him why he had struck her husband, and in reply he knocked her against the lamp-post giving har a black eye.—Benjamin Tasker, 19 Orchard-st., was dismissed on a charge of assaulting his wife Alice Tasker on January 10th, but a summons was granted to the stife• for desertion</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.--13;fore Messrs. T. J. Gillespie (chairman),l H.Pearson, J. L. Wood and R. Stone. DRUNEENNE69.—James Costello, Newton, and Joseph Jackson, Newton, were each fined for this offence, and William Davenport was dismissed. ASSAULT ON AN OLD WOMAN.—Gerald L. Fitmgerald was charged with assaulting Dinah Middletun at Newton on January Ist. Mr. R. Stuart, who prosecuted, said that toe complainant was a widow, residing in Mill-lane, Newton. Between seven and eight o'clock ,m Monday week she went to the defendant's house to ask him for some money which he owed to her, he having been a lodger at her house, and left her in debt. A dispute arose, during which the complainant received two black eyes.—The complainant gave corroborative evidence.— Defendant denied that he struck her, and said that he simply put his hand on her shoulder and pulled her out of the house.—The chairman said that the bench were of opinion that the defendant had committed an unpro- voked assault upon the woman, and would bind him over for six months in a surety of £lO. CAPTURE OF POACHERS.—Mark Luke was charged with poaching in the day-time, on land in the occupation of Peter Longion, at Win wick, on the 7th inst. The com- plainant was represented by his brother, Mr. H. J. Long- ton, who said that at about 3 30 on tho afternoon in question, the complainant saw four men in a field, belonging to him, poaching for rabbits. He went into the field after tl-em, and three of the men ran away, but the defendant remained until complainant got close to him. He was afterwards given ha charge.—The defendant denied the charge, and said that he had not owned a ferret for four weeks.—As the defendant had been previoasly convicted for a similar offence, he was titled 103. and costs.—Samuel Mains and John Williams, were each charged with night poaching on the 7th inst. in the same field that the last defendant was charged. Mr. I..ongton again appeared fcr the complainant. He said that the two defendants were sent off the field several times by Mr. Longton, and they threatened to set his stacks on fire. They also threw stones at him, and severely hurt the finger of his man John Parsonage. The first defendant was a very well-known character. He was instructed to press the case.—The complainant and his man gave corroborative evidence.—The prisoner Mains said that he had been before the magistrates many. a time before, but this time he was not aunty. —Thp magistrates fined Mains 203. and costs, and ordered him to be bound over for twelve months, himself in £lo,ami two sureties of £5. Williams, who made his first appearance at the court, was fined 103. and costs, and also bound over for twelve months.</p>
        <p>_ SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASE.—Peter Travis. Golbornr, was fined ss. for neglecting to scnd his child regularly to school. Mr. Henry Pegg prosecuted.</p>
        <p>WIDNES POLICE COURT. MONDAY.—Before Councillor W. W. Gossage. IMPUDENT BEGGARS.—TWO men named James Haworth and James Whittaker, who said they belonged to Black- burn, were charged with begging on Saturday night.— P.C. Carter and Inspector Pattison, stated that numerous complaints had been received about defend- ants insulting those who refused to give them anything. Defendants were sent to gaol for seven days. DRUNMENNESS.—WiIIiam Holding, no fixed abode James Riley, Pitt-street ; James Dircey, Sankey-street ; Mary Jane Noon, Fisher-street ; and Harold Collins, Mid- wood-street, were fined for this offence. AN OLD COAL STEALER.—A married woman named Mary Walton, Old Marsh, was brought up charged with stealing Thcwt. of coal, valued at is., the property of the London and North-Western Railway Company.— Superintendent Keighley said that coal stealers had given a great deal of trouble lately, and prisoner, who had been committed for stealing on four occasions, seemed to be one of the ringleaders. She told him that she had lived on the Old Marsh two years, but during that time had not bought any coal. He asked that she be sent to the Sessions for trial Doferidasit was committed to take her trial at the Liverpool Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. Alfred Thomas and Mr. James Handley. _ _ _</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.—EIizabeth Barker, an old offendtr,was fined 103. and costs for being drunk and disorderly. For alike offence Martin Conway was fined 10s. including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ROBBERIES AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ROBBERIES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Petty Sessions on -Monday before Mr. A. Thomas and Mr. James Handley, Albert Walker aged 11 years, of Dover-street, was charged with having on the 11th January stolen a pair of boots value 68. from the pawnshop of William Henry Lightfoot, High-street, Runcorn. The boots were hanging inside the doorway until Thursday evening when they were. missed. On the same evening the prisoner took the articles to the pawn- shop of Mr. Craddock, Church-street,and said his father had just pulled them off, and his mother had sent him to pawn them because they had no food in the house. Mr. Craddock advanced 3s. upon them. The boots apn':ared to have been worn. SubAequently P.C. Gregory appre- hended the prisoner who admitted the theft, and said he dirtied the boots in Alcock-street, before taking them to pawn. He said he had hidden is. 9.1. under Phillip's back door in Wilson's row, and the constable found that sum there. Superintendent Atherton said the prisoner only a week ago had been birched for theft, and in December last he was bound (Ivor for judgment for steal- ing coal.—Mr, Thomas said he would now receive twolve strokes with the birch rod: if he were ever again brought up he would be sent to a reformatory. At the same court, James Fearnett a young man was charged with stealing two. towels worth 24. 4d., the pro- perty of Lewis Addleston, draper, of 5, Winningtori Court, Witton-street, North wicb. On Saturday the prosecutor had a still on Runcorn Market Ground and he alleged that just before eleven o'clock, the prisoner loitered about his stall, and when he was closing he saw him take the towels, place them under his coat and walk away. He gave him into the cus,ody of P. C. Davies. The prisoner said there was a crowd about the prosecutor's stall,and as he crossed the parapet he picked up the towels from the ground. Then some persons said "see that young man has got the towels." The pro- secutor charged him with stealing thtnwis whereuprrn he threw them to the floor saying if they were his he must take them from where he found therm—Superin- tendent Atherton said because the prisoner was a local man and a man of good character, he had been out on hail.—The magistrates imposed a penal;y of 103. includ- ing costs._</p>
        <p>'Enoch Shaw, Heath-road, Richard Price, Egerton- street, and William Harrop, back Cooper-street, each nine years of age, were all charged on Monday with hav- ing stoles a purse containing 13s. 63. from the person of Miriam Kenwright, of Heath-road. On Saturday after- noon the prosecutor went to the market and purchased some fish. She then discovered that her purse had dis- appeared. Later she gave information to the police and P.C. Gregory ascertaining that the prisoners had been spending money freely in potato chips, and toffee and had also been to the theatre, apprehended them. Harrop and Price alleged that Shaw was the actual pickpocket. Price said the purse had been thrown away at the top of Ellesmere-street, but it had not been found after a long search. Price and Shaw had been previously charged with theft. Each prisoner was sentenced to receive six strokes with the birch rod.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>• THE UNEMPLOYED QUESTION.</head>
        <p>• THE UNEMPLOYED QUESTION.</p>
        <p>A deputation from the Manchester and Salford Trades Council waited upon the Mayor of Salford on Monday to urge that the Corporation should do what they could to find work for the unemployed. Mr. G. D. Kelley, the secretary of the Trades Council, said there was a slight increase in the number of skilled workmen, out of em- ployment as compared with this time last year,and a large increase in the number of un:kil4ed men who were with- out work. The Mayor said he would communicate with the chairman of committees, and had go doubt that an effort would be made to put in hand as much work as possible. 'file Cleansing Committee and the Parks Com- mittee of the Manchester Corporation found employment on Monday for about four hundred men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LITTLE SISTERS OF THE POOR.</head>
        <p>THE LITTLE SISTERS OF THE POOR.</p>
        <p>The Little Sisters of the Poor have much pleasure in bearing their testimony as to the efficacy of Allcock's Porous Plasters. They have seen great benefit derived from them on account of the relief they so quickly give the sufferer. They find them a very soothing remedy for all sorts of pains.—T.M.T. Home for the Aged Poor, Parkfield-avenue, Birkenhead. - - - .</p>
        <p>The REV. MARK GUY PEARSE says I find then a very breast plate against colds and coughs." Mr. HENRY THORNE (evangelist) says : —" I have used them for pains in the back and side arising from rheumatic and other causes, always deriving benefit from their application. They are easily applied and very comforting." Over twenty millions of Allcock's Plasters have already been sold in Great Britain, the most telling testimony to this great external remedy. Allcock's Plasters stop all aches and pains and sprains and strains, Lumbago, Sciatica, Rheumatism, Ruptures, Bronchitis, Asthma, all affections of the Throat and Lungs, acting directly on the tissues, dilating the pores of the skin, giving instant relief.</p>
        <p>ALLCOCK'S PLASTERS are of great efficacy in INDIGESTION if worn over right side of the Pit of the Stomach.</p>
        <p>ALLCOCK'S—Of all Chemists, 111 k.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>".` Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the head office. Bewsey-street. Warrington. nit later than ten o'clock on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>GEORGE G. BUSSEY and Co. are leading makers of Footballs, Hockey, Golf, and requisites for Sports, Games, and Gymnasia. Catalogue may be had on application ,)o the Sports Manufactory, Peckham, London., S.E. BEST &lt; CCD BIUND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>RUGBY.</head>
        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. BROITGLITON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>Happiness reigned once more among the Warrington, football enthusiasts, on Saturday. As it iseometimes poetically put, "every cloud has its silver lining," and the cloud which has hung over the Warrington football slub—owing to the deterent effect of the frost,, and the unwillingness of opponents to allow victories to be scored—was effectually removed on Saturday. The club met Broughton Rangers in the Lancashire Cup Competition, and before a gate of some five thousand persons notched another- victory on the- " counting stick" and climbed another rung of the statistical lad- der. A considerable time ago, the Rangers. met the War- rington team and administered unto them a " licking " which served to instil into the Wirepedlers a determina- tion to return the compliment at the earliest possible occasion. And this they very easily did on Saturday. Warrington were still without Moscow, his place being filled by Heesom, and the only addition of note to the ordinary team was H. Barber of Stockton Heath, who made a creditable aleaut at three quarter. Promptly at three o'clock the teams of the following composition marched on to the green sward :—Warrington.—F. Heesom, back; P. Carey, J. W. Evans,J. T. Massey,and H. Barber, three-quarters ' • James Bate and John Bate, half-hacks; Fair Turner,J.Jolley,W.Nevins, J. Taylor,J. Buckley,J.T.Thorniley,E.Bailey.and J.Dakin, forwards. Broughton Rangers.—J. N. Hacking. back; E. Howe, H. Cl spman, R. Whitehead, and J. Priestley, three- quarters; C. H. Thompson, aud J. Wilson, half-backs; J. A. Squires,S. Oman.J. Nowell, J. Fitton,J. M'Gowan, W. G. Campbell. Cleminson, and Greer, forwards. I Referee, Mr. H. Vipond (Manchester Free Wanderers). The opening movements resulted in the home forwards taking up a position on the Rangers' goal line, and, alter "Smack "Bate had seemed to wriggle over without catching the referee's eye, Whitehead neatly stole an opponent's pass and gave to Chapman. The latter made a fine, dodgy run to halfway, but could not get past Evans. From the succeeding scrimmage the ball was sent to Priestley,who, however, mulled it, and Hack- ing only just managed to kick dead in time to prevent Barber scoring. The Rangers' forwards broke away finely, and with a little more dash would have scored, but, as it was, Chapman almost dropped the ball over the bar from a penalty kick. Heesom saved well, and was responsible for changing the venue of the play, and a miskick by Chapman gave Barber an easy chance for a try, but over eagerness lost it. Both sides were trying to play an open game, but the smart tackling of the rival halves nipped most of the attempts in the bud. A scrimmage broke up in the Rangers' half, and the visi- tors, appearing to think the ball had been made dead, began to reform, but the Warrington pack rushed on, and Dakin grounded the ball over line. The try was allowed, but Evans failed at the place kick. The Ran- gers were not discomfited, and immediately forced their way to the Warrington line, mainly through a grand run by Chapman. Heesom sent the leather by a poor kick right into Newell's arms ten yards from the line, but in- ability to do the right thing at the right moment lost the opportunity. A moment later Heesom made his only slip in the match by mulling Whitehf ad's kick, but as none of the Rangers' forwards had thought it worth while to follow up closely, a try, which would have been a regular gift was thrown away. The interval came with Warrington a try to the good, and with the wind at their backs the game seemed safe. Soon after the restart Hacking very foolishly tried to kick the ball through a slender youth named Thorniley with as little success as could be expected, and as they were on the goal line it was wonderful how a try was saved. The two Bates started a rush, and " Smack" got over, but was recalled, though the next minute he and Evans sent the ball to Barber, who took it at top sped and ran in splendidly at the corner. There was a little confabula- tion between the referee and the umpire, but the try was registered. Fair rumor made a poor attempt to convert it. The game livened up considerably, and a lot of passing and kicking was indulged in by both sides though the accuracy of the play degenerated. The home team did most of the attacking, and Wilson in saving a dangerous rush, got doubled up, and his knee giving way he had to be carried off the field. Massey made a well-judged drop at goal, and though the cheering of the srowd indicated a goal, those near the posts knew it had gone about a yard wide. Barber received a neat pass from " Smack "Bate, and made a grand sprin' the corner flag, where he was pushed in touch. The next moment the ball was thrown in and Nevins had dropped over the line before the Rangers had even thought of lining up. Taylor nearly placed the goal. This made the result certain, and after a dead ball bad been conceded to Broughton, time was called amid loud cheers, with the result. :</p>
        <p>sprint to I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Warrington 3 tries (9 points) Broughton Rangers</head>
        <p>Warrington 3 tries (9 points) Broughton Rangers</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON A v. BROUGHTON RANGERS A.—This eon': test came off at Broughton on Saturday, the play being opened by Warrington. Then the warfare opened out, Clark made a fire run, the Raugers retaliating by drib- bling in the visitors' 25. More scrambling amongst the forwards and halves ensued, until Hampson, Handles, Freeth, and Teare led a fast rush into their opponents' quart, rs, where Clark threw to Broady, who almost dodged over. A dash by Holmes Wa :. nullified by Hart- ley, and the home team had a glorious opportunity of scoring, which was, however, badly missed. Lawless, Donohue, Coggins and Thornton cleared, and Cross in- tercepting a pass ran and kicked to the Broughton terri- tory, Broady dashing up at top speed and tackling Tweedale on the line. Some kicking between Boscow and Hartley was followed by Gibson seizing possession, and eluding his opponents he got a splendid try behind the pests, which Cross converted. Then the Rangers at- tacked, only to see Thornten, Hampson, Freeth and Cross retaliate with smart work, which was supple- mented by Clark getting beautifully away., and when near the line he pass-d to Smith, who completed the try. After this Holmes made a splendid run and half time ar- rived. Restarting Broughton attacked • in the first moment, and Hartley made a good effort to drop a goal, the oval just missing the posts. Warrington, by the aid of a splendid drop out, were again on the attack,Broadv making a firm effort to get in, hut the Rangers; tackled , dtterminedly. Capital passing, in which Hampson, Lawless., Clark, and Holmes participated, found the latter dashing to the line. Garrity, Hartley, and Cum- mings quickly worked the ball to the Warring- ton quarters, Handles getting clear with a splendid run and pass, which was missed. Broady dashed to the line, after which a grand bit of work was witnessed. Lawless threw to Clark, who re-passed quickly across the pack, and Gibson taking the opportunity dashed over the line, Cross placing a second goal. Good kicking by Cross, and some good rushes by '['tare, Freeth, Coggins, Donohue, and Lawless continually tested the home defence, Garrity ultimately clearing to mid-field. Walsh sprinted to the visitors' line, but Cross, Thornton, Handles, and Hampson led a rush to the other end, when Clark got over splendidly. Then Broady dodged across and gave Smith a fine chance, but this player, who was badly hurt earlier on, was unable to accept the onpor- tunity. A hard I„ame ended Warrington 2-2-1 to 0-0-2.</p>
        <p>IIIiTNCORN V. LIVERPOOL.—The second match of the season between these teams was played at Runcorn on Saturday, in springlike weather. The crowd was poor, considering the standing of the Liverpool organiration. Both clubs had got strong teams, Liverpool playing— Melly, back; Fraser, Todd and Pearce, three-quarter backs ; Allen and Birkett, half hacks; Nelson, Stoddart, Nett, Wright, Chancellor, Bospher, Mortimer, Claughton and Forwood, forwards. Runcorn : Houghton, back ; Warder, Richardson. Morris and Myers, three-quarter backs ; Abrams and J. Faulkner, half hacks ; W. Faulk- ner. Farmer, Taylor, Stubbs, Reece, Moores. Antrohus, and Garter, forwards. Faulkner, the home captain, opened the play, and subsequently passing by the home halves to Richardson, who threw to Morris, gained some ground, which was not held very long, as Allen, Todd, and Fraser made quick exchanges. The wing man being easily stopped, the ball at once travelled back to the Liverpool qnarter, hut the city men transferred to mid-field where hostilities located for a lengthened period. Birkett dribbled from the scrimmage, and Hoyghton, picking up, sent the ball hack to touch past thelialf-way flag. Again, by the aid of Allen, Todd and Pearce, ti-7e visitors were prominent with some short passine. and in a subsequent forward effort, Houghton missed the hall. Warder, however, picked up, and punted to touch. From the succeeding play J. Faulk- ner, 11 arder and Richardson passed, the latter being well held by Pearce and Todd. A free kick t 3 Liver- pool gave Warder the opportunity of getting the ball into the visitors' quarters, then, from a scrim- mage, J. Faulkner passed to Abrams ; then Morris got the ball, and scored. Warder, however, missed the goal. Re-starting, play was very fast. Some neat pas-- ing by the Liverpool three-quarter line was appreciated by the crowd. The advantage gain,, was quickly neu- tralized by J. Faulkner, who cot clean away, and, pass- inc to Richardson, this strong runn.r was only held under the posts by M,lly. Thus a certain try was pre- vented. The pressure gained was well held. The Run- corn forwards, principally led by Taylor, Farmer, ard Stubbs, gave the Liverpool men a lot of trout- le. Even- tually Abrams gave J. Faulkner an opportunity to score 1 under the pots, Warder kicking a goal. After the interval Wilson started. The borne team soon got the upper hand, and Liverpool being well beaten forward, were nev,r dangero.is. The first attack was made by Taylor. who wit away with a dribble, and some loose I scrambling play nearly let Runcorn in. Allen kicked out i and Warder taking the ball made his mark. Faulkner tock the kick at goal, which tell short. and Stubbs corn- !</p>
        <p>ing up at full speed grounded the ball right under ,Nieily'; nose, much to his suprise, thgreby scoring a capital try. The goal kick failed. From this Liverpool appeared wel beaten. A strong forward attack gave the visiting back plenty of work. Pearce got the ball away, and kicking on, Houghton picked up and raced to Melly, then throw- ing wide to Morris, the latter romped over again. Still the point was not improved. During the remaining few minutes of play Morris scored again, which brought an interesting and keenly contested game to a close with the result—Runcorn, 1 goal 5 tries: Liverpool, noth- ing,. RUNCORN RECREATION T. BIRKENHEAD WANDERERS "A " 'num.—This matth was played at Runcprn on Saturday. 'The Recreation team were short of Dixon and Basnett, backs ;:but able substitutes were found in E. Speakman and Harry Cousins. The Wanderers placed a very strong team in, the field, including Herbert (half back) and Ingham (forward) of their first team, and a new threequarter from South Wales. Whitney kicked off at 3.30 for the home men, and a good return being made play at once became very interesting. Both sides put in some excelleat passing. The Recreation held the advantage for a time, Pendlebury and Whitney almost getting over. The visitors by the aid of Herbert re- moved operations to the centre, and during the remain- der of the first half play was very evenly contested. When half-time was called neither side had scorea. On re-starting the Wanderers were very prominent, andlgave the home men some trouble, until Cousins put in a neat kick to touch.which was supplemented by a fine run to the visitors' line by Hughes. From this point the home side held the upper hand, and but for the fine saving tactics of the Wanderers' half-backs, the home men would have scored cos several occasions. Jonesgot over after a short run, hut was called back for having, been in touch, and a moment later another player of the Recs. was pushed into touch in goal. Not to be denied the home team made another strong attack on the visitors' line, and from a scrimmage Simpson got over near the corner. Dunbavand took the place kick, and put the splendidly over the bar from the touch lino.. Imme- diately after, time was declared, leaving the Recreation team winners by one goal and two minors to Birkenhead Wanderers nil.</p>
        <p>Sr. HELENS RECREATION V. STOCEMI.T..—PIayed at Stockport. Fildes and Cheetharn were absent from the visiting team, and Stockport were without Potts and Saville. The home team kicked off, Harper returning to touch at half-way. Molyneux dribbled well to the Stockport 25, and from a loose scrimmage Seddon obtained possession and attempted a drop, but only a minor resulted. Gill made a very good run, and from the succeeding scrimmage a splendid burst of passing by the Recs. was witnessed. A good dribble by Chapman and Gill brought the ball to the Stockport 25. A nice bit of passing by Stockport ensued hereabouts, and then from a scrimmage Gill raced away and passed to Dolan, who scored near touch, Seddon failing to improve. A series ot brilliant exhibitions of passing by both teams was then witnessed, which left play in neutral territory, nothing further beingiscored up to the interval. Half- time score :—Recs. 1 try, Stockport nil. After the re- start Bailey got a try for Stockport, and though each side triad hard to score a winning peint, the match ended in a draw of 1 try each. ST. HELENS V. BLACKLEY.—This match took place at St. Helens, before about 3,000 spectators. Blackley had a very poor team, being short of the two Oswalds, Cooper, Hilton, Isherwood, and Jones. Cross started, and play was taken to the visitors' lines. From a pass by Cross,Appleton scored under the posts, Cross kicking a goal. Considering their handicapped state, the visi- tors played well. Brownbill got over for the Saints, Cross again landing a goal. A mull by 'Trainor allowed Ashton to get away, and when collared on the line by Foulkes he passed to Radford, who scored,Cook convert- ing. This reverse was a surprise to the Saints, who had apparently held their opponents very cheap. Half time score :--St. Helens 2 goals, Blackley 1 goal. St. Helens played better in the second half. Final result t—St. Helens 3 goals, 2 tries, Blackley 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>LIVERPOOL JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN A TEAM v. MAGAZINES.—The return engage- ment between these teams took place at Seacombe on Saturday ,i n splendid weather,and before a fair attendanCe of spectators. It will be remembered that the first meet- ing at Runcorn, on October 14th, was a very close thing, ay: home team just winning on the call of time by one point after a very sensational game. In view of this the Magazine,s fully anticipated lowering the champions' colours, and had whipped together a thoroughiy repre- sentative team. Runcorn had not their strongest com- bination,the following composing the team ,—Lightfoot, back ; W. Gayter, liickson, and Gregory, threequarter backs ; Hollingsworth and Woodward, half backs ; W. Evans, Clarke. Moores, Jolley, J. Pendlebury, Walker, and Smith, forwards. Referee, Mr. R. Wilcock. H. Evans started on behalf of Runcorn, Fletcher making a good return. The game for a few minutes was waged at centre. Runcorn soon became aggressive, the for- wards pushing the home contingent back on their lines. Here a hot attack was kept up, and passing between Woodward and Hollingaworth resulted in the latter get- ting over, but on appeal the try was disallowed. How- ever, the same player soon had his revenge, and suc- ceeded in scoring a meritrrious try, which Lightfoot failed to improve. Following this the home team played up, Hughes and Booth each making good indivi- dual efforts. But the defence of Lightfoot was extremely good,and by judicious kicking again forced the "Mags," to defend,which they did stubbornly,for although Run- corn pressed continually they failed to break through, and half time arrived with the score Runcorn one try, Magazines nil. The second half was very uninteresting and monotonous, too much scrimmaging being indulged in. Runcorn experienced hard lines in not scoring on many occasions. However, after heavy pressure Jolley by a strong effort got over, but failed to augment the point, and when time was called Runcorn had scored their ninth victory in the above competition by six points to nil. A glance at the doings of the A team may perhaps prove interesting to the supporters of VI, club, their record is somewhat unique. Matches played 19,w0n 16,and drawn 3. They have scored 21 goals and 42 tries, against 1 goal 6 tries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>BASEBALL ASSOCIATION RESERVE V. POST OFFICE.— Played on the ground of the former on Saturday, and resulted in a win for the home team. Baseball, 2 gals Post Office, 1 goal. The visitors had a strong team, while the Baseball were minus the services of Bate and Jones, but found good substitutes in Calfilwell and Tas•lor. Storey and Gallop played a good game for the winners.</p>
        <p>ORFO RD CRESCENT V. FARNWORTTI C.E T.S.—Played at Orford on Saturday. Farnworth started the game. Nithing was scared during the first half, but during the second half the Crescent forwards played with more combination, the result being a good shot from Lawton which rroved to be the only point of the game, The Farnworth front rank played a splendid game through- out, but the home backs were always on the alert and gave no chances. Result :—Orford Czescent, 1 goal ; Farnworth C.E. 1.5., nil.</p>
        <p>WA RRINGTON RESERVE V. PADGATE INSTITUTE. —This match was played on Warrington's ground on Saturday, the visitors being assisted by Clayton and Smith, of St. Elphin's. A strong wind blew towards the railway goal, which the homesters defended in the first half, Padgate did a deal of pressing for some time, but the home t-acks played a souNcl game. At length the for- wards were enabled to break away, and Mather drew first blood. Then the visitors went away with a rust), and from a bully in front of goal, the ball was kicked through. Shortly before the interval, Mather gave his side the lead. The homesters did all the pressing in the second half and fairly outclassed their opponents. Five more go)ls were added by Mather (two), Reid,Wilkes,and Ogden, and the game c'osed with the score—Warring- ton, seven goals ; Padgate, one.</p>
        <p>PENFETH A F.C. v. EARL ROVERS.—The first meeting of these clubs came off on the Rovers' ground, Newsham Park, on Saturday. Dumbill kicked off for the visitors against the wind, and play remained even for the first twenty minutes, each goal being visited in turn. After this the visitors began to press and N. Parson scored from a scrummage in front of goal. The home right made headway to the visitors' territory, where Bowyer was called upon, but cleared at the expense of a futile corner. The interval' arrived with the visitors leading by one goal to nil. On re-starting the visitors were soon on the aggressive, the home custodian making some splendid saves by "fisting" out grandly. N. Parson scored a second goal for the visitors. The home forwards now showed some " tricky" combined play, but found the defence of Elliot and Lewis too strong to break through. A pass from Dunsbill enabled W. Parson to score a third goal. The home forwards again showed their " tricky " play, but good work by Dykes and Whitfield placed the visitors again on the aggressive. N. Parson scored from a pass by his brother. The homesters now troubled Bowyer,but with a huge kick he cleared into midfield. The rest of the game was in the Rovers' territory, they being kept on the defence. IV. Parson, Boswell, and Bowyer each scored goals. When time was called the score stood—Penketh 7 goals, Earl Rovers nil.</p>
        <p>A woman named Knorr wag executed in Melbourne, on Monday. She was a baby-farmer, and had murdered several infants c.mmitted to her charge. On the way to the scaffold she joined heartily in the singing of hymns.</p>
        <p>SHE USES THE RIGHT SOAP. —lltalSOH'S Snap saves her clothes. Anything washed with Hudson's Soap is thoroughly W shed therefore remains!rnuch longer clean.—A 'pure Dry Saap in fine powder.— -Advt. (1</p>
        <p>Berlin telegrams state that the Nihilists in Russia are very active, and that the authorities have been corn- plied to take extraordinary measures in order to defeat their murderous des4;ns.</p>
        <p>RAPID CURES of Severe Cough and Asthma by Dr. Loarcic's Pulmonic Wafers.—From Mr.liarrison, 265, Glossop rout Sheffi.ld,—" Many customers Imre expressed them- sel% es highly gmt' fied with the beneficial results of Dr. Locock's Ws.,ers in Asthma, Coughs, Ize. I was myself troubled with a sev,re cough, and was completely cured by a few Wgfers," They instantly relieve and rapidly cure Asthma. Consumption, Coughs, Colds, Bronchitis, Itheu- m.tism, and all Nervous complaints. and taste pleasantly, Sold by all druggists at le. 11d. and 2s. 9d, per box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WIDNES SCHOOL 130 A al).</head>
        <p>WIDNES SCHOOL 130 A al).</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall, on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. H. Wade-Deacon pre- ,ided and there were present the Rev. J. Clark,Rev• Dean Finegan, Messrs. C. E. Barlow, S. Quinn, B. Brown, J. W . Wareing,lt. Shaw, D. Lewis, C. Flynn, and G. H. Denby (clerk.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>DISTRICT EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee reperted that during December two free places were granted. 126 parents were summoned before the Committee for contravention of the Educa- tion Act, and six were prosecuted before the magistrates and convicted. Dean Finegan pointed out that the aver- age attendance was low at present, and he urged that more stern measures should be adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>DEARTFI OF TEACHER 3,</head>
        <p>DEARTFI OF TEACHER 3,</p>
        <p>_ The School Management Committe recommended that Miss C.E Rathbone be engaged as a certificated assistant mistress in Simm's Cross Schools at a salary of £6s,rais- ing according to the scale of the Bbard.—Dean Finegan pointed out that the vacancy now existed, and Miss Rathbone would not be at liberty until July.—Mr. Bar- low said if the Board did not appoint a teacher now,per- haps they might not get one at all.—The Clerk paid he had spent £6 of £7 advertising for three tw-inths for a certificated assistant mistress,and he had not had a single application. The Committee authorised him to appoint the first suitable candidate. Miss Rathbone would not leave college until June. —Mr. Wareing said the dearth of applications might be because the emoluments were not sufficiently tempting. If they had to wait six months before anyone would accept the Board's terms they were going to be in a curious position.—The Chairman said Mr. Wareing had perhaps hit :he right explana- tion, and the Committee ought to look into the matter. Tae appointment was made..</p>
        <p>MR GOVERNMENT GRANTS.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee reported that during the past year the Government grants earned at Simms Cross Schools had averaged 193. 7-4., an increase of 11,1., on the previous year ; at Warrington-road School 195.511.,5ame as last year; at West Bank School 10s.101 an increase of sd. the total grant earned was £2,932 163. as compared with £8,893 93. for 1892. The average grant per head was 203. 21d., an increase of al. over the previous year. The average for England and Wales in 1892 was 19s. 3td. Out of 53 pupil teachers and can- didates presented for examination,only two failed. There were 405 pupils examined in cookery, and the grant earned was £Bl. In 1892 there were 368 pupils examined and the grant amounted to £73 12s. The grants earned at the Science and Art examinations amounted to £195 195., a decrease of /' Is. compared with 1392.—The re- port was considered highly satisfactory and on the motion of the Chairman the Committee was thanked for its preparation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>_EVENING CONTINUATION SCHOOL. REGISTERS.</head>
        <p>_EVENING CONTINUATION SCHOOL. REGISTERS.</p>
        <p>On the recommendation of the School Management Committee it was decided to support the petition of the Bradford School Board to the Education Department with regard to the marking of registers in the Evening Continuation Schools. The petition pointed out that the present method of checking the attendance for each subject was a serious hindrance to teaching, and advo- cated that each student should not be marked not more than once during the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>TEE COST OF TILICNING.</head>
        <p>TEE COST OF TILICNING.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee presented a series of tabu- lated statements,showing that the gross cost of teaching in the Board's schools was £6,023 /2s. 6d., or £2 ls.sid. per head. The total receipts ailiounted to £5,093 175.8,1., allowing a net cost of teaching £929 14s. 10d , being a net cost per head of 65.41 d. The amount received from the rates was 6s. 9d. per scholar, and in 20 large towns there was only one received a less ameunt,and that was Hull, where the amount received from the rates was only ss. 2il. per scholar. But Hull only gained grants of 17s. 11d, per scholar, whereas Widnes gained £1 is. 11d. Widnes received Is. 7d. more from the rates and gained 4s. per head more from grants.—Mr. Flynn moved the adoption of the report.—The Chairman said it was very satisfactory, and said while the average amount received frora the rates had iner,ased in Eng- land, in Widnes it had decreased from 6s. 11d. to 63.9 d. While the total expenditure per scholar in England was £2 Bs. 4d. in Widnes it was only £2 2s. 6d.—The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ESTIMATES,I</head>
        <p>THE ESTIMATES,I</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee presented the estimates for the year ending September 29th next, as follows . School Management Committee, £1,396; District Educa- tion Committee, £400; Sites and Building Committee, £l,OBO ; Finance Committee, £2,155; total £5,031.-1t was recommended that a precept be served upon the Corporation for the sum of £b,ooo, payable on or before the first March next.—After some discussion the recom- mendation was passed. •</p>
        <p>THE TIMM'S FUND.</p>
        <p>Ort the recommendation of the Finance Committee, it was resolved that in accordance with the terms of the " Timmis Fund," grants of 153. per month be made to Mr. John Pile, 95, Halton View, and Mr. John Baldwin, of 6, Chester-street, whose sons have just obtained free scholarships at the Farnworth Grammar</p>
        <p>EDUCATION OF THE BLIND. A circular with respect to the education of the blind was referred ta the District Education Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>LIBERAL SOIREE AT ST. HELENS. THE LIBERAL CANDIDATURE FUR ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL SOIREE AT ST. HELENS. THE LIBERAL CANDIDATURE FUR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Councillor W, Woods, J.P., ex-Mayor of Wigan, who has been fixed upon as the next Liberal candi- date for the county-borough of St Helens, was present at the tea party, concert, and ball held under the auspices of the St. Helens Liberal Asso- ciation, and the St. Helens Women's Liberal Asso- ciation, in the Volunteer Hall, St. Helens, on Wed- nesday night. Over 400 persons partook of a substan- tial tea, the tables being presided over by ladies con- nected with the associations. Mr. Woods, who had been entertained by the officials of the association at the Fleece Hotel,was received in the Armoury at the hall by a lerge number of gentlemen,who accorded him a hearty welcome. The chair was afterwards taken by Councillor J. Forster, J.P., Chairman of the Executive Committee, and he was supported by Mr. Woods ant Councillor J. Wilson (Wigan), Councillors El. Martin, .J.P., (treasurer of the Association), J. P. Mearns, (lion. sec.), Jas. Burchall, J.P., J. Collins, T. M`Lachlan, I. B. Rose, J.B. Leach, F. W. Leach, and J. A. Chadwick. Mcssrs. J. Chadwick, J.P., G. W. Rawlins. C. Sharpies, J.P., M.J. Connell, 8.A., S. Glover, B. A. Dromgooie, J.P., J. Mor- ton, A. Muir, W. Webster, E. L. Edwards, NV. Seath, W. Shields, R. Shaw, J. Shields, R. Marsh, J. Bart, D.Chad- wick. J.P., J. Povey, Connor, T. Jenkinsen, H. Moxon, W. Fletcher, F. J. Fletcher, G. Hexvit',. son., W. Bur- rows, H. Chesterton, G. Hewitt, jun., H. Bardsley, and J.Bain (association secretary). A letter of apology was nad from Alderman Cook, president of the assooiation, who had been called to Bradford on business. The Chairman said they in St. Helens had not much to complain of in their position. they were better off than they had been before. The promises they made at the last election were being fulfillt'd pretty rapidly, and the prophecies they made as to the other side were also being fulfilled in a fashion which would not go down with the people of St. Helens. In the House of Commons there had been 482 divisions on Goverment measures. The member for St. Helens had voted once and missed twice. mat was the way he had been going on all the time he had represented St. Helens. At the last election certain questions were propounded by the Miners' Federation. One was with regard to the Employers' Liability Bill being made compulsory, and the member for St. Helens promised to support a measure of that sort. Had he done so. (" No, no ") ? Only three weeks ago he voted in the lobby for the permissive power of contracting out. The miners would rather have no bill than a bill of that sort (applause.) The G-overrnent were doing first rate, and the member for St. Helens was do- ing very bad, so, taking the two things together, they were in a very happy condition. (Applause.) The Chairman having introduced Mr. Woods as a friend of the working man and a labour champion of the very first order. lfr. Woods, on rising, was received with considerable cheering. He said he would call them Radicals, as he was sure they would -prefer to be called by that name rather than by Whig or Tm.y, or any other milk-and- water term. Although president of the Wigan Liberal Association, he was more a labour man in politics than anything else, He was described as such by the Tory Press in Wigan a year and a half ago, when he stood for St. Andrew's Ward, and since he had been in the Wigan , Council-chamber he had done his best on behalf of the labour party who returned him,and in conjunction with the leaders of the Liberal party who were already there , in pressing forward such measures and in urging such referms as he thought would be for the benefit of the working classes. He (Mr. Woods) used to belong to the Tory Democratic party, who called themselves the friends of the people, but lie found their friendship consisted of nothing but lip service. When he found they olajected to colliers and others in dangerous employments having an eight-hours day by Art of Parliament, he resigned the Mayoralty of his native town, and, throwing in his lot with the labour party, assisted to return his namesake Mr. Sam Woods to Parliament for Ince. (Loud applause.) What did the great Tory party do then ? They ejected him from their ranks, because he had ventered to co in for an eight hours' day for colliers and others. He cared not for that, and had been very happy since he had been outside their ranks. (Laughter and loud applause.) Mr. Woods then proceeded to comment upon some mea- sures brought in by the Liberal Government. He approved of the Act of Mr. H. H. Fowler, who, by a stroke of the pen, had reduced the qualification neces- sary for a man to become a guardian from a rateable value of £3O, £4O, and in some cases £5O to £5 a year. He urged the Liberals of St. Helens to force their mem- ber, whoever he might be, to vote for the abolition of the plural or property vote, and also to insist upon vot- ing by ballot for boards of guardians as wellas for other public bedies. He was glad to find that working men magistrates had been appointed, and that in St. Helens his friend Mr. Tom Glover had been appointed as a representative of the colliers. In the county the matter was somewhat different. The Hon. T. W. Legh, M.P. for Newton, had said that the Lord Lieutenant should</p>
        <p>.•</p>
        <p>I look out for men of good social position. To his (air. 1 Woods's) mind it seemed an extraordinary thing that a workingman could deal with offences in a borough like St. Helens, and yet could not deal with offences in the county, which even the Tory party would not say were different. He hoped to see some of those anomalies removed, and to see equal rights to all classes of the community, and that the county bench should not remain a private social preserve for the wealthy classes. (Applause.) There was another very important matter —the question of one man one vote—which was contin- ually crcpping up. It cropped up at Iforncastle where there were 900 out voters. He was glad to find that the committee appointed by Mr. Asquith to inquire into the condition of the chemical workers were unanimous in recommending an eight hours day. (Applause.) There was one subject on which he entirely disagreed with the policy of the Radicals. That was the policy of continu- ing the grant of £lO,OOO a year to the Duke of Edinburgh now that he had become a foreign Sovereign. (Hear,hea.r. ) The Tory party had obstructed all the measures brought f Tward, and the representative of St. Helens was a m-tnber of that p irty which by obstructionfhad prevented those measures from becoming law.. The electors of the country had the power in their hands, thanks to the extension of the franchise.. Their harvest was ripe, but it lay rotting on the ground. They had only in their might and power to stretch forth their right hand and gather in by returning a man to Parliament who would represent their ideas, and they ceuld determine whether their views, their interests, and their bills should be slighted and amended to destruction,, or perhaps vetoed altogether by 600 irresponsible peers, who represented not the powers of this country, but themselves. They, the electors of St. Helens could determine whether their member should be standing in that crowd, should be a member of that band, who, when the question of the House of Lords came to be discussed', should join in the shout, and raise the cry, " Cut them down, away with thern,why cumber they the ground." (Applause.) Mr. Councillor Martin proposed a vote of thanks to the ladies who had presided at the tables, to the concert party, and to Mr. Woods for his address. Mr. Connell seconded,Councillor Wilson (Wigan) sup- ported, and it was carried unanimously. Mr. Woods replied, and proposed a vote of thanks to the chairman, which was also carried.</p>
        <p>An excellent programme was rendered by the Orpheus Glee Party, consisting of Messrs, Walter Dennett, George Barton, jun., Alexander Woods,, and F. Dennett, and dancing was kept up to a lake hour. Mr. T. Boardman's Quadrille Band occupying the orchestra. A Wigan correspondent writes :—Mr. W. Woods, now the probable Liberal candidate for St. Helens, is the son of the late Mr. H. Woods, who represented Wigan in Parliament during 1868, He is a wealthy millowner, and largely interested in the coalfield of Wigan. He first became associated with the public life of the town in 1290, when he was elected by the Conservative party as Mayor. During his year of office he had a banqueting- room erected adjoining his residence at Whitley Hall, and hospitably entertained the leading bodies of the town. /n 1892 he resigned his office of Mayor, enclosing a cheque with his resignation in payment of the penalty prescribed by law when the civic chair is vacated on personal grounds. He contented that he could not con- tinue to serve in the office whilst having convictions which differed from the principals of the party by whom he had been elected. When he severed his connection with the Conservative party it was anticipated that he was about to seek Parliamentery election in the place of Mr. Thomas Aspinwall, the adopted labiur candidato This,.however, was only a rumour. Since that time he has been warmly interested in the progress of Liberalism in the town, and now represents St. Andrew's Ward as a labour candidate. On many occasions he has been prominent in the Council chamberin connection with the expenditure incurred in banquets connected with public functions, advocating on one occasion that those who attended should pay their own expenses. When the coal crisis was on he was thefirst subscriber to the relief of the distressed, sending £5OO for distribution in Wigan and district along with St. Helens. Mrs. Woods is a lady with many accomplishments, a fluent speaker, and enthusiastic in the Liberal cause. Mr. Woods has two residences—lVarnford Park, Hampshire, and Whitley Hall, Wigan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ST. LUXE'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>- --- The annual tea meeting and concert of the above church was held on Monday evening. There was a cap- ital attendance. After tea the chair was occhpied by Mr. H. B. White, who said he was pleased to see Bank Quay so well represented. It was some years since he had presided over their gathering, and he did so that evening with great pleasure. He could congratulate them on having a school separate from the church. (Ap- plause.) A miscellaneous entertainment was then pro- vided, an enjoyable feature being a circle and sketch given by the White Star Minstrels. During an interval in the programme, the Rev.E. C.E. Carleton, vicar of Holy Trinity Church, expressed the pleasure it afforded him to see such a larva gathering. He referred to the improvements which had been effected in the parish during the year, and said that he estimated the amount spent on both churches while he had been in Warring- ton at not less than £16,000. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE. The Rey. J. Yonge presided at the P.S.A. meeting in Wycliffe Church on Sunday afternoon. In the course of a short address he referred to a letter he had received from a body of workingmen, expressing their apprecia: tion of the work he was enabled to do in relation to the recent distress in the town. Ile was very thankful for that vote of thanks. Whatever work he did in connec- tion with the recent distress it was a very great pleasure for him to render. But he hoped the other ministers would get a similar recognition, as there were others who worked quite as hard as himself, and he hoped they would be treated alike. He (the chairman) hai a very strong feeling that that P.S.A. brotherhood of men might bring about a reform in the direction of pure speech in the town. Mr. Yonge mentioned that since the P.S.A. was started he had been told there was a very marked improvement in that respect in one of the work- shops of Warrington. The Rev. W. H. Drummond was the speaker for the afternoon, and he gave a short account of the life and a ork of the Italian patriot, Jos. Mazzini, author of "For God and the people, being the religious creed of a democrat," a laook he advised his hearers to purchase. The address was a very interesting. and instructive one, and wL s listened to with close atten- tion. Mrs. R. H. Pearson was the soloist and she sang " Waiting for the King" in a praiseworthy manner. Mr. T. Wallington and Mr. H. Barker also took part in the service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>"SATAN'S SLAVE."</head>
        <p>"SATAN'S SLAVE."</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening, at Wycliffe Congregational Church, the pastor, the Rev. John Yonge, preached on "Satan's Slave." Be said some no doubt would recog- nize the title of this disc,iurse as being also the title of a stage play that had been recently performed in one of the theatres of the town. He did not go to theatres, either here or anywhere else, and so he had no know- ledge at all of the particular drama that bore the above title. But he was struck by the title. It seemed to be in itself a sermon. If possible, he was even more struck by thr fact that nearly all the bills which he saw announcing the performance of "Satan's Slave" were in public-house windows. That title, " Satan's Slave," staring at the passers-by from places where often, and for many men with only too much literal truth Satan's Seat was, fastened his attention and compelled his thought. He had wondered somewhat whether the unusual title had aroused the thought of any of those whom it described ; and whether as he had made hia habitual visit to the public-house the slave of drink had recognized his true description on the play-bill in the window ; whether also any spectator of the play had seen himself portrayed therein. It was ' possible, no doubt, for the drama to both teach and preach even the sternest and noblest truths. But it was not often that a sermon appealed to men from a public-house window Mr. Yonge went on to speak of the slavery of drink, and urged all who were beginning to be conscious that drink had fascination to totally abstain. None of them, he supposed, would question the aptness of the title as applied to the sensualist, the man who could not govern his animal passions, but who was ever lusting to satisfy them, and who cared not how much evil and wrong he ipflicted upon others lives, so long as his lust was ministered to. He advised them to beware of the slavery of lust. Mr. Yonge then dwelt on men with ungovernable tempers, profane men, envious and uncharitable, avaricious, covetous, miserly men as being satan's slaves. These men would not enter the heavenly kingdom with these evils hugged in their heart, and he asked them to consider that unquestionaVe truth. These slaveries must be abolished and abandoned. There wai only one power mighty enough to burst and destroy these bonds. Satan was stronger than they. Christ Jesus alone could set them free, and keep them free.</p>
        <p>Replying to an inquiry from the working clothiers Mr. Campbell-Bannerman says the eight-hour &amp;Ay is under consideration, and it is hoped it may be found Possible to reduce the hours of labour at the Army Clothing Factory, Pimlico.</p>
        <p>This choir finished their week's visit to Earles- town on Saturday evening. A large audience assembled on Friday evening and fully enjoyed the bill of fare placed before them, Madame Buxton proving herself a capable elocutionist in her des- criplions of the dioramic pictures. On Saturday afternoon an entertainment was announced for school children, at a cheap rate, but such a large crowd gathered, that fully one half were unable to obtain admittance, and it was found necessary to give a second entertainment to satisfy those who were disappointed. The children appeared greatly pleased with the entertainment, Professor Fix, with his ventriloquial sketches, securing a goon share of their appreciation, while Miss Kitty Wood- ford amused them with the song" Daddy wouldn't buy me a pow-wow." In the evening the hall wa, again packed with an appreciative audience, who heartily applauded the various items submitted. The visit has been in every way successful, and return may not be far distant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PERFECT C,</head>
        <p>A PERFECT C,</p>
