        <p>THE COAL TRADE.</p>
        <p>MEETING OF THE CONCILIATION BOARD. On Monday, the second meeting of the Coal Trade s Conciliation Board appointed in pursuance of the Rosebury Conference to settle the question of miners' wages was herd at the Westminster Pahce Hotel, Mr. A. M. Chambers, of the Coalowners' Federation, presid- ing. There were fourteen representatives of the coal• - owners present, and thirteen of the miners, Mr. Pickard, M.P., being unable to be present, on account of ill-health.. The conference, which sat with closed doors, lasted from half past one to half past three, and then from four to half past five. The following official report was then handed to the representatives of the press :—"The pro- ' cedure rules were partly discussed, and at half past five • o'clock the meeting was adjourned until Tuesday the 13th February, at 1 30." Preliminary meetings were held by the representatives on the Conciliation Board of the coalowners and miners respectively. The names of the Federated Coalowners were as follows :—Lancashire and Cheshire: Mr. Alfred Hewlett, Mr. Richard Pilking- • ton, Mr. W. Kellett, Mr. William Johnson. Smith Yrirk- shire : Mr. A. M. Chambers and Mr. David Davey. West' Yorkshire: Mr. A. Currer Briggs and Mr. W. D..CIBIe. Derbyshire: Mr. M. Deacon and Mr. Arthur G. Barnes. Notts: Mr. Walter Salmond. Cannock Chase: Captain Harrison. North Wales : Mr. Henry Dennis. Glouces- tershire and Somersetshire : Mr. J. Ramey; Mr. T. Rad- cliffe Ellis, Secretary. The following represented the Miners' Federation :—Mr. Sam Woods, M.P., vice-presi- dent ; Mr. T. Ashton, secretary ; 'Mr. Enoch Edwards, treasurer. Yorkshire: Mr. Edward Cowey, Mr. William ' Parrott, Mr. John Frith. Lancashire: . Mr. Thomas Aspinwall, Mr. Robert Isherwood. Derbyshire: Mr. James Haslam, Mr. W. E. Harvey, Cannock Chase: Mr. Albert Stanley. Notts: Mr. William Bailey. Leices- tershire : Mr. T. Chambew. Somersetshire : Mr. S. H. Whitehouse.</p>
        <p>A correspondent states :—The special business of the • meeting was toagree upcn the rules for tbe future government of the Board. Since the previous meeting, both organisations had submitted draft copies of rules,. and private meetings of the coalowners' representatives and the miners' representatives were held. The miners' draft rules contained a clause fixing the minimum wage. at 30 per cent, above the standard wage of January, 1883 ; but the owners' rules were framed so as to leave the Board absolutely free and unfettered in dealing with, wage questions. At the meeting yesterday the formal rules for the constitution of the Board were considered and agreed to. The Miners' Federation proposal for-the fixing of a minimum wage at 30 per. cent, above the standard of 1888 was submitted and provoked much dis- cussion. The owners contended that the basis of agree- ment at the Rosehery Conference was the formation of a Board with absolute power to determine future wages. It was not found possible, even after prolonged discus- sion, to arrive at any decision upon the crucial question of a minimum wage, and it was nitimately resolved to refer the question for the consideration of the full Board at the next meeting on Tuesday the 13th of February.. It is expeeted that before that meeting the Speaker of the House of Commons will have intimated to the res- pective secretaries of the two organisations of the name of the gentleman whom he has nominated to act as in- dependent chairman of the Board. It was rumoured yesterday that the Speaker had already approached one gentleman with a view of acting as chairman, but that he had declined to accept the delicate and difficult posi- tion. The mcre probable explanation of the delay which has occurred in the nomination of an independent chairman for the Conciliation Board is the recent indis- position of the Speaker. The Speaker of the House of Commons has, it is stated decided not to appoint the chairman of the Coal Trade Board of Conciliation till he returns to London for the re-opening of the sessi 3n.</p>
