      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WARRINGToN INFiltlialtY ANNUAL</head>
        <p>WARRINGToN INFiltlialtY ANNUAL</p>
        <p>BALL. • On Tuesday evening, the annual ball in aid of the funds of this institution, was held in the Assembly Room at the Lion Hotel. The attendance, owing to absence from home and family bereavements, was somewhat smaller than usualt The decoration and furnishing of the room was carried out by Messrs. S. E. and R. Johnson. An invention of the firm in the shape of an attractive portable " eosy corner" was much admired. It may be used for a variety of purposes, including a single or double screen, a window seat and a centre seat, while the panels of the screen are suitable for the the introduction of photographs or miniature landscapes. The crush room is a very desirable addition to the ballroom, and it proved exceedingly useful during the evening. Two daintily furnished drawings were provided. The music was supplied by Mr. R. H. Pearson's band, the programme being as follows " All in a Garden Fair ''; Waltz, " Adoration " ; waltz, "Sur la plage ; polka, "Tambourins " : waltz, "Loin du Bal "; lancers, " Santa Claus "; waltz," Amour et Printemps " ; waltz, " After the Ball " ; waltz, " Soldaten Lieder " ; polka, " Marguerite" ; lancers, " Round the Town ; "waltz, " Bavadere" waltz ; "Weiner Blot" ; waltz, "Rosen ans dem .Suden ;" polka, ." See me Dance ;" lancers, "Oh ! Mr Porter :" waltz,"Golconda ;"waltz," Liebenestrue ;" waltz, "Dans tes yeuz ;" waltz," Garrison Ball ;" gale!), " Post Horn."</p>
        <p>The following gentlemen had been appointed stewards: —Captain E. H. Molyneux Seel, Mr. Henry Greenall, Major Pickmere, Major Nicholson, Captain Withington, Mr. John. White, and Mr. Harrison Tinsley, the secre- tarial duties being again most, efficiently discharged by Mr. E. Gaskell Davies. The supper was an excellent one. It was served in the billiard room. The tables were most elaborately decorated. The caterers were Mr. and Mrs. Taylor, the manager and manageress of the hotel.</p>
        <p>'lhe following is a list of those present : Appleton, Mr FM Long, 14r and Mrs W Bolt ,n, Mrs G Y Long, Miss Bolton. Miss Dorothy Lewis, Miss Whyndharn BI sine. Mrs (TarporleY) Ludlow, ldr P Blaine, Miss (Tsrporley) Litton, Miss Gertrude Blaine. Mr W (Tarporley) Litton, Miss Amy Barker, Mr H G Melville, Mr Stanley Broadbent, Miss Melville. Miss Broadbent, Miss M S Mortimer. Mr H Percy Broadbent. Mr W Molvneux Seel. Captain E H Broadbent, Mr John W (King's Regiment) Broadbent, Mr C 11 NeGon. Miss Be,resford, Mr Nelson, Miss P Brabazon, Mrs Nisholson, Major Brabszon, Mlss Powles Mr A. A Bleckly. Mr A S Raigate, Miss (Suffolk) Bii-ltby. Mr H Reynolds, Mrs Camden, Mrs Roynolds, Mr Guy (sth Dra- Coombe. Miss goon Guards) Coombe, Miss K Reynolds, Miss Disraeli, Mr Coningsby, M.P. Reynolds, Miss J Davies, Mrs Vera B Stubs, Miss Davies, Mr Vera B l• ilverthorne, Captain Davies, Mr E Gaskell Silverthorne, Mrs Davies, Mr Leonard G Tilney, Mr Deusham. Mr H P Tinsley, Mr Hugh Dun. Mr R 11 Tinsley, Miss Davies, Miss B Tinsley, Mr James, jun. Eccles. Mr H Tinsley, Mr Harrison Edelsten, Mr J A Tinsley, Mrs Harrison Edclsten. Mrs Thomas, Miss Evanson, Captain Vincent, Miss GE Fowler, Ca..tain V A Waters, Mr Graves, Mr Herbert Williams. Vlr J Graves, Mrs Wynne-Jones, Mr R I Gage Mr WvnriP-J nes Mrs R I Gardner, Mr E Whitley, Mr J J Gardner, Miss Wrigley. Miss Greener, Mr W B Wrigley, Miss R Gill, Mr if Wilson. Miss Violet Gill, Mr W Williamson, Miss liingstan. Mr A A (York) Williamson, Mr Johnson-Ferguson, Mr, M.P. White, Mr John Johnson-Ferguson, Mrs, and White, Mrs Jolla party Wright, Mr D Logan Wright. Mrs D Logan</p>
        <p>The Medical Annual advise, Practitioners to remember that when recommending Cocois, as food and beverage I°'r invalids, the name CADBURY WA variy. micwi, of Cocoa ja gu4ranteec4 puity.—Advt.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE NEW TAYLOR FA.</p>
        <p>The tender of Mr. Josep Ellison for the erection of a ,shelter-house in the new Taylor r.trk, at a cost of £75 was accepted en Wednesday by the Parks Corynnittea of the St. Helens Corporation.</p>
        <p>SEQUEL TO THE COAL STRIKE. At St. Helens on Monday, the magistrates had a list ef 130 cases in which working people principally were summoned for non-payment of rates. niny of them were residents of the colliery districts round the borough, •and the non-payment of the amounts due appears to • have risen during the coal strike.</p>
        <p>SEIZURE OF BAD MEAT. • 'A,large quantity of tad meat was seized by. Inspector 'Smirthwaite, at Si. Helens, on 'Thursday morning, and being taken to the Town Hall, was condemned by the medical officers of health, and a magisterial order for its destruction was obtained. The meat was dressed n ready for sale. ST. HELENS FOOTBALL CLUB. Mr. T. Shdlow, of the St. Helens Football Club, made an application to the St. aelens magistrates on Thurs- day., tor permission for Mr. Liptrott to sell intoxicating liquor:en the. Knowsley-road ground from 2.30 to 4.30 on Saturd -v, when Lancaster will be the visitors. Permis- sion was granted. RESULTS OF THE COAL STRIKE. In consequence of the very serious depression in trade during the coal strike, the St. Helens Café and atecreation Company, which usually pays a satisfactory dividend, will not be able to pay a dividend for the past half-year.. It is feared that other local companies will be in a similar position. DREADFUL DEATH OF A YOUTH. .Henrf Vincent Dewar, aged 15, son of the late Peter :Dewar, tobacconist, of Bridge-st., S. Helens, was found lying dead in a vat of boiling liquor at Hardsbaw Brook ' Chemical. Works,St.Flelens,of the United Alkali Company,, • on Thursday morning. Deceased was learning chemistry. one saw the accident,but it is supposed he was goring '.llp a ladder, and by some means lost his footing and fell in. He could only have been in a few minutes when • discovered. The greatest sympathy is felt for the Widowed mother and family. CEMETERY CHARGES. At the meeting of the Cemetery Committee of the St. Helens Corporation on Wednesday, Councillor Hatton • complained of the irregularity in the cemetery charges. —Councillor Johnson, of Thatto Heath, said the Church of England .lees were higher than the Nonconformist fees.—The Town-clerk said the Corporation had abso- lutely no control over incumbents' charges. The fault perhaps lay in the Nonconformist and Catholics charging too little.—Councillor Hatton did not think it right for ' the Town-clerk to receive half-a-crown paid on each agreement. This however, it appeared, had been sanc- tioned by the Council. ;.ANOTHER SHOCKING COLLIERY ACCIDENT NEAR PRESCOT. On Thursday morning., about eleven o'clock, a fall of , roof took place in the Wigan and Whiston Colliery Corn- pany's pit at Whiston. Two men were under the spot -at the time. One, named Webb, residing at Whiston, :received such injuries as to leave little hopes of recovery_ "The.other, who belonged to Chester-street, Present, and brother to David Jones, who was recently killed at the s'Eccleston Hall Colliery, received serious injury ta his -head, and a man named Williams, who went to their res- •cue,,had his head and feet crushed by the fall.</p>
        <p>• REC.F.MION AT TILE GYIINASIII3I. •The President of the St. Helens Young Men's Christian Association, ,(Colonel,W. W. Pilkington, J.P.), and Mrs. 'Pilkington gave a reception in the St. Helens Gymnasium 'On Thursdays night. , About 700 invitations were issued. The guests" having been received, light refreshments Were served, after which Colonel Pilkington extended a 'hearty welcome to the members. During an interval in the entertainment, which consisted of gymnastic exer- cises by members, songs, duets, and recitals, an address Was delivered by the .Rev. J. A. Mitchell, 8.A., the 'newly-elected pastor of the Congregational Church. arti.T.LWAY SERVANTS • GATHERING. The annual dinner given by local tradesmen to rail- :way ;servants connected with the passenger traffic at the .18t. Helens station. of the London and North-Western Railway Company took place at the Raven Hotel on 'Thursday night. . About seventy persons were present, 'including Mr.lßoardman (station master), Mr. T. Lace (thief -el•erk), and the station masters of the district. After dinner, Mr. Boardinan presided, and the proceed- gs incluried.several toasts, the princival b-ing "The Town sand 'Frade of St..Hel,ms "and " The Directors of e London And --.North.Western Railway Company." 't?tlgs,,recitztions, and selections of music were also</p>
        <p>Tan WG,TER. ENGINEER. • 4;:pecial,:neeting of the St. Helens Town Council, Qn nesday, .considered the recommendation of the ”• ater Committee that .the salary of the water engineer lc' be qui ,inttd shrmld commence at :.c.;41-00, and rise by 'n.9taitnents of £2O until a maximum of £5OO was neached,--CaiincillorToote proposed as an amendment "'at The salary start at £3OO and rise by £5O at the end c'attl 'two .years until 4;500 Was Keached.—Councillor hat seconded.—lt was pointed out that St. Helens , ad a lar e• geand growin water estate, and new works ilad to he , constructed.--:-Vor the amendmentCouncillors °°te• nattotti"biorgan, and Johnson, of Thatto Heath, votC d ; and for the recommendation of the committee wenty-four .voted.-4 resolution appreciative ot Mr. "askin's services for seventeen years was unanimously carried.</p>
        <p>SWIRRESTING,GATIIERING. Councfiller.s.l3.,Leach oriThursday night entertained at King's Coffee-rooms, Si;. Helens, a little eampany of roeti who usually 'hang about the station approaches, touting tor ‘4!carries:" The men mustered at site o'clock and were iterited;to take their seats at a table laden with meat Dies, sandwiches, sweetG, and plenty of tea. The donor said grace, Which,was repeated with considerable fervour, and then -they were, told to clear the plates, Nthich was do,ne•with .moro than ordinary despatch.— Mr. Leach afterwards addressed to the men somet,oniely words, and he advised them to seek regular einploynaent at the works in the town, and, thus improve their-posi- tion in or - "e• The men ecxpressed their gratitude to -Mr. Leach for his kindne-ss. A year or two since Mr. leeacti had a similar eatitering, and as a result several men, obtained and foLFowedzegular employment. A ta.tionr) !DM TiZZ LEMENT. u.At St. Helens on Monday,before Alderman Cook, Mr.,8.l Glover' and Mr. W. :Gamble, Henry William Bird, a; 'esPectably-dressed young man,or,9l3,Lyon-st. was sum-; 1.11"eli for erobenti.ng an stealing Bs. 61. the moneysi of the Provident °wiling .Club. r. J. 0. Swift prose.; tcit:tedi and Mr.J.P.Mearns defended. Mr. Swift said that: e total embezzlements .amounted to a onnsideratliel sttnething over .100. Mr. Joshua Kelly Waddilovo; Prosecutor, and carried ,on •husiness known as rovident Clottting ()lob. in April defendant wa!-..,; ;?,-a'aged as collector, and in September he appeared to. 177 e taken to drink. Se had received altogether about! fr,-,4„,"Mount,fs, and matey of these,he had entirely omitted' miumsthe weekly sheet&amp; submitted to theauperintendent, lie was only ,entitled rto go into three thP one charge, and if the bench were satisfied that athPriwo-facie case had been made out, he would ask next --e defendant be committed-to take-his trial at the quarter sessions.—The ease was dismissed. rat, AT —AGisrnArEs AND TrAIiniediaT'