      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>"NATURAL CAUSES."</head>
        <p>"NATURAL CAUSES."</p>
        <p>An enquiry was held at the Old Crow Inn, New- ton, on Tuesday morning, by Mr. S. Brighouse, Coroner, into the circumstances attending ti-e death of Patrick McGuinness, glass cutter, of 48, Gas-st., Newton, who died on Friday last, from injuries said to have been received by falling, or being pushed, out of the confectioner's shop of Mr.J. Clarke, Bridge-st. on Friday, Dec. 22nd. Mary McGuinness, widow of the deceased, said her husband was 45 years of age, and resided at 48, Gas-st., Newton. She recollected that on the Friday before Christmas Day, deceased went out about half-past seven to go to Earlestown. He had been working until six o'clock that day. He was not perfectly soberenor was he drunk, but he had had some drink. He came home again about ten o'clock, and he had had a good drop of drink. He was assisted upstairs to bed, but she did not remember him complain of having received injuries anywhere, until Saturday morning. On that morning he complained of pain in his left arm. The doctor called on Christmas Day. She remembered her husband saying he had, been put out of some shop, but he did not say whose shop. He afterwards said he had been put out of a confectioner's shop in Bridge-st., but did not say by whom. She did not think he was sufficiently sober on Friday night when he came home to remember what had happened. He died last Friday. 'By Inspector Kelly : Her husband was in bed when the doctor •called and she did not remember his having gone out. to the cutting shop. Dr. Mnuncey, said he was called • for the first time to see deceased on the 12th January, between 11 .and 12 o?cloc'It. His Means tenens Dr. Fowler had been .attend- ing deceased, but he had now gone away, and wit- ness could not say where he would be. When he was ,called to the man he was in bed unconscious, and he died a few hours afterwards. When ,witness saw him, he was ina very feverish state. He had two wounds on the back of his hand which had evidently been suppera- tinge lie made'a post mortem examination of the body onthe Ifith inst. .Deceased was much emaciated, the back of the left arm was red and inflamed. He found on the back of the left hand two old wounds, each an inch long, running obliquely .along, and laying bare the tendons. At thertop of the left elbow, there was a small round hole. On further examination he found that the wounds on the back of his hand communicated with a large cavity on the back of the forearm, aryl which also communicated with the hole in the elbow. He examined.' this and found the tissues disorganised ; the glands in the armpit lie found .to be inflamed. He ex- aminedthe brain and found general menengitie, small abcesses in the upper part of the brain, particularly on thuright side, an&amp;there was a large quantity of fluid. The man was generally in a very unhealthy state, and he was of opinion that death had resulted from blood poisoning, which had arisen, ha believei, from the in- juries on the arm. In reply to the Coroner, he said a mere seratchto a man in such a state of health as de- ceased ,WAS. ,would have led to .such fatal results. John Clarke, gas fitter, said his wife kept a confec- tioner's shop in Bridge-st. On Friday-the 22nd Decetn- b,T, deceased came into toe shop, and asked for a bun. His wile supplied him with one, and he sat down on a chair and commenced to eat it. ,In a few minutes he pulled a penny out of his pocket, and put it on the coun- ter, but his wife told him to put it baekeas he had al- ready paid:for the bun. The man then became somewhat abusive. When he came into the shop he was helplessly drunk. Beset down on a chair near a shelf on which some glasses were fixed, and put his feet on the counter. Some people then came in to be served, and Mrs. Clarke asked witness to request deceased to move his , feet. He teuched him on the shoulder, and asked him if he would mind moving a hit. Fearing he would upset the shelf, he got hold of him by the shoulder to take him to the door. Deceaseclthen defied him to put him out. 'Witness went to the door, and seeing two . policemen he sent a boy to tell them, but before they arrived he had assisted the man to the door, still holding Wei by his coat, and as they got to 'the door, the man raised his hand to strike him and witness twisted out of his way. Deceased oVer- balanced hiinself and fell on the pavement, leaving a portion of his coat in witness's hand. The policemen came up soon after. , e.c. Burns said at twenty past nine he was at the Ram's Head corner, and noticed a crowd standing near the confectioner's shop. He went towards the place, arid found deceased lying in theroad,and:Mr.Clarke standing in the doorway. By Mr_ Kelly.: 'Deceased was very drunk, and could hardly walk without .some.assistance. Mary Eea,n, living in Birley-st., Newton, stated she was in Clarke's shop on ,December'22nti, and saw the de- ceased come in. Site generally corroborated the state- ment made by the witness Clarke, and added that Mr. Clarke did not push him out, the man fell. •While she was in the shop she noticed there was same mud on his ceat, as if he hadfallen before he came in. The jury returned a verdict of ." Death from natural causes."</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CONCERT AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The Town Hall, at Newton, was on Wednesday evening crowded with an audience which ,included , the this of Newton, on the occasion of the annual concert given by the scholars of St. Peter's See ools. For feur years in succession Mr. Singletoneand his merry band of clever children have delighted the Newton people by the excellent manner in which ' they acquit themselves in the difficult tasks allotted • them, and too much praise cannot be bestowed, first, on the children, and secondly,, on those who hawe trained them. Such things as children's cancerts in Newton were at one time quite an unknown quantity, but the advent of Captain and Mrs. Royse., altered matters a little, and, on the solicitation of this worthy couple, Mr. Singleton was persuaded to maize a venture, and al- though the first attempt was suite a simple affair, compared with the productions of last year and the present, it was seifucLly evident that in the schools there was lying dormant a large amount of ability, which but for the suggestion of Mr. and Mrs. Royse, would never have been disccvered. Of course, the tabour of training the children was a gigantic task to undertake, but this to some extent minimised by the natural aptness of the children.. Again;i before any respectable representation could be madiethe pockets of the parents had to be considered, but the re- sult on the eventful eight showed that they had taken; , the liveliest interest in the idea,and even after four years: this interest shows not the slightest signs of flaggingsbut: teather increases, each evidently viemg to outdo theJ other. This year Mr. Singletsn and Miss Liptrott must:: •haYeenceuntered enormous difficulties, although they have been ably backed up by their assistants, and Cap- tin and Mrs. Royse, but they must experience intense, ,satisfaction, when it is cauveyed to them that their: •offorts have been eminently satisfactory. The platformafor Wednesday evening's entertainnient had been considerably enlarged as belore, this being ne- Cessary from the fact that something like 100 children had to he. accommodated upon it. As an opening,the school song, "The Sea is England's Glory," was sung by the whole body of cnildren a solo being very nicely rein' derea•bsebliss Ada Bethel. We might here remark that in thieeand the other songs, the time kept was remark- aislsecorrect, due to the close attention the children paid ; to the beat of the conductor. The infants, very little , mites, in charge of Miss Liptrott, had the next turneind gave an action song entitled "The windmill,"in which ten youngsters each having hold of variedcoloured ribbensgo round, amnrepresent at the end of each verse, the wind blowing from,all points, and describing what effect they have caponthe,mill and the weather. A musical drill, with dumb bells by a number of boys, was exception- ally weilsione. and called forth hearty applause. It would be well if.a little more of this sort of education was ,een in our fi.larlestown schools. Another action song by the infants. entitled " Three little mice," was capitally given, Miss Raynor, Miss Grace Bethel, and Master F. .ward, leing attired to represent the three mice, and very well they acquitted themselves. The girls afterwards executed a musical drill with Indian clubs, and it wield be hard to say whether this or the dumb hell exercise was the cleverest. There is not the slightest doubt., however, that the girls deserved all the applause thee received. Miss Ada Bethel', as Brutus, and Miss Mabel Leather as Cassins, very cleverly recited the conversation between those two Roman characters contained in Shakespeare's "Julius Ceasar." A musical drill with skipping ropes was nicely executed by several infant girls, sind this was followed by a tableau entitled " The Matola Girl." The recitation was very well given by etiss Gnaw, Bethel!, and the pi, tures of the epiaodes in tins match girl's life on a cold winter's , day were very good, the make up of the barefooted match girl toeing almost true to life, her death in the snow heing full of pathos. This concluded fittingly the first part of the pro- gramme. The second part was taken up with Grieve's " Old friends with new faces." The char- acters in this were Sister Ann (Miss Cassandr i Haynes), Mistress Mary (Miss Amelia Wilkinson), Jill (Miss Ada Chapman), Mother Hubbard (Miss Mabel Mayor), Little Red Ridhug Hood (Miss Ada Bethell). Humpty Inanity (darter Charles Fletcher), Jack (Master Thomas Ove), Little Boy Blue (Master Harold Mayer), Little Jack Herne: (Master Edwin Perry), Bo Peep (Miss Annie EBam). It is almost invidious to attempt to give it detailed description of this, or to praise either one or other of the aaracters.. Of the plot we cannot speak, simply because there is none. It is merely a represen- tat ion of, or bringing into notice by harmony, the old characters, so often mentioned in fairy story hooks. We Will therefore, content 4)ot-selves by saying tleit the dresses were rich, and appropriate to the characterseind each child performed its little task in a le .t! Is' credi- table manner, the choruses being very efficiently fettle. During the reprffeetation, a number of boys, who had been previously drilled by Sergeant Instructor Wondine, gave a clever exhibition of the bayonet exercise,- Master Churls Fiftt!her acting as, Captain. A skipping dance was elei given by the girls, which created amusement, and called Isiah seine applause. • Toe finale was capi- tally worked out, and tite grouping of the children was very effeetive, and, to the aecempaniment of coloured lights and loud applause, the curtains were tit awn. Mr. , Tett, resistant master, and Miss Frost accompanied the van i ,es items.</p>
        <p>Before the eloqe, the Rec. F. W. Johnson said, AS the Vicar and Dr. Watildas were absent, he had been asked</p>
        <p>to say a few words. He wanted to return the thanks Of all the performers for the attendance of such a large audience, and for the very kind way they received the entertainment. And again, he would like to convey ths thanks of the audience t the performers. That kind of thing could not be got up without a lot of trouble, and he thought they were all convinced that everything had gone off very well indeed. (Applause.) Be would, therefore like to convey their thanks to Mr. Singleton, Miss Liptrott. Captain and. Mrs. Royce, and to show that they thoroughly appreciated the concert. (Applause.)—Cap`ain Royse said it was always a plea- sure to have anything to with these performances. He wanted to mention something that had been forgotten on previous occasions, and he asked them to notice how vary well the children were dressed. This was all at t'Ae expense of the parents, and it showed the lively interest they took in the performances. He also want4thein to thank their neighbours, Mr. ansl Mrs. Gritnki:; Drayton, who had kindly provided buns and oranges for the chil- dren. (Applause.) Last year the proceeds went to the gymnastic fund, and this year Mr. Singleton hoped to engage a sergeant to drill the children. Be had now a pleasing duty to perform. He believed Mrs. Royse and himself tried to get this performance when they first came to Newton. They got it started a few years ago, and since that time it had always been a great pleasure to work with Mr. Sinsleton and Miss Liptrett, and to show his appreciation, he wanted to present Mr. Singleton with a baton, and hoped lie might be able to conduct. these per- formances for many yeah to come. Miss Liptrott has always assisted his wife in making dresses,ikc., and dur- ing the past fortnight had had very little time ;Aber dis- posal, and he hoped she would accept that little case of scissors to assist her in cutting out in the future. (Laughter.) Speaking officially, he would sny,that he had never seen better work as far as his department (drawing) is concerned, than is done at St. Peter's school. kApplaiise )—Mr. Singleton, in reply, said both Miss Liptrott and himself were agreeably surprised wit h the presents. They had no idea of any such thing, and he was sure Captain and Mrs. Royse would accept from him an expression of their heartiest thanks, and also of the great pleasure they experienced in working together. (Ap,alause.) The entertainment was repeated on Thursday night, when the hall was again crowded.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL CONFERENCE OF MINERS'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>FEDERATION.</head>
        <p>FEDERATION.</p>
        <p>The annual Conference of the Miners' Federation f Great Britain was opened on Tuesday at Leicester. Mr. S. Woods, M.P., presided, in the absence of Mr.. Pickard, M.P., who was too ill to attend. The Mayor of Leicester gave the delegates a hearty welcome, anti also invited them to dinner. Mr. Cowey, of the Yorkshire Miners' Association, expressed gratification on behalf of the dele- gates at the kind reception they had received. It was, he said, the first municipal recognition they had 01 - tained, and it came as a pleasant surprise after a long struggle in the coal inaustry which had interfered with other trades. Only formal business was transacted.</p>
        <p>The Miners' Federation Conference was resumed on Wednesday at Leicester. Mr. Pickard's address as pre- sident, was read by Mr. S. Woods, M.P. It referred to the recent coal struggle, and said though some hat: fallen in thelight the spoils of victory remained with the men. Touching on the joint conference,he said they had found in Lord Elosebery one who held the balance faiKy, and the agreement that the men should return to work at the old rate of wages had been almost generally carrkd out. He hoped that a minimum wage would be agreed upon, and that a permanent Board of conciliation would be arranged, so that these terrible labour struggles might be avoided. It had been truly said in respect to the recent conflict that both sides had got a good licking and would never care to go through another exper:ence like it. He urged the miners to be loyal to the Federa- tion, and trusted they would have a prasperous year. Resolutions were adopted in favour of moving an amend- ment to: the Queen's speech unless the Government.gave a piodge that they would press forward the MinerS'. Eig,htHours' Bill, against the stacking' of more than,one week's coal for pit use,and admitting the Scotch district' to the-Federation on condition that they also formed a Scottish Miners' Federation.</p>
        <p>The Miners' 'Federation Conference was resumed at Leicester ha Thursday. Mr. B. Pickard, M.P., presided. Considerable discussion took place on the qupstiox of coal stacking and the restriction of output. Mr. Small a Lanarkshire delegate, advocated the establishment of a department of the Federation to watch the accumula- tion of stocks, to inquire as to the number of working days, and to ascertain the amount of trade done in the various mining districts. A resolution was adopted in- structing thefrExecntive to collect information as to the: accumulation, of coal at the depots and pit banks, and communice,te,Nrith the different branches of the Federa- tion when they considered such accumulation dangerous to the interests of the miners. It was decided that the , members of the Federation should pay a contribuqon of sixpence per week for labour purposes. Other resolu- tions were tarried in favour of more frequent inspection of mines and shorter hours for the colliery engine keepers. Oa Thursday night the delegates were enter- tained at dinner • by the Leiceste,. branch of the National . Union of Boot and Shoe Operatives. Mr. Harvey, one • of the Drbyshire kaciers,said the men would not accept Arbitration unless they had a voice in fixing the prices at which coal was to be sold.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>EMPLOYERS' LIABILITY BILL AND</head>
        <p>THE EMPLOYERS' LIABILITY BILL AND</p>
        <p>CONTRACTING OUT.</p>
        <p>The Trade Councils of Lancashire have receiveJ the following letter 10, Downing-street. Whitehall, January 11. Gentlemen,-1 am desired by Mr. Gladstone to ac- knowledge the receipt of the letter enclosing copies of the resolutions passed ,by your councils emphatically condemning the insertion of the contracting-out clause in the Employers' Liability Bill, and t say that Mr„ Gladstone heartily ,approves of such working men's councils as the trade ,councils of Lancashire passing such resolutions.</p>
        <p>A meeting summoned to consider the contracting-out clause of the Employers' 'Liability Bill was held in the -Picton Lecture Hall, Liverpool, on Monday evening, and was attended by representatives of the London and North-Western Railway from the different goods and .passeneer stations, and by representatives from the South Metropolitan Gas Works and Belfast Steamship .Company. The meeting had not proceeded far, before it was discovered that at strong element of opposition to the Lords' amendments would have to be reckoned with from amongst the dockers, and also the represen- tatives of the alkali and (x),p,per works at Widnes and St. Helens. Railway representatives claimed their rijit, to spend their wages as they thought proper, free 'from Government interference; whilst dockers and other representatives in opposition to the House of Lords asserted their right to propoee an amendment condemn- ing the action of the Peers. The chairman refused to accept this latter view, contendlog that only those in f.vour•o: the contracting eut clause were summoned to the meeting : and eventually the meeting broke up in disorder.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held at the Crewe•Town Hall on Tues- day evening to consider the Empioveri' Liability Bill. Mr. A. Ni. Hornby presided. A resolution in favour of the insertion of a clause in the bill allowing mutual in- surance soc,ieties to no continued was carried. Mr. W. S. B. M•Laren, M P.. attended the meeting to explain his position, and met with a mixed reception. The bulk of the audience seemed to be in favour of Lord Dudley's amendment, And Mr. M.Laren was questioned as to his reason for opposin,, that atnamiment. The bonAnember stated that he could see that there was no chance of the Commons agreeing to that proposal, and he hoped- that the Lords, When the bill was sent up to them ag,.ain, would agree to the limited proposal which he had made in reference to the existing mutual insurance companies being allowed to contract out of the bill. The proceed- ings were occasionally very noisy.</p>
      </div>
