      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>letters to tin Ctittor.</head>
        <p>letters to tin Ctittor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO COURESPONDENTS. Mr. E. Foulkes :—Received.</p>
        <p>THE RIVER AND THE SIM' C..k.NA.L. Sir,—Suiely the P.D.has been at his iit_tle games—play- ing tricks with what would otherwise have been a very god and accurate report ef the haerview between your representative and Mr. R. T.Fairclough. It is the hydro- graphic bits that I refer to—the influence the Ship Canal will he likely to have upon the river, Icc. Of the purely bminess part I do not feel capable to say anything worth hearing ; but of the river I have had practical experi- ence for many years. This being so, I find that I shall have to unlearn much that I thought was settled and satisfactory knowledge. That is if Mr. R. T. Fairclough has been faithfully reported—and it what he is reported to have said be true. "o more floods or freshets." Too good to be true ! While I fully believe that the Ship Canal will have lessened the tot&amp; amount of stream- water at all seasons—still a large fresh will never find the Ship Canal ready to take the whole of it. The Com- pany would find it expensive to make their canal a set- tling ground for such freshets as we have had. Can anyone imagine the water of the fresh of 1866 going down the canal in place of the river ? But this the Ship Canal will do—it will take all the water tram the river in dry weather. Such a season as this last summer will dry up the bed of the river and leave it to be silted up by the tides. This shoaling will soon occur when the dawn scour is taken off—as it must be—for the canal must have water—and to get this it taps the Mersey basin. It will be news to anyone ac- quainted with the river, or running water generally, that the freshes (for that is the local name, freshet being the book term for the same) run on opposite sides of the river to the flood tide. This would have puzzled Ad- miral Spratt himself ! I must have been a very unob- servant person for the la,t 35 years not to have noticed this! Solomon said there was nothing new under the sun. But this is new to me, and, I think, to the river. The strongest run of the flood tide is where the strong- est of the down stream ran under the " frets "on the outer bend of the curves. But it is funny to find any- one—even a P.D.—thinking that to lessen the down scour of a tidal river will d, epen it! It will be the pockets of the shareholders—through the medium of the dredgers that will effect this—if it is done.—Yours, JOHN PEERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>HUMOUR QV ECONOMICS.</head>
        <p>THE HUMOUR QV ECONOMICS.</p>
        <p>. Sir,—Since Mr. le hitham cannot, for a considerable time, reply to Mr. Peer's comments upon his article which appeared on January 6[h, perhaps I may be al- lowed to refer to one or two points raised by the latter gentleman. The times are favourable for the growth of Socialism. It is becoming clearer every day in 'which direction men must look for an escape from the evils of our present commercial and industrial system. I think Mr. Peers mistakes the purport of Mr. Whitham's article when he represents him as advocating •‘ the right of the poor man to demand relief from the rich." Dismal as may be the subject of economies there are, it is clear, many latent possibilities of humour in it. The maker, the creator of wealth, allowing the rich man to seize it and then demanding relief! Excellent joke. by Jove! Two great causes of poverty and distress—over production of men and over production of wealth for their enjoyment! Too many people in the world, and too much for them to eat and drink and wear. Great conundrum this ; capital fun! Do not let your readers mistake. It would not be fair for me to claim credit for these flishes of economic wit, responsibility must rest with our involuntary jokist, Mr. Peers. No ! demanding " relief " frem the rich is not exactly what we want. We want the full fruit of our work. Whether or not we ought to regard the Workhouse with aversion, all the same we unquestienably do. We ought to feel thankful perhaps that after alife of toil, whereby many have been able to live in luxury, we have got such a' thing as a Workhouse to end our days in, but, alas, for the frailties of eiuman neture., we are ungrate- ful creatures. Fact is. we don't consider it good enough. Yet a tremendous proportion of working people end their days there after all. Seciedism laughs at the terrible bugbear" over-pro- duction." The Socialist would pull no long faces when Nature's cornucopia was emptied at his feet. Now-a-days a bountiful crop is not always regarded as an unmixed good. Socialism would alter all that. Yet here are we poor creatures, taught to deplore over-production as an evil. It is not over-production that is the cause of poverty and elistress ; it is unequal distribution. The" poor man" is a terrible creature. Not only does be do the world's work, letting the rich have the best of everything and contenting himself with a,mere subsist- ence, but as Mr. Peers says e` he too frequently eats and drinks." Vulgar varlet. Shocking bad ha-bit—eating and drinking. Why can't he leave this sort of thing to the wealthy, who can do it artistically with the aid of silver plate and at the rate of a guinea a bettle. Seriously, there is too mach of this kind of talk about the vices of the poor man. So long as you have exhaust- ing toil and povertyeand, as a consequence,a lowering of physical health,so long will you have excessive drinking. I:wish seine of these censorious individuals could change places for a time with the poorer brethren they never cease to scold. They would be surprised at their own eating and drinking capacity after a short period of hard honest toil. Mr. John Peers knows that for a rose to grow well it must have suitable soil and conditions favourable to its development. I am conscious of the importance at thrift and temperance, but only so far as they make for morality, and improvement of the race, and not as a remedy for si cial inequalities. Under a system of individualism where labour was unorganized these would prove a delusion and a sire re.—Yours, &amp;c., COMMON JUSTICE.</p>
        <p>A CRITIQUE ON TWO CRITICS. "Extinguished theologians lie about the cradle of every science, as the strangled snakes beside that of Her- cules; and history recerds that, whenever science and orthodoxy have been fairly opposed, the latter has been forced to retire from the lists, bleeding and crushed, if net annihilated ; scotched, if net slain. But orthodoxy .7 is the Bourbon of the world of thought. It learns not, neither can it forget; and, though at present bewildered and afraid to move, it is as willing as ever to insist that tlie first chapter of Genesis contains the beginning and the end of sound science ; and to visit, with such petty thunderbolts as its half paralysed hands can hurl, those who refuse to degrade nature to the level of Primitive Judaism."—Prof. Huxley, in "Lay Sermons." Sir,—Your correspondence columns in last issue show how perversely men will, whether ignorantly or designedly, confuse and confound the simplest issues. Both of my critics in last issue afford abundant evidence of their facility in getting "mixed." To drag in . matters unrelated to the subject is an excellent way to confusion and mental obfuscation; while dialectic, skill is shown by him who keeps the threads of argument clear from entanglement, nor attempts to mix incompatibles. Your correspondent John S. Howell, attempts to dis- parage, Rationalism—i.e. reasonableness—by the use of what he takes to be" reasons." This is as though an artizan were to curse his own tools, by which alone he can work. Now the botch always blames his tools. The man who sees in Anarchy the "only consistent end of Rationalism" is in this state. He is bungling with un- accustomed tools. Dare Mr. J. S. Howell attempt to champion the "primeval fall of man," and its equally illusive consequence the "imaginary salvation?" If Mr. Howell will do this I will do my best to afford him more "amusement." even though his laugh be on the wrong side of his mouth. With marvellous blindness he drags Prof. Mivart upon the stage to prate about Agnos- ticism being illogieal! What a witness in such a case! Mivart wrote some papers on "happiness in Hell." He wrote as confident!, as though he had been there on tour personally coouocted by the proprietor, and had come back to tell us all about it. Be this as it may,he told us that he quite believed that there might be happiness in hell. Now it is not so much the leve of God as the fear of hell and the devil, that drives folk into the clutches of the priest, so this illogical babbler had to recant by order of the Pope! A little Agoosticiem would have saved poor Mivart from making this double exhibition of him- self. As an Agnostic I know nothing of hell; albeit many of my orthodox friends predict my fuller—if future—knowledge thereof,but up to now I lnow nothing of it. Hearsay is not knowledge. From this position I shall not recant even at the dictation of alien ecclesiastics. Then we are told how much better off—happier and had higher ideals and a" rosier outlook " than now—our forbears were in the dark agts. From this we are led to infer that this superior beatitude arose from their free- dom from Rationalism and the prevalence of unreasoning belief. To be happy,then, one ought to be ienorant and credulous. If this be so, what a state of bliss the major- ity of my countrymen must be iT ! And then the" rosier outlook 5' might be the result of the lurid glow of the auto-da-fe, while, if more intelligent than his neighbours, he might have the outlook—or look out—lest he be burnt as a wizard and his wife as a witch. It requires the bemused intellect of a sectary to find any connection between Rationalism and, as a consequence thereof, Matahele wars, starving thousands, bomb-throwing, or murders and suicides. Would the world be any the better today for believ- ing that Jonah did swallow the whale, or vice versa ? No, sir, even the saline transformation of Mrs. Lot must now be taken wftli a very large grain of salt ; and Joshua's sun has set for ever. " Common Justice" reminds me of the month of March—he came in like a lion aeol now, how lamb-like he is—not to say sheepish. As this is my last word to him, may I recall to his warid,iring mind my original contention. Mr. S. Woods, M Foes an M.P. has no right to incite to intimidation those who look up to him for advice. This he did. As a member of Parliament he was bound to uphold the law as it stands—or constitu- tionally to amend it. Now the coal owners may be, for aught I know, the biggest villains unhune, but, as two blacks don't make a white, I refuse to temuddle the issues of this debate by setting one immorality against the -ther. Finally, why do nor Woods an Co. introduce a bill for the legalization of intimidation ? Why, they dare not av lw openly what they advocate covertly.— "Yours truly,—</p>
        <p>JOHN PEERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>NEWTON COMMISSIONERS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln reading in last week's "Examiner " the report of Commissioners meeting held on the Bth inst., I was much struck with the complete absence of enthusiasm. Although it was their first meeting in the new building six of the ten Commissioners only were present. One would have expected a full attendance and that there would have been great congratulations and rejoicing. Three of the absentees were from Newton. Even our " much respected representative" Mr. Evans, of whom it was said he "always voted for Newton ,"was not in his ' place, and it would appear that the Earlestown friends have not only succeeded in carrying all their points, but ' have also manage to drive the Newton representa- tives away altogether. Poor Newton ! Its glory is de- parted, and Mr. Ratcliffe is happy. The next thing I observed was the length of the chairman's opening speech, about three fourths of a column. It is even longer than the inaugurative speech made by Mr. Rat- cliffe at the memorable meeting when the press were excluded. It is to be hoped this is the dawn of a new era for the present Board ; that in future we shall have much fuller explanations of the town's business, and amongst other matters, that the press will not be again excluded ; that we shall be informed when the Local Government Board refuses) application for loans as well as when it accepts them; and that, generally speaking, the Board takes the rateplayers into its confidence. If this were done fairly and openly it would to a great ex- tent disarm criticism. _ _</p>
        <p>The chairman, in the first place, referred to the pro- gress Earlestown had made during the 26 years he had been in the town,and I feel sure there is a general concen- sus of opinion that during tte first twenty of these years the town was exceedingly well governed. The death-rate .waa low, and there was no lack of entvrprise in adopting and carrying out the latest sanitary Acts of Parliament. The ratepayers' money was well and wisely spent and the rates were very reasonable indeed; but all this was by no means due to Mr. Emmett, although I at once admit from the position he lsas been placed by circum- stances he has had a fair share in them. The chairmen we have had during these 20 years were most excellent business and far-seeing men, and, without ostentation, guided the helm of affairs to the entire satisfaction cf the inhabitants at large. The differences have only ariseg during the last few years and during which time Mr. Emmett has been chairman. But lam wishful to give him this credit, that he has done more during the time he has held the office to educate the ratepayers, than all the previous chairmen the town has had since the Act was passed in 1855, for there is no denying that there is nothing which will make the ratepayers look into matters more than to unnecessarily touch their pockets, and this has been done with a vengeance. And the worst of it is that two-fifths of the people do not, nor never will, derive any hcnefit from the foolish expendi- ture, and a great many of the remainine, three-fittlis will have very little ben-fit and say they did not want it. After giving details of the gas and water arrangement with the railway cvmpany, which if put to the test he would have some difficulty in proving, he states the directors would have raised the price of water in 1890, but he advised differently, and his advice was taken. Its 1893 the price wits doubled, and then the chairman's . .</p>
        <p>inconsistency comes in. He says "he wished it to be distinctly understood that all he had to do with the water arrangements was to allow it to flow from Park- side into the company's tank at Earlestown and from there into the Commissioners' mains. Ile had no control whatever over the price charged." It seems to me very strange that he controlled the price of water in 1890 but in 1893 he had nothing to do with it. The rates had by this time increased one-fourth, and with every likelihood of a further increase. The railway company being very large ratepayers, is it unreasenable to think that its rating experts did not look the matter up and found that by doubling the price of water they could be re- couped, and also have a large sum over. The chairman blanvis the Commissioners for not treat-</p>
        <p>ing the company fairly as to the price of gas. lie has been in full power some six years, and could almost have passed any resolution. If his predecessors had done wrong, why did he not six years ago arrange for the company to be treated better? It is ridiculous to blame Mr. Gillespie. He was in a small minority, and could neither reduce nor raise the price of gas, or, in fact, de anything else without Mr. Emmett's consent. Then the statement is made " that if anyone is to blame for the erection of the Town Hall it is the rate- payers, AS the Commissioners had only carried out their wishes." I deny this in tote ; the ratepayers were never consulted either as to the Building or the Site, as they should have been. The torch light procession consisted of about one-third ratepayers, and two-thirds who were not ratepayers. There was, however, a kind of tacit understanding that there should be a Town Hall at Earlestown, at the cost of £4,500. and if this had been adhered to, there would have been no complaints. The majority of the Board afterwards on their own res- ponsibility, and without consulting anyone, increased it to—well, I cannot give the exact sum (I even question if Mr. Thompson or any of the other efficient commis- sioners could do so), but if the yearly exp,-nditure of a caretaker, d.c., be capitalized and included, which it should be, I would not be surprised if the cost be from £12.000 to £15,000.</p>
        <p>'filen the Chairman speaks of the fine, ornamental,and substantial building the Town Hall is. I congratulate him on what would seem to be his keen sense of archi- tectural beauty. He might just have gone a little further and told us to which of the five noble orders of architec- ture it belonged as I do not think many of the rate- payers know. This being an important point I hope at another time he will make a further statement.</p>
        <p>He closes his remarks with the wish that the township of Earlestown and Newton wouid continue to increase, Ecc. It would be obliging if he would in his next speech, explain hew he works these names in. For it your readers refer to advertisoments,circulars on church doors, itc., they will find the name is the Improvement Com- missioners for the district of Newton-in-Makerfield and this I understand is the correct name. Has the chair- man got an Act slipped through the house on the quiet, to alter the name?</p>
        <p>Itis gratifying to hear tho ward question is making progres9. It will lam sure make a change in the com- position of the Board.—l am, yours resp.crfully.</p>
        <p>• 11A.TEl'AYER. Newton-in-liakerfield. January 16th, 1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>PENKETII.</head>
        <p>PENKETII.</p>
        <p>M UTUAL IMPROVEME NT Assoc.' ATION.</p>
        <p>Oa Wednesday night in connection with the above Association a most interesting lecture was given by Mr. Madeley of the Warrington Museum. The lecture was on "Some Obsolete Methods of Corporal Punishment." Mr. John Peers occupied the chair. The Lecturer i-a the course of his remarks described the punishment of the stocks and several specimens of stocks, including those of Lymm, Grappenhall, etc., were thrown on the screen the lantern being manipulated by Mr. Dutton. The pillory was next described and was in Mr. 'Madeley's opinion, a much more dangerous punishment, the tem- per of the populace being vented hr flinging stones. &amp;c., at the, head- of the prisoner. Several pillories were shown on the screen, and afterwards the gibbet was des- cribed at length and several sets of irons were repre- sented, including the set presented to the Warrington Museum by Mr. Beamont, which are almost perfect. The crank or scold's bridle, was next described and the Lecturer remarked on the singular fact,that of the crank's preserved the majority are from Cheshire, Lancashire coming second. Several pictures were shown of these articles of torture and at the conclusion Mr. Madeley received a hearty round of applause. Mr. R. Garnett, J.P., in rising to propose a v )te of thanks, said it was a pity that the use of the stocks had died out, as he shohld certainly advocate their use in punishment for certain offences. He remembered the stocks that used to exist in Periketh. The vote of thanks was heartily accorded. MUSICAL EVENIN(L—On Friday evening last week the inhabitants were favoured for the first time in its his- tory, by a visit from the choir of Dr. Stephenson's "Children's Homes." This visit had been arranged for a considerable time, and was eagerly looked forward to. The concert took place in the Elementary Day School, • Mr. C. E. Parker presiding. There was a very large at- tendance. The children frem the Home were accom- panied by the Rev. N. Curnoek, Mr. F. A. Mann (musi- cal director). and Miss Stephenson (Sister Dora). Amongst those present may be mentioned the Revs. J. Kirtlan, G. D. Mason, and J. Gunnell ; Messrs. R. Gar- nett, J. C. Routledge, J. Digniim, J. Peers, .T. Watson, R. E. Garnett. W. Brough, J. Millington, W. J. Wigfield, R. Cook, and Dr. Murray. At seven o'clock the meet- ing was opened by singing the hymn " Hark, hark, my Soul," the Rev. J. Kirtlan afterwards leading in prayer. Mr. C. E. Parker, then in a few well chosen words, in- troduced Mr. Mann and his choir to the audience. The conduct of the programme was then handed over to the Rev. N. Curnock. It consisted of songs, solos, trios, choruses, recitations, and instrumental pieces on the mandolin, dulcimer, xylophone, and other instruments. Me pieces throughout were excellently rendered, great attention being paid to the modulation and harmony of the voices in the part songs and choruses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>OLD FOLKS' fItEAT COMMITTEE A PRO' ONGED liITRINESS MEETING EXPECTED.</head>
        <p>THE OLD FOLKS' fItEAT COMMITTEE A PRO' ONGED liITRINESS MEETING EXPECTED.</p>
        <p>Mr.HaltotLof the George Inn, applied to the War- rington magistrates (Alderman I larrison,DrAiornall, and Councillor Roberts) yesterday, for extension of an hour and a half that evening, to allow the Old Men and Women's Tea Committee to settle up their business.—Mr. koherts I think it is a shame to ask such a thing.—Mr. Halton said that the house would he closed except to the committee.— Councillor Roherts said that he was not opposed to a reasonaLle applica- tion, hut he thought that the present occassion was a ridiculous one to ask for an extension of time. It was a bad precedent to set to grant th 7, application.—Dr. Clor- nail thought the mornin, w. nld be a hotter time for the committee to settle up the affairs.—Alderman Harrison : Could you not appoint an earlier hour, Mr. Halton ? Mr. Halton : The committee say they cannot settle their affairs before then.—G,uneillor Roberts said he did not mean anything personal to Mr. Balton, but he thr,n,,h, the present application was one which it would have been well to have left alone.—Mr. Halton said that he did not want to he refused. It was his first application to the court, and he would withdraw it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>TURD TE HEX A MIN ER r.vu . SA Y JAN 'CAE Y 20, 1.894.</head>
        <p>TURD TE HEX A MIN ER r.vu . SA Y JAN 'CAE Y 20, 1.894.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES A:i\:D</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>The Warrington team were tasting the sweets of re- venge on Saturda), when they administered a d.rubbing to the Rangers from Broughton. The Rangers were the first team which Warrington met in the League Competi- tion, and the thought that a defeat was in store tor the Wirepullers when they visited Brought', never entered the heads of their admirers, for never before had the Rangers come out on the top. They, however, gained a substantial victory. Warrington had ample revenge on Saturday, however, for they "nailed " the Rangers, which coming after the victory of the latter over Tyldes- ley, is not a bad performance.</p>
        <p>Warrington were minus Potts on Saturday. Everyone knows bow difficult it is to hold this young gentleman whem he fairly gets going, but the absence of Potts gave another chance for Barber, of Stockton Heath, to dis- tioguieh himself, which he kd to everybody's satisfac- tion. B irber bids fair to become prominent figure in the Warrington team, for lie possesses qualities which stamp him as a player very much out of the ordinary. ; The re•-t of the three-quarters were in very good form, and were decidedly superior to tqeir opponents. 'rite gymnasium practice is having a very good effect in this department, for there is a vast improvement since the time when Oldham ran away with them. Heesom as usual was very safe at full back, making only one slip during tha whole of the match. Ills two Bates took all the shine out of the opposing halves, hut the forwards were about equally matched. It was the back play of Warrington that won the game.</p>
        <p>To-day,Warrington team go to play In "foreign parts" in a league fixture. They meet the redoubtable Tyldws- ley team, and will have a severe tussle to come out on the right side. When the teams met at Warrington, Tyldesley had to return home a beaten team. Will War- rington repeat their performance P Draws are the usual results at Tyldesley when these teams meet. Football is very uncertain,bat perhaps it will be interesting to know that the last league game of the Broughton Rangers pre- vious to meeting Warrington. was • with Tyldesley at Tyldesley when the Rangers won. By the way the goal kicking of Warrington is of a very fluctuating character. Everyone was grumbling at the ill success in this depart- ment, some weeks ago, until they met Rugby, when lo! from thirteen attempts, they put on ten goals ! Since that match, the Warrington team has not gained a single goal.</p>
        <p>The rewults of the Lancashire leagun competition up to Saturday last was</p>
        <p>Phi. Won. Lost. Drn. GT. G.T. Pt. For. Ag t. Oldham lO ... 8 ... 1 .1 ... 15 17... 3 4 ...17 Swinton 9 ... 6 ... 0 ... 3 ... 9 15 . 1 2...15 Wigan 9 ... 5 ... 1 ... 3 ... 4 7 ... 1 2...13 Warrington Salford Tyldesley Rochcla;e Hornets Broughton Hangers 9 ... 3 ... 5 ... 1 ... 6 2 ... 6 10 ... 7 Barrow Broughton 9 ... 0 ... 9 ... 0 -. 1 4 ...21 22 ... 0 The following fixtures were postponed :—Broughton Rangers v. Broughton, and Swinton v. Wigan.</p>
        <p>The A team made r o mistake with the Broughton Ran- gers Aon Saturday, It ugh they had to fight before Broughtonians. Tw go its, two tries, ft o nil, is a capi- tal performance, and 1., oves that the team has again re- gained its old form, which it appeared to have lost in the earlier part of tiis season, They meet Tyldesley A to- day,and will no doubt again endeavour to aciminister the terrible drubbing they gave this team two years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>GYMNASIUM.</head>
        <p>GYMNASIUM.</p>
        <p>The committee—and also the members and conductor —of the Gymimasium have much cause to be highly pleased with themselves. The success which attended their efforts on Saturday night at the display, confers upon them the right to figuratively pat themselves on the back, inasmuch as they provided an exposition second to none that has been given in the institution. There was an entirely new feature introduced into the display. This was the performance of several difficult exercises by juvenile members both boys and girls rang- ing from about six to twelve. These little people showed by their performances how well they had been trained. Perhaps the chief item on the programme was a per- formance on the horizontal bars by Mr. Kinsley the conductor. It was the most extraordinary exhibition of gymnastic skill that has been given in Warrington, and together with Mr. Kinsley's conduct during the evening, went to prove that the institution have been fortunate in securing the services of an able leader and trainer.</p>
        <p>By the way, last week I mentioned that the Warring- ton eight would meet Stockport eight in the 200 guineas S;iield Competition. This has been confirmed, and the meeting will take place on Thursday next, at Stockport, the latter having overcome the shyness to which I al- luded last week, in respect to " performing before rela- tives and friends." The team selected by Warrington is as follows: J. E. Warren. G. N. Vernon. A. Vernon, J. Podmore, F C. Bennion, G. Clay, J. A. Mottram, and A. Dickens; J. Holmes has been selected for the reserve.</p>
        <p>It should he mentioned that Mr. R. A. Naylor has pre- sented to the Gymnasium 50 forms, which will be of much ;ervice at future displays.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WARRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>- r _</p>
        <p>k meeting of this Board was held at the Board Offi,:•es,Bewsey Chambers, on Wednesday afternoon. Captain T. Ellames Withington, occupied the chair. There were also present : Messrs. John Grounds, ' Thos. Houghton, Reginald °wen jun., Wm. Roy- ston, Thos. Millington, Richard Ormrod, Philip I. Bate, Jos. Clare, Thos. Wright, Henry Bate, 11. B. White (clerk), and 3. Thompson (surveyor),</p>
        <p>AN IMPROVEMENT AT CROFT.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor reported that the trustees of St. Lewis' Roman Uathlc Mission, at Croft, were willing 63 sell 241 square yards of land required by the Board for an improvement at Croft, at 61. per square yard. The Board to cut down the trees, and to hand over the prc- ceeds to the trustees, and to level the land. Mr. Owen pryposed that the agreement be accepted. Mr. Bate seconded, and it was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>PROPOSIID BRANCH RAILWAY AT ARPLEY.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor communicated to the Board that he had received notice that application was intended to be made to Parliament in the next session by the Manches- ter Ship Canal Company for an Act to construct a short branch line of railway in the Arpley Meadows. Conse- quently he had examined the deposited plans, and he found that a subway would be required for the passage of pedestrians where the proposed line crosses the pub- lic path leading to Walton Arches. The other parts of Coe scheme appeared to he satisfactory so far as the Board was concerned. Mr. Thompson said the propos( d subway would cut through the embankment. He thought one from five to six feet wide would be suffici- ent. The proposed branch line was to lead to the new Warrinetm dock?, and would boa &amp;utile line. Che Surveyor was instructed to see the Manchester Ship Canal engineer on the matter.</p>
        <p>HALTON 110AltD BRIDGH AGAIN.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor referring to the proposed improvement of the road at Halton Bridge, Penketh, said he had seen Mr. Tunstall. and the amended plan he now submitted he thought Mr. Tunstall would be willing to grant the required land for it. The improvement would cost abcut £750, and he thought the County Council would carry it out if the Board would offer the Council some- thing towards the cost. It was proposed by Mr. Owen that the Surveyor be empowered to make an offer on be- half of the Board, if it was required.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Since the It meeting of the Bolrd £l,OOO had been received from the County Treasurer,i and £7O 6s. 10d. from Mr. Thompson. There was a balance in hand of £791 7s. 61. Accounts amounting to £1,505 were passed for payment. The accounts were passed on the motion of Mr. Royston, seconded by Mr. Houghton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>INDEBTED TowNsuirs.</head>
        <p>INDEBTED TowNsuirs.</p>
        <p>TheCletk stated that the Navember calla had not yet been paid by the following townships ;—Great Sankey, Lowton, Poulton-with-Fearnhead, Win wick-with•lme and Warrington Extra Municipal.—Mr. Owen : What do you intend to de with those townships.—The Clerk : Threaten them with proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>INCREASE OF SALARIES.</head>
        <p>INCREASE OF SALARIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thompsbn, swirveynr, in applying for an increase of salary wrote that £lOO per annum had been allowed fir the last three years by the Main Roads Committee of the County Council for salaries and establisMinent charges in connection with the Main Roads Committee, but up to the present no application had been made, either by the Clerk or himself for any portion of it. The w( 'k of the Board had erown so excessive that he felt bound to ask the Board to kindly arrange for the ac- count to be apportioned as mentioned at the last meet- ing of the Board. Mr. Thompson gave oi,,,ht reasons for appyling for an increase of salary. Mr.H.B•White,the Clerk,in his application for higher remuneration,pointed out that he had not received an increase of salary since the 16th of Mar, 1877, when his salary was increac.ed from £5O to £7O, at which it now stood. The work of the Board had been nearly doubled since the last ad- vance. The Clerk and the Surveyor having left the room, Mr.OWen in proposing that £7O advance be given ' to Mr. Thompson, and £3O to the ()lerk, said they had a Surveyor second to none in Lancashire. Mr. Ormrod seconded the motion. Mr. Royston moved an amend milt that the Surveyor receive £5O advance, and th., Clerk £2O, and that the £5O balance from the County Council he kept in hand for establishment charges-. The Assistant-CI, rk said that would be £309 per year for Mr. Thompson, and £OO for Mr. White. Nlr. Houghton seconded the amendment. On the amendment helm, put to the meeting- six voted for it, and five voted for the ori,vinal motion —The Chairman : The amendment is passed.—This concluded the hnsiness.</p>
        <p>Miss Henrietta Crampton, of Wsr,grave National Scho,l, has gaineci the first prize in a drawing corn- peti'ion given by the Editor of the 'Schoolmistress."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT BRIDGEWATER LOCK.</p>
        <p>On Friday evening the Danish brigantine, MEendor, Captain Binch, when ent-ring Bridgewater Lock from the Mersey, struck the lock gates which were somewhat damaged. The ship's figureheai was splintered on one side. The vessel was laden with boneash from Rio Grande.</p>
        <p>DROWNED FROM A RUNCORN BOUND VESSEL. The captain of the schooner Fortunah, which arrived in Runcorn on Friday night reported the death by drowning, of a member of his crew named William Dea- con, of Charlo,town, Cornwall. The crew went ashore at Liverpool, on Friday, leaving the schooner anchored in the Mersey. On returning the b.at struck the vessel's bow aud capsized, the occupants being thrown into the water. Deacon got under the hoe.% and was drowned. The others were rescued.</p>
        <p>COLLISION AT RUNCORN LAY BYE. On Saturday afternoon in consequence of the approach- in darkness, the captain of the S.S., Fez, bound fGr Manchester decided to stop at Runcorn Lay Bye until next morning. In endeavouring to moor astern the brig Matatidin, or St. John, N. 8., which is unloading a cargo of timber there, the Fez collided with the stern of the vessel, causing it to lurch so heavily that one of the iron mushrooms to which it was moored was smashed ,clean off from its fastenings. Neither vessel sustained much damage. THE DUES TO RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A petition has been prepared, and is being signed, for presentation to Mr. S. Taylor, Chairman of the Board of Commissioners, requesting him to call a public meeting at an early date to protest against the unfair treatment of Runcorn by the M inchester Ship Canal Company and to pass such resolutions as thi- meeting may deem advis- able. In the meantime matters remain unaltered. While the concession as regards pottery material, which was fully reported last week, has given much satisfaction, it is still argued that Runcorn is entitled to the same pri- vileges as Ellesmere Port. THE FIRST COTTON STEAMER FOR MAN- C El ESTER.</p>
        <p>The m.s., Finsbury entered the Silford doeka on Tues- day afternoon with a cargo of about a thousand tons of cotton from Galveston, Texas. This is the first cotton laden steamer that has come up to Manchef.ter. Her captain, while he believes that it would have bean saier to construct " lay byes " at intervals, speaks in high praise of the canal, and his opinion of it is endorsed by his chief engineer. The Finsbury was followed into the lock at E4stliam by another ship with a cargo of cotton, the s.s. Glen File, also from Galveston.</p>
        <p>NARROW ESCAPE FROM DROWNINO AT STOCKTON</p>
        <p>HEATH.</p>
        <p>A boy named Carpenter, who lives on the old read, which was closed when the swing bridge at Stockton Heath was completed, had a narrow escape, from drowning in the Ship Canal on Friday evening week. He had been playing to.) near the bank ef the canal where there is no fence for protection and fell in. The &amp;ides being built of masonry, it is impossible for anyone who,falls in to ff et out again, even if he be a swimmer. The greatest difficulty was experienced in reecuing the lad owing to this circumstance. Two men ran to the spct, and by holding on to each other they managed to grasp.him and pull him out just as he was about to sink for the third time. The men are certainly,to be coin- mended,for their prompt action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ANXIETY IN LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>ANXIETY IN LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>It is stated that anxiety is felt in commercial circles In Liverpool with regard to the Manchester Ship Canal as a competitor. It states that a conference is being ar- ranged between the Mersey Dock Board, the Chamber of Commerce, the railway companies, and the Liverpool Co.poratiou to promote through traffic and to facilitate trade on the Mersey. On Tuesday, at a meeting of the nuance Committee of the Mersey Dock Board, proposals were made, for approval by the whole Board, for the re- duction of port charges and other dues. At a meeting of the Liverpool Land and House Owners' Association a member stated that a tenant paying £lOO a year had given notice, intending to move his office to Man- chester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SAILINGS THROUGH LATCHFORD. The following vessels have passed through the Latchford Locks since Saturday :---</head>
        <p>SAILINGS THROUGH LATCHFORD. The following vessels have passed through the Latch- ford Locks since Saturday :---</p>
        <p>INGOING. / Acacia, Treeport to Manchester. Emily, Cardiff to Manchester. Fez, Vatenth, to Manchester. Princess Helena, Gla-gow, to Manchester. Dynamic, Belfast. to Manchester. Ibis, Rotterdam, to Manchester, Red Star. Rotterdam, to Manchester. Crag, London, to Mancherter. Edith Hough, London. to Manchester. Finsbury, Galveston, to Manchester. (First cotton vessel, 4,170 bales.</p>
        <p>OUTGOING. Princess Alexandra, Manchester,to Belfast. Acacia, Manchester, to Swansea. Renwick, Manchester, to Swansea. Lodore, Manchester, to London. Lamprey, Manchester, to Glasgow. Emily, Manchester, to hiverpool. On Thursday and yesterday a considerable number of vessels passed up and down the canal. Yesterday at noon the Venango, from New Orleans, one of the largest vessels that has yet entered the canal passed through Latchford on its way to Manchester. From some cause, however, the tug drawing the ves-el met with an acci- dent, which considerably delayed her arrival at Man- chester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GYMNASTIC DISPLAY AT WAItRINGTON.</head>
        <p>GYMNASTIC DISPLAY AT WAItRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A SUCCESSFUL TOURNAMENT.</p>
        <p>The large attendance at the Gymnasium on Saturday evening to witness the gymnastic display given by the members under the conductorship of Mr. J. Kinsley, evidenced that the Warrington pub- lic take considerable interest in the doings of our local gymnasts. The display may be said to have served three purposes, namely—as a good adver- tisement for the "gym," in the hope of getting new. members; an aid to the funds of the institution; and an opportunity for some of the members to perform in public, previous to the competition in the National Shield Competition, and to help in the choosing of the " champion eight." The Mayor (Alderman E. Greenall) had promised to be chairman, but swing to his ill-health the position was filled by Mr. Thomas Morris, F.G.S. There were also present Mrs. Morris, Alderman Wright, Drs. Kisby and Adams, Mr. and Mrs. D. Jones, Messrs. Rowson, Fergusson, Hesketh, W. W. Dimmelow (secy.), McGregory, C. Lee, and others. In opening the proceedings, Mr, Morris spoke of the success the Gymnasium classes had attained since their formation, and be also alluded to the beautifying of the building which had been carried out by Mr. Hesketh, of Sankey-street. In referring to the benefits of the insti- tution he said one of the most commendable features of it was that it helped to keep young fellows from idling away their time, and in many cases from d eteri- , orating into that detestable figure of society, the corner man. In conclusion he expressed a wish that the War- rington public would give further and increased sup- port to the institution. (Applause.) The first item in the programme was an exercise with the barbells taken part in by the following :—Messrs. J Podmnre, J. E. Warren. G. Vernon, A. Vernsn, F. Bennion, A. Dickens, J. Vernon, W. Fletcher. C. Noble, G. Clay. W. }Wilber, A. Gough, A. Mottram, F. E. White, G. Dean, T. Fowles. W. S. France, C. Dodds, H. Whittle, J. W. Bibby,E.Gerrard. F. Stringer, J. Doncasfer,and J.Miller. The exercise was conducted by Mr. Kinsley, and the ac- companiments were played by the pianist, Miss Stirrup A performance on the rings was next given by half-a- dozen of the advanced pupils led by the conductor. Each performance was well executed, and showed care- , ful training. The audience evinced their appreciation 1 by loudly applauding each item. A lengthy perform- ance was afterwards given on the parallel bars, in two stages. The first was by the elementary scholars, who for distinction were dressed differently from the colours worn by the club. The following took part :—Elemen- tary : Messrs. C. Noble, W. Bamber, A. Mottram, G. Dean, G. Clay. and C. Dodds. Advanced: Messrs. J. Podtnore, G. Vernon, A. Vernon, F. Betinion,A.Dickens, asid W. Fletcher. A most interesting feature of the exercise was the represention of a sunflower on the parallel bars by the followirn! members :—Messrs. J. E. Warren, F. Bennion, J. W. Bibby, F. Stringer, J. Pod- more, and A. Dickens, and Mr. Kinsley. The effect pro- duced was very striking, and proved to be very pipe- lar with the audience. A damb-bell exercise given by the boys' and girls' classes followed, and for keeping good time and precision they excelled the elder pupils. The following are the names of those who took part : Misses Edith Whittle, Edith, Elsie, and Aggie pleave, Emma Allen, Evelyn Grirnshaw' Beatrice Whittle, Annie Summerfield, Polly Burrows, LecSie Houghton, Lena Dobson, and Mary Hill; Masters J. W. Scott, J. Monks, J. .Leslie, T. Houghton, W. Houghton, L. Sutton, B. Evans, D. Farr, and T. Boscow. The next item was the most interesting and exciting ot. the evening. It was a performance on the double (-loni- a intal bars by Mr. Kinsley, the conductor. A descrip- tion of the various feats in this exhibition would occuny too much space, but it is only doing justice to Mr. Kinslev to state that his performance is considered to be without precedent in Warrington. It was one of the most remarkable and startling exhibitions of skilled gymnastics that we have seen, and we endorse the expressions of admiration and approval which were so freely expressed by the audience. Performances on the Iterizontal bars as a rule are seldom indul.ed in by the average gymnast because of the difficulty which attends 1 taiere,sed the degree of daneer whieh is always present. On this occasion, however, G. and A. Vernon,A. Bennion d A. Dickens successfully performed several of the feats, and gave thereby still another proof of the high excellence to which the senior members of the institu- tion have attained in all the most difficult exerc ses.</p>
        <p>By the way, a clown, in the person of Mr. T. Locker, was introduced into the exercise, and his mimiery was a pleasing diversion. A score of young fellows next entered with clubs, and went through a number of exercises... Mr. F. Turner, looking like a modern Her- cules next entered, and gave an exhibition with a 131 b. club. The two tug-of-war competitions which followed 'formed the most excitable portion of the programme. The first encounter was between the Gymnasium and the Warrington Rugby Football team. But the encounter was not as evenly contested as might have been expect- ed, and the football team, who were by far the heavier lot, ran out easy winners by two p )ints to nil. The tearas were composed of the following :—Football team: Messrs. F.Turner, Thornily, Ashton, Jolley, Potts, Donohue, Clark and Nevins. Gyinnasium : Messrs. J. E. Warren, T. Locker, J. Podmore, F. Bennion, W. Barn- ber, C. Noble, W. S. France and A. Dickens. The second ' competition was between the boys' and girls' classes. Eight girls being pitted against nine boys. Whether the boys thought they had "an easy thing on" and thus became careless, or did it for " the fun of the thing " is questionable, but it has to be chronicled that the girls won the contest, and by two points to one. The inter- est increased as the competition progressed, and when the girls pulled the boys over the line for the second time there was quito an ovation of applause, especially from the female sex. The next item, that of a boxing match, was not quite so interesting. The combatants were two brothers, Percy and Stanley Fair. Three spirited rounds were contested, after which the broth- erly combatants retired, having given a skilful exposi- tion of the " useful science." Mr. C. Dodds afterwards , gave a clever performance with a couple of clubs. The . vaulting horse was the last item on the programme, and proved not the least interesting. The jumping over the wooden horse by men, boys, and girls was particularly ' good, and Edith Gleave was a conspicuous success. The feat of taking a sominersault over the horse with- out touching it, was the most difficult, and Mr. Kinsley ' was only able to a few of the advanced pupils to follow him. A most enjoyable entertainment concluded by the ' singing of " God save the Queen." A mead of praise is due to the conductor, Mr. Kinsley, and the Committee, for the excellent programme submitted, the carrying out of which reflected great credit on the performers, and conductor alike. It may be stated that the number of persons taking advantage of the gymnasium at , Present is as follows : .Men's class, 65; boys, 16; ladies, 20 ; girls, 33.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>LIMM.</head>
        <p>LIMM.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF Mn. J. D. DRWHIIRST.—It was with great surprise arid regret that the news of the death of Mr. John D. Dev,burst, of Oughtrington House, Lvinm, on Saturday morning, was received in the district around. Although the deceased gentleman had not been well for some months past, his sudden decease was quite unex- pected, and it has cast a gloom over the village, as he was well known and highly respected. Mr. Dewhurst, who was 49 years of age, was the eldest surviving son of Mr.George:C.Dewhurst,of Beechwood,and went to Harrogate about five months ago, accompanied by his wife and family. While there he was taken ill, and he had never thoroughly recovered. He returned from Bournemouth, his winter residence, no later than Wed- nesday last, leaving his wife arid daughter there, and took up his residence at his father's house at Beechwood. He resumed business as a member of the firm of Messrs. G. and R. Dew hurst, cotton manufacturers, and com- mission agents in Manchester, being at the offices on Friday afternoon, after which he returned to Beech- wood. Although not looking well—and this fact was observed by his sister—he made no complaint, and re- tired to rest as usual later on in the day. Next morn- ing, between eight and nine o'clock, his son was horri- fied to find him dead in bed. The funeral took place on Wednesday afternoon in Oughtrington Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>GILCHRIST LECTURES AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE GILCHRIST LECTURES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In compliance with the request of the Gilchrist Trustees, the hon. secretaries appointed by the local committee—Messrs, J. 1). Jones and B. Crosby —have forwarded a report to them, in which their questions are fully answered. In the first place it is set forth that the attendance at the respective lectures had been as follows :—The first by Sir Robert Ball, 1,150 ; the second by Professor Lawrie, 1,005 ; the third by Professor Marshall, 999 ; the fourth by Dr. Andrew Wilson, 983; the fifth by the Rev. Dr. Dallinger 1,129 ; and the la,t by Professor Seeley, 959. The aver- age attendance was 1,038. With regard to the measures adopted to secure the success of the lectures the com- mittee ma6e themselves responsible for the sale of the tickets allotted to them, and within fourteen days be- tween 600 and 700 tickets were sold, while up to a fort- nieht before the first lecture no fewer than 1,180 course tickets had been disposed of, and so successful had been the personal efforts of the Committee in arousing interest in the lectures, that applications for several hundred more tickets had to be refused. In answer to the ques- tion as to the specific object of the lectures, the report states that it was to strengthen the hands of the Tech- nical Instruction Committee.which had been established some time, in their endeavour to provide a Technical Institute for the town. The efforts of the Gilchrist Lectures Committee had been attended with great suc- cess, as would be gathered from the fact that the Chair- man of the Technical Instruction Committee publicly stated that the lectures had produced a strong feeling in favour of the early completion of their scheme,so that the funds at their first meeting after the announcement of the course were augmented by upwards of £2OO, and that the Technical Instruction Committee had seen their way to accept a tender for the erection of the In- stitute.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At a special sitting of the court on Tuesday. before Major Hazlehurst and Mr. Handley. Patrilk Corcoran was charged under a warrant for being drunk and dis- orderly, on October Bth. Having been convicted six times, he was fined ss; and 143. costs, or in default 14 days' incarceration.—Mary Lyne,a married woman, was charged with being drunk and disorderly on the previous night. She was fined ss. and 6s. 4d. costs, or 14 days.— Joseph Brimelow, for disorderly conduct, had a penalty of £1 and 83.6 d. casts imposed upon him.—Alice Hamp- son, a notorious character, as charged with indecent conduct on the previous night. The prisoner said the Bench might as well give her a long term as a short one. She cared not what they did with her. Inspector Cooper intimated that she bad 51 previous convictions recorded against her, and that she only came out of gael the day before. She was a perfect nuisance to the town. She was now committed to prison for three months with hard labour. The Prisoner : You are very good, cirs.— Harriet Baxter for being drunk and disorderly in Bridge- at., on the previous night, was sent to Knutsford for a month. This was her 30th appearance. BILLIARD MATCH AT WIDNES LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>A billiard match between eighteen members of Wid- nes Liberal Club and a similar number of Runcorn Liberal Club, took place at IA idnes on Thursday after- 1 noon. Some interesting games resulted,and eventually , Widnes won by a majority of 96 points. The visitors were very hospitably entertained a capital knife and fork tea being provided by Mr. Joseph Parkinson the caretakgr. The proceeeings throughout were very en- joyable. Result : WIDNES. RuNcourr. J. Parkinson P:Quinn Mat Conner 62 W. Wright (captain) John Sailer lOO W RilFy F Jones F. Deacon A. Parkinson (captain) 86 H Barnes 'F. Wriwht IF. McGovern P. Wilkinson I F Harrison 57 J. Page lOO J. Brady Wm. Ince lOO Johnsen IJ. Kershaw J Quinn • lOO H. Heesom l9 lE. Jolley 59 J Goodier lOO</p>
        <p>A. H. Pickering 43 C. Stubbs lOO J. Finn lOO J. Moss B2</p>
        <p>Total 1561 Total 1465 RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this committee was held on Tuesday evening, in the Town Hail, when there were present—Mr. Handley (chairman), the Rev. Canon A. Maitland Wood, Messrs. T. Riley. W. M. Sowerby, S. Pope, and J. D. Jones (librarian). The librarian submit- ted his monthly statement, from which it appeared that during December there were 1,887 volumes issued in the lending department, made up as follows :—Theologv, 30; philosophy, 14 • science, 90; history, &amp;c., 136 ; Ipoetry, 18; fiction, 1,387 ; miscellaneous, 155. These figures showed a decrease of 54. In the reference sec- ! (ion there bad been issued 57 volumes, or an increase of 16. The librarian stated that there had been presented during the month by Mr. J. Simpson. Curmock's " Native Musings" ; Wray ard Stephenson's " Life of W. 0. Simpson." edited by J. Bush ; Pen Oliver's" All But " ; " A Danish Parsonage " ; " Swiss Family Robin- son " ; Nugent's " Memorial of John Hampden " ; Hall's " Life with the Eaquimaux " ; Greswell's " History of Runcorn " ; and Fowler's " Visitors Guide to Runcorn "; also by Mr. II Foulkes, Bullock's" What Church " ; and by Mr. B. Birckat Musgrove (the composer), "The Evelina Gavotte." The chairman said that the volume of Greswell's " History of Runcorn," !presented by Mr. Simpson, was of exceptional interest, and remarked that it would prove very advantageous if the medical officer ! could bring the town back to the salubrious condition named in the volume. Mr. Jones was making a collec- tion of o d historical documents. Towards these they had received the first second-class ticket issued from Runcorn to Liverpool. They were also receiving a handbill fram, d, announcing the opening of the rail- way to Runcorn in 1860. The ticket might be placed with it. The librarian submitted a balance sheet respecting the Free Library lectures of last year. Their deficiency had been cleared off through the Gilchrist ! Lecture Committee having handed to them their balance as well as a sum received from a gentleman who did not wish his name to be divulged.</p>
        <p>A</p>
        <p>RUNCO RN PARIS Ft CRU Rai TEA PARTY.</p>
        <p>The annual congregational tea party in connec- tion with the Runcorn Parish Church wits held in the National Schools, Church-street, on Monday evening, and the re-union was in every way a success. There was a large attendance at tea, and the wants of the guests were attended to by the follow- ing ladies, who presided over the tables :—Mrs. Wood. Mrs. Imison, Miss Smith, Miss Savage, Mrs. Littler, Miss Rogerson, Miss Tatters, Mrs. Johnson, Mrs. Digges, Mrs. Meredeth, Miss Smith (Stanley Villas), Mrs. Raschen, Mrs. Crosby, Mrs. Abel, Mrs. Hamilton, Miss Bate, Mrs. Gregory, Mrs. H. White, and Mrs. Sut- ton. The list of doners to the tea is as follows ; Mrs. flazlehurst, Mrs. Broadhurst, Mrs. Mason, Mrs. Weston, C-inon Wood, Rev. J. Holden, Messrs. W. Tim- mins, Rogerson, Gregory, Littler, Crosby and Sons, Meredith, Johnson, Bul.±, H. White, John Leicester, Dr. MacLennan, Messrs. J. S. Davies, J. K. Digges, W. B. Kay, W. Sutton, T. Nicholls, G. Shone, J. H. Hamilton, R. flampson, W. Bate, F. Smith, A. Jordan, E. Imison, Mr. and Mrs. Speakman, Messrs. R. Abel, J. Ball, A. Salkeld, H. Briscoe, Jos. Savage, M. Barker, W. R. Wass, J. Bridgewater, C. R. H. Harrison, E. Newton, and W. Whittaker. Subsequently a concert was given under the presidency of the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, vicar. and the room was crowded with an appreciative audience. The choir epened the programme with the glee " The Iron founders," and during the evening thev also contributed "The clang of the hammer ""The village blacksmith," and " When the lights are low." Each item was admir- ably rendered, under the conductorship of Mr. James Littler. Mr. Pownall sang " Sailing across the Sea " with capital effect and be was deservedly encored. Mrs. Comerford was also recalled for an expressive and taste- ful rendering of " Kathleen alavourneen." The Man- doline and Guitar Dind (Mrs. Warburton, Mrs. Burn, Mrs. Parkinson, Miss Carter, Miss Warbutton, Mr. Williamson and Mr. Burn) made two appearances and on each occasion they were enthusiastically encored. The music is qaint, and catchy and as such a band is a novelty there will no doubt be numerous demands on their services. Miss Gregory made a very successful debut, she has a voice of good quality and she sang with great expression, " Augus MacDonald" and she had to respond to an ur.animous recall. Master Wilkinson was loudly applauded for his singing of " Why do you weep Mother," Master H. Newton and Mester E. Newton sang respectively : " The Farmers Boy "and "Silver moon- light winds are blowing" and their fresh young voices were heard to great advantage. Mr. Caygill gave " The skipper." Mr. Clare received a well merited encore for his singing of "The blue Alsatian mountains." Miss Simkin and Mr. James Litter gave the duet " When the wind blows in from the sea,"—a very pretty piece of vocalisation. Mr. Littler was also with Mr. Branch in the duet " The moon has raised her lamp above" and the plaudits by which the item was greeted was well de- served. The duties of accompanist were carried out by Miss Crosby Mr. J. Johnson and Mr. Lancaster. During an interval in the programme. The Chairman said the past year in the parish so far as church matters generally were concerned, had been one of steady advance. He did not care to go into de- tails; he had a horror of publishing figures, partly because it was said there was nothing so false as figures except facts, or vice versa. But still it would be in- teresting to know that the numbers of communicants connected with the churches had steadily increased, and that at Easter last year and at Christmas the numbers were greater than they had ever been before. Since the last tea party, Mr Seeley had left them and now he was going to no less distant a place than Rangoon and he (the Vicar) hoped he would be a blessing to these amongst whom he was going to work. Since the be- ginning of the year, Mr. Stables who was to work Wes- ton had arrived but he was unable to be present. One could not refer without great sorrow to the sad fact that the Parish Church and St. Michael's Church had lost two members of one family, who were greatly esteemed and who were very active in the work of the church. He need not tell them that he referred to Mr. Isaac Speak- man, a sidesman in that Parish Church, and to Mr. Thomas Speakman, one of the sidesmen in charge, or churchwardens of St. Michael's. This had been a very serious matter to all of them. There had been such a great expression of feeling that it was unnecessary to say more. Passing from church matters, he took it that the most important event that had happened since last year was the opening of the Ship Canal. (Laughter.) He thought the general opinion might be expressed in this way. They hoped that Liverpool would not lose anything, that Manchester would gain a great deal for their enterprise and that Runcorn would be allowed to have a pretty good slice out of the plum pud- ding. (Applause.) Alluding to church going, the Vicar said he wished to point out the fact that the churches, and he was credibly informed the chapels also in this town, were open on Sunday mornings. (Laughter.) He humourously, yet strongly urged upon his hearers the necessity and desirability of attending morning ser- vice, not only for their own sakes but for the sake of an example to their children. If the wives were compelled to stay at home to prepare a good dinner—and he was not one of those who thought they ought to eat meat cold on a Sunday—there was no reason why the hus- bands should not attend. Alluding to the condition of Greenway-raad, after divine service on Sunday nights,</p>
        <p>, the Vicar said it was a great promenade. They might I call it Veal Parade, because veal came from a calf, and a calf was an immature creature, and the immature creatures of Runcorn were to be found streaming up and down Greenway-road in their thousands on Sunday nights. There they were, three and four in a row, arm in arm—(laughter and applause)— and it was really becoming quite a serious nuisance. He was not at all sure which was the worst, the big lads or the big girls. They blocked up the whole of the pavement and people like himself had lately be- gun to walk in the middle of the road, but these hob- bledehoys and hobbledhoy girls had now invaded the middle of the road, and the whole of the area from Doc- tors Bridge right up to the Vicarage was occupied by boys and girls laughing, talking, smoking, swearing and doing many other disagreeable things. It ought be put -down and must be put down. (Applause.) If he might make a suggestion to the Commissioners and the police it would be this : not to take any active measures, or slaughter any of these young people, but that Runcorn people should be given to understand, what all London people understood, that whichever way they might be moving along in the street, and met a fellow creature the proper thing to do was to kesp to the right—make it a Custom that any person meeting another on the path should keep to the right just as carriages always keep to the left. Then they would have all this extraordinary disagreeableness of walking up and down the road done away with ; they would be able to pass one another comfortably. In conclusion the Vicar expressed his own gratitude and the gratitude of the rest of the clergy to the laity who had organise the tea and entertainment and had brought the gathering to such a satisfactory conclusion.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of the Chairman 21P cordial vote of thanks was accorded to those who had provided the en- tertainment, special mention being made of Mr. James Littler. A similar vote Ix as also accorded to the donors of tables, to those who had prepared tea, and to the ladies who had presided at the tables, on the proposition of Mr. James Littler and seconded by Mr. W. Shaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>MISSIONARY MEETING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MISSIONARY MEETING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The anniversary in connection with the Wesleyan Foreign Missions, Camden Branch, Runcorn, was held on Sunday, when two sermons were preached by the Rev. W.Silcox, of Widnes. In the afternoon, a juvenile missionary meeting was held under the presidency of Mr. A. Harrison. An interesting ad- dress was delivered by Mr. G. Littler. Mr. J. T. Nancollis, the secretary, submitted the report, show- ing that in the boys' classes there had been collected dur- ing the year £9 4s. 11.1 id., in the girls' classes £4 1973. 8-1-d. The children by in,ans of collecting boxes had obtained £lB 7s. 6-U, and Miss Temperley by her efforts had realized £2 Bs. The school collections came to £2 15s. 41. The annual meeting was held on Monday evening, in Camden Chapel, when Mr. R. Rawlinson occupied the chair. He was supported by the Revs. E. R. Eslick (formerly of Bangalore, Central India, and who attended as a deputation,, 'l'. Wilkes, W. Arrowsinith, and J. Hanby ; Messrs. S. C. Beckett, A. Harrison, T. Simister, E. Taylor, and J. .f.Nancollis. . . .</p>
        <p>A limelight exhibition was given by the Rev. James Hanby, who was assisted by Mr. B. Crosby. The views consisted of many of the principd places in the Indian Empire. An interesting item comisted of throwing up- on the screen the names of those who had collected money for the cause and the amounts realized. They were as follows :—Mr. Charles Littler, £2 15s. ; Miss S. E. Littler £3 is. ; Bertha Gibso,i, £2 Os. iOi. ; Willie Nancollis, ;F. A. Yates, Li is. ii. ; Lily TemperleV. ; Ida Clucas, 15s. ; Jessie Duncalf, 143. 31.; Mary E. , Britian&amp; 13s. 9:11. ; Samuel Watten, 133, 3i ; James Hinde, 10s. ii. ; Sarah Ann Hussey 9-. ; Harry Burton, 9s. stpl. ; John Fitton, 83. 3?;1. ; Lift Pope, 6s. 9J. ; Lottie Rathbone, 6s 51. ; and Percy Mcfcorton, 55.31 d. An interesting address was delivered by the Rev. E.R.E3lick,whosooko of the work in connection with the Missionary Society which was being carded on in India, where he had laboured. He spoke of the necessity of the greatest possible assistance being given to the or- ganisation under whose auspices they were met in order that the work might he continued with success. At the services, the choir rendered special anthems, and they were greatly assisted by the Sunday Schtnd Orchestral Band, under the conJuctorship of Mr. T. Bazlev. The organ was presided at by Mr. S. Littler. The collections made the total amount received during the year into £42 33. 3.1.</p>
        <p>Commencing on Sunday lust in the Earlestown Wesleyan Cbapel, and following on the week of united prayer, united Evangelisttic services have during this week been held. Since Monday, the services have taken place in the Town Hall, and have been conducted by the Rev. C. Musgrave Brown, of St. Albans, Liverpool. The services have been well attended, and are being conducted in an earnest aud hearty manner, good results are expected.</p>
